window.DOM_SEARCH={"cv-introduction":"Introduction to the Heart and Circulation 1. Why do we need a circulatory system? Every cell in the body needs a steady supply of oxygen and nutrients. At the same time it keeps making waste, including carbon dioxide, which must be carried away. Why can't cells simply rely on diffusion? Over very short distances, diffusion is fast: oxygen crosses the few micrometres from a capillary to a nearby cell in a fraction of a second. But the time diffusion takes grows with the square of the distance. Moving oxygen 1 mm by diffusion alone takes minutes, and 1 cm takes hours. Distance Rough time for oxygen to diffuse 10 µm (capillary to a nearby cell) about 0.02 s 1 mm a few minutes 1 cm several hours A body many centimetres thick could never be supplied that way. So the body uses bulk flow instead. The heart pumps blood quickly through vessels to within a few micrometres of almost every cell, and diffusion only has to cover that last tiny step. The lecture sums up the heart's job simply: to move the blood about, sending it to all the tissues. The circulatory system has three parts: Heart: the pump. Blood vessels: the pipes that carry blood to every tissue and back. Blood: the carrier. It transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, waste products and heat. When a tissue's needs rise, the circulation responds. Working muscle during exercise receives more blood, which brings extra oxygen and removes extra carbon dioxide. Source note: Diffusion times are approximate (time ≈ distance² ÷ 2D, with D for oxygen in water). They are foundation material added to explain why a pump is needed. They are not on the lecture slides. 2. The two major circulatory pathways Blood travels through two circuits, one after the other: Pulmonary circulation: The right side of the heart pumps blood to the lungs. There, carbon dioxide leaves the blood and oxygen enters it. The oxygen-rich blood then returns to the left side of the heart. Systemic circulation: The left side of the heart pumps oxygen-rich blood to the tissues of the whole body. The tissues take up oxygen and add carbon dioxide, and the blood returns to the right side of the heart. Figure 1 — Pulmonary and systemic circulation. Image credit OpenStax College, original illustration, CC BY 3.0; unmodified. The two circuits are in series. Blood must pass through one circuit before it can enter the other: body → right heart → lungs → left heart → body. There is no other route. Why must both ventricles pump the same amount? Because every drop the right ventricle sends through the lungs arrives at the left ventricle. Over any period longer than a few beats, the right and left ventricles must pump the same volume per minute: about 5 L/min each at rest. If the right ventricle pumped even slightly more than the left for a long time, blood would pile up in the lungs. If the left pumped more than the right, the lungs would slowly drain. ( → Cardiac Output, §1) Within the systemic circuit, the organs sit side by side (in parallel). The aorta divides into branches that supply the brain, the heart muscle, the gut and liver, the kidneys, the skeletal muscles and the skin. Because each organ has its own branch: every organ receives fresh, oxygen-rich blood from the arteries, not blood another organ has already used; the flow to each organ can be turned up or down on its own, by narrowing or widening that organ's small arteries, without starving the others. ( → Haemodynamics, §8) The lungs are the exception: all of the blood passes through them, in series. The heart feeds itself the same way. The heart does not take its oxygen from the blood inside its chambers. Its muscular wall is far too thick for oxygen to diffuse in from the chambers to most of its cells. So the heart muscle has its own parallel branch from the aorta: the coronary arteries. ( → Coronary Circulation, §1) Series and parallel One loop runs right heart → lungs → left heart → aorta, labelled in series. The aorta then splits into side-by-side branches for the brain, the heart muscle (coronary arteries), the gut and liver, the kidneys, skeletal muscle and the skin, labelled in parallel. The branches rejoin in the veins and return to the right heart. Gut and liver are drawn as one branch because blood from the gut passes through the liver. Source note: The lecture gives about 5.5 L/min for each side of the heart; 5 L/min is also widely quoted. The parallel arrangement of organs is foundation material for the regional-circulation chapters. 3. The heart and its four chambers The heart is a muscular organ that works as two pumps side by side. The right heart pumps blood to the lungs, and the left heart pumps blood to the body. Each pump has two chambers: Right atrium: receives blood returning from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae. Right ventricle: pumps that blood into the pulmonary trunk, which divides into the pulmonary arteries to the lungs. Left atrium: receives oxygen-rich blood returning from the lungs through the pulmonary veins. Left ventricle: pumps that blood into the aorta, which carries it to the whole body. The atria mainly receive blood and top up the ventricles. The ventricles do the main pumping: they generate the pressure that drives blood into the arteries. Figure 2 — Internal structure of the heart. Image credit Blausen Medical Communications, Inc., original illustration, CC BY 3.0; unmodified. How does blood flow through the heart? Follow the path: Body tissues → Venae cavae → Right atrium → Tricuspid valve → Right ventricle → Pulmonary valve → Pulmonary trunk and arteries → Lungs → Pulmonary veins → Left atrium → Mitral valve → Left ventricle → Aortic valve → Aorta → Body tissues. What keeps blood moving one way? Four valves: The inlet valves sit between each atrium and its ventricle: the tricuspid valve on the right and the mitral valve on the left. Together they are the AV (atrioventricular) valves. The outlet valves sit between each ventricle and its artery: the pulmonary valve on the right and the aortic valve on the left. Together they are the semilunar valves. Valves contain no muscle. They open and shut passively, pushed by blood pressure. A valve opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front of it, and shuts the moment that reverses. So blood can only move forward. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §2) Arteries and veins are named by direction, not by oxygen. The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood, and the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood. This is because arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins carry blood towards it, whatever its oxygen content. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why can't diffusion alone supply the cells of the body? A. Diffusion stops working once blood starts to flow B. Oxygen cannot cross cell membranes without a pump C. Cells use oxygen faster than any blood flow can bring it D. Diffusion time rises steeply as the distance grows Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Diffusion time grows with the square of the distance. Micrometres take a fraction of a second, but centimetres take hours. The circulation carries blood close to every cell, so diffusion only covers the last few micrometres. 2. For a week, a person's right ventricle pumps slightly more blood per minute than the left. Where does the extra blood collect? A. In the systemic veins, which hold most of the blood volume B. In the pulmonary vessels, between the two ventricles C. In the right atrium, because the tricuspid valve shuts D. In the aorta, which stretches to store the extra volume Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The circuits are in series. Everything the right ventricle pumps goes through the lungs to the left heart. If the left ventricle sends on less than arrives, the difference builds up in the lung vessels. 3. Why does the brain still receive oxygen-rich blood when the leg muscles are working hard? A. Blood reaches the brain before it reaches the leg muscles B. The brain extracts oxygen faster than the leg muscles can C. Each organ has its own branch from the aorta, side by side D. The heart pumps a separate circuit just for the brain Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Systemic organs are supplied in parallel. The brain's branch carries fresh arterial blood, not blood the muscles have already used. Flow to each organ can also be adjusted separately. 4. A vessel carries oxygen-rich blood towards the heart. What is it? A. A pulmonary vein, because it carries blood towards the heart B. A pulmonary artery, because its blood is rich in oxygen C. A systemic artery, because oxygen-rich blood leaves the lungs D. A coronary artery, because it supplies the heart muscle Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Vessels are named by direction. Veins carry blood towards the heart, and the pulmonary veins bring oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium. 4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right? Both ventricles pump the same volume each minute ( §2). Yet the left ventricle's wall is two to three times thicker than the right's. The difference is pressure, not volume. What sets the pressure a pump must make? Blood flows only when there is a pressure difference to push it. The more the vessels oppose flow (their resistance), the bigger the pressure difference needed to keep the same flow. The lecture puts it as: flow = pressure difference ÷ resistance. ( → Haemodynamics, §2) Systemic circulation: a vast network of small, muscular arteries and arterioles, so total resistance is high. The left ventricle must reach about 120 mmHg with each beat. Pulmonary circulation: short, wide, thin-walled vessels, so resistance is low. The right ventricle needs only about 25 mmHg. Generating more pressure takes more muscle, so the left ventricular wall is thicker. Same volume per minute, different pressure. Why is it useful for the lungs to have low pressure? Lung capillaries are extremely thin, so that gases can cross them quickly. High pressure inside them would push fluid out into the lung tissue and air spaces. This is pulmonary oedema, and it gets in the way of gas exchange. Low pulmonary pressure keeps the lungs dry. ( → Microcirculation, §5) 5. Systole and diastole Systole means contraction and diastole means relaxation. Always say which chambers you mean, because the atria and ventricles contract at different times. Atrial systole: the atria contract and push the final portion of blood into the ventricles. Atrial diastole: the atria relax and fill with blood returning from the body and lungs. Ventricular systole: the ventricles contract and eject blood into the pulmonary trunk (right) and the aorta (left). Ventricular diastole: the ventricles relax and fill for the next beat. The order matters. The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole, just before the ventricles contract. By then, most ventricular filling (about 70% in the lecture) has already happened passively. Atrial contraction only tops it up. At a resting heart rate, one beat lasts about 0.8 s. The ventricles spend about 0.3 s in systole and 0.5 s in diastole. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §1) 6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system To pump well, the heart needs two things: force and timing. Force: cardiac muscle (myocardium). Cardiac muscle cells are electrically linked to one another. An impulse therefore spreads from cell to cell, and the muscle of each chamber contracts as one unit. ( → Cardiac Muscle, §2) Timing: the conducting system. The impulse starts in the SA node, at the junction of the superior vena cava and the right atrium. It spreads across the atria to the AV node, which holds it back briefly. It then races down the bundle of His and the Purkinje fibres through both ventricles. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §2) The lecture notes that every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. The SA node normally sets the pace because it fires fastest, so it triggers the rest before they can fire themselves. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §3) Electrical activation always comes first; contraction follows. The order in which the impulse spreads sets the order of contraction: the atria first, then, after the AV-node delay, the ventricles. Source note: Lecture connection: Circulation 1, opening slides (the heart's structure and function, conducting tissues, systole and diastole). The diffusion explanation and the parallel arrangement of organs are added foundations. Understanding Check: Sections 4–6 1. Both ventricles eject about 70 mL per beat. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker? A. It must eject a larger volume of blood each minute B. It must generate higher pressure against higher resistance C. It contracts for longer because the aortic valve is smaller D. It must also pump blood through the coronary arteries first Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The two ventricles move the same volume, but the systemic circulation offers far more resistance. The left ventricle needs about 120 mmHg against the right's 25, and more pressure needs more muscle. 2. Why is low pressure in the pulmonary circulation an advantage? A. It lets the right ventricle pump less blood than the left B. It slows the blood so that more carbon dioxide can be made C. It stops the pulmonary valve from opening during systole D. It keeps fluid from being pushed out of the lung capillaries Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Thin lung capillaries leak fluid if pressure inside them is high, which causes pulmonary oedema. Low pressure keeps the lungs dry. Both ventricles still pump the same volume. 3. During atrial systole, what are the ventricles normally doing? A. Contracting, so the atria and ventricles squeeze together B. Ejecting blood into the aorta and pulmonary trunk at once C. Relaxed, finishing filling just before their own contraction D. Relaxing after ejection, with all four valves still shut Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. They top up the relaxed ventricles just before the ventricles contract; the AV-node delay keeps that order. 4. Every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. Why does the SA node normally set the heart rate? A. It fires fastest, so it triggers the others first B. It is the only part connected to the nervous system C. It lies in the left ventricle, the strongest chamber D. It holds the impulse back so the atria contract first Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The fastest pacemaker wins: the SA node fires before the others reach their own firing point. D describes the AV node. The SA node lies in the right atrium. 7. Understanding Check 1. For several minutes, a patient's left ventricle pumps less than the right ventricle. Where does blood build up first? A. In the lungs, raising the pressure in the pulmonary vessels B. In the leg veins, causing swelling around the ankles C. In the right ventricle, whose wall thickens to cope D. In the pulmonary veins, which then carry oxygen-poor blood Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The lungs sit between the right and left ventricles. If the left side sends on less than the right side delivers, blood accumulates in the lung vessels first. 2. Which statement about the heart valves is correct? A. Valves open when their muscle fibres contract B. Valves open and shut because of pressure differences C. Valves are opened by impulses from the AV node D. Valves shut as soon as the atria begin to relax Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Valves have no muscle and no nerve control. Pressure behind a valve opens it, and reversed pressure shuts it. 3. The right ventricle pumps oxygen-poor blood into the pulmonary trunk. Why is the pulmonary trunk still called an artery? A. It has a thick muscular wall like the aorta B. It carries blood at the highest pressure in the body C. It carries blood away from the heart D. It carries blood back to the left atrium Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins carry it towards the heart, whatever the oxygen content. 8. Chapter Essentials Diffusion only works over micrometres. The circulation brings blood within a few micrometres of almost every cell. The pulmonary and systemic circuits are in series. Both ventricles pump the same volume per minute (about 5 L/min at rest). A lasting mismatch makes blood pile up in one circuit. The systemic organs are in parallel. Each gets fresh arterial blood, and flow to each can be adjusted separately. The heart muscle has its own branch: the coronary arteries. Four chambers: the atria receive blood and top up the ventricles; the ventricles generate the pressure. Valves are passive. They open and shut with pressure differences, keeping flow one-way. Arteries carry blood away from the heart; veins carry it towards the heart, whatever its oxygen content. The left ventricle is thicker because it needs more pressure (about 120 vs 25 mmHg), not because it pumps more volume. Low lung pressure keeps the lungs dry. Systole is contraction; diastole is relaxation. The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. Electrical activation (SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje) comes first and sets the order of contraction.","cardiac-muscle":"Cardiac Muscle Physiology 1. What makes cardiac muscle different? Heart muscle has to do something no other muscle does: contract about once a second for a whole lifetime, without tiring, and relax completely between beats so the chambers can refill. Its special features follow from those demands. Like skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle is striated. Its actin and myosin filaments are arranged in repeating units called sarcomeres, which shorten when the muscle contracts. Unlike skeletal muscle, it is involuntary, and its cells are short and branched, usually with one central nucleus. Every heartbeat follows the same chain of events, and this chapter follows it link by link: electrical signal → calcium enters the cell → the cell's calcium store releases much more → actin and myosin pull → calcium is removed → the muscle relaxes. Four features make this chain work in the heart: Intercalated discs join the cells, so the signal spreads and the cells pull together ( §2). A long action potential with a plateau lets calcium in and stops the muscle from locking in contraction ( §3, §6). Calcium-induced calcium release links the electrical signal to contraction ( §4). Many mitochondria supply a constant stream of ATP. Heart muscle depends almost entirely on aerobic metabolism, so it needs oxygen all the time. ( → Coronary Circulation, §1) A few specialised heart cells can fire on their own; this is automaticity. Ordinary working muscle cells normally don't: they wait to be excited by their neighbours. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §3) 2. Intercalated discs: how separate cells function together One heart muscle cell on its own cannot pump blood. Thousands must contract together, and each cell's pull must pass to the next. Both jobs happen at the intercalated discs, where neighbouring cells meet end to end. Structure What it does Fascia adherens Anchors the actin filaments of neighbouring cells, so the pull passes from one cell to the next Desmosomes Hold neighbouring cells firmly together, so they don't tear apart under repeated force Gap junctions Tiny channels that let ions (electrical current) flow straight from one cell into the next When one cell is excited, current flows through its gap junctions into its neighbours and brings them to threshold. The signal spreads from cell to cell, and the whole chamber contracts almost as one. The other two junctions hold the cells together while they pull. So heart muscle behaves as a functional syncytium: syn (together) + cyt (cell), meaning separate cells working as one unit. The atria form one syncytium and the ventricles another. Between them lies a ring of fibrous tissue that does not conduct. The only normal electrical route across it is the AV node, which is why the AV node controls when the ventricles contract after the atria. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §5) Figure 1. Cardiac muscle and intercalated discs. Image credit Image: OpenStax · CC BY 4.0 · unmodified. 3. The cardiac muscle action potential An action potential is a brief electrical change across the cell membrane: the inside of the cell swings from negative to positive and back again. It is the signal that tells the cell to contract. In ventricular muscle, the cell rests at about −90 mV. Its action potential lasts about 200–300 ms, roughly 100 times longer than a skeletal muscle action potential. That extra length comes from a long, flat plateau. Phase What happens 0: Rapid upstroke Fast Na⁺ channels open. Na⁺ rushes in, and the inside of the cell becomes positive within about a millisecond. 1: Small dip The Na⁺ channels close, and a brief outflow of K⁺ pulls the voltage down slightly. 2: Plateau L-type Ca²⁺ channels (L = long-lasting) let Ca²⁺ in, while K⁺ leaks out. The two roughly cancel, so the voltage stays nearly flat. 3: Repolarisation The Ca²⁺ channels close, the K⁺ outflow wins, and the inside returns to negative. 4: Rest The cell stays steady at about −90 mV, held there by K⁺ channels that are open at rest. Why does the plateau matter? It has two jobs: It lets calcium in. The Ca²⁺ entering during the plateau is the trigger for contraction ( §4). It keeps the cell refractory, meaning it cannot fire again until its contraction is nearly over. This prevents tetanus ( §6). You can see the action potential on the ECG. The ECG lecture links the flat ST segment to the plateau, when all the ventricular muscle is depolarised. The QT interval roughly matches how long a ventricular action potential lasts. ( → ECG, §4) Figure 2. Electrical activity, refractoriness and contraction. Image credit Image: OpenStax College · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. Important distinction: this is the action potential of a working ventricular muscle cell. Pacemaker cells in the SA node have no steady resting voltage, and their upstroke is driven mainly by Ca²⁺, not by fast Na⁺. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §3) OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO Cardiac Action Potential — Alila Medical Media Revisit the ventricular action potential: identify the fast Na⁺ upstroke, Ca²⁺-dependent plateau and prolonged refractory period. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation. Watch video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Neighbouring cardiac cells stay firmly attached but no longer contract together. Which junction has most likely failed? A. Desmosomes, which hold neighbouring cells together B. Gap junctions, which let current pass between cells C. Fascia adherens, which anchor the actin filaments D. T-tubules, which carry the signal into each cell Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Gap junctions carry the electrical current from cell to cell. Without them the signal can't spread, so the cells stop contracting together, even though desmosomes and fascia adherens still hold them firmly attached. 2. What keeps the ventricular action potential flat during the plateau? A. Fast Na⁺ channels staying open for about 200 ms B. The Na⁺–K⁺ pump stopping until contraction ends C. Ca²⁺ flowing in while K⁺ flows out at a similar rate D. Gap junctions holding the voltage of neighbouring cells Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Inward Ca²⁺ through L-type channels balances outward K⁺, so the voltage barely changes. The fast Na⁺ channels close within a few milliseconds of the upstroke. 3. On the ECG, which part matches the plateau of the ventricular action potential? A. The ST segment B. The P wave C. The QRS complex D. The T wave Show answer and explanation Answer: A. During the plateau, all the ventricular muscle is depolarised at once, so no current flows between regions and the ECG trace stays flat. The QRS complex is the upstroke spreading through the ventricles, and the T wave is repolarisation. 4. Excitation–contraction coupling: from electrical signal to force Excitation–contraction coupling is the chain that turns the electrical signal into force. The link between the two is calcium (Ca²⁺). The signal travels into the cell. The action potential spreads along the cell surface and down the T-tubules, tubes of surface membrane that dive deep into the cell. A little Ca²⁺ enters. The change in voltage opens L-type Ca²⁺ channels in the T-tubules, and a small amount of Ca²⁺ flows in from outside the cell. That Ca²⁺ releases much more. The incoming Ca²⁺ opens the release channels ( RyR2, ryanodine receptor type 2) of the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR), the cell's internal calcium store. The SR pours out far more Ca²⁺. This is calcium-induced calcium release (CICR): the entering Ca²⁺ is the trigger, and the SR supplies most of the Ca²⁺ that drives contraction. Ca²⁺ unlocks the filaments. Ca²⁺ binds to troponin C on the actin filament. This shifts tropomyosin, uncovering the spots where myosin can grab actin. Myosin pulls. Myosin heads attach to actin and pull, using energy from ATP. The sarcomeres shorten and the cell contracts. The calcium cycle, step by step One simple drawing of a heart muscle cell shows the cell membrane with a T-tubule, an L-type channel, the SR with its release channel (RyR2) and its pump (SERCA2a), the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger (NCX), and one actin–myosin unit. Next/Back steps through seven stages. At each stage the part involved lights up, with one plain sentence underneath: (1) the signal arrives down the T-tubule; (2) a little Ca²⁺ enters through the L-type channel; (3) the SR releases much more Ca²⁺; (4) Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, uncovering actin; (5) myosin pulls, and the cell contracts; (6) SERCA2a pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR; (7) NCX pushes Ca²⁺ out of the cell, and the cell relaxes. Ca²⁺ is shown as dots that move between compartments. The text explains everything without the widget. Figure 3. Cardiac calcium cycling. Image credit Image: PeaBrainC · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. Why does heart muscle depend on calcium from outside the cell? In skeletal muscle, the voltage sensor in the T-tubule is mechanically linked to the SR's release channel, so the signal opens the store directly; no outside Ca²⁺ is needed. Heart muscle has no such direct link. Ca²⁺ must first enter from outside to open the store. Skeletal muscle uses a direct mechanical link; heart muscle uses CICR. So anything that reduces Ca²⁺ entry, such as calcium-channel-blocking drugs, weakens the heart's contraction, even if the SR is still full. 5. How does cardiac muscle relax? To relax, the cell must lower its Ca²⁺ again. Ca²⁺ leaves troponin C, tropomyosin covers the binding spots on actin again, and myosin lets go. Two main pumps remove the Ca²⁺: SERCA2a (the sarco-endoplasmic reticulum Ca²⁺ pump) is a pump in the SR membrane, powered by ATP. It pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR, ready for the next beat. NCX (the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger) sits in the cell membrane. It uses the Na⁺ gradient to swap Na⁺ in for Ca²⁺ out, removing roughly the Ca²⁺ that entered during the beat. Other pathways, including a Ca²⁺ pump in the cell membrane, play smaller parts. Relaxation therefore uses energy, just as contraction does. What if SERCA2a works too slowly? Relaxation is delayed. Ca²⁺ stays high in the cell for longer, so the muscle stays partly contracted and the ventricle fills less well. Later beats can be weaker. Less Ca²⁺ goes back into the SR, so less is available to release on the next beat. Good pumping needs both a strong contraction and a fast, complete relaxation. 6. Why can't cardiac muscle undergo normal tetanus? In skeletal muscle, the action potential is over in a few milliseconds, long before the twitch ends. A second stimulus can arrive while the muscle is still contracting, and rapid stimuli add up into one sustained contraction: tetanus. Heart muscle can't do this. As the lecture puts it, it is impossible to tetanise cardiac muscle because of the long refractory period of its action potential. The refractory period lasts almost as long as the contraction itself. By the time the cell can fire again, it has already begun to relax. Why this matters: a heart locked in sustained contraction could not refill, so it would pump nothing. The long refractory period guarantees that the ventricles relax and refill between beats. Figure 2 shows how the refractory period and the contraction line up. Understanding Check: Sections 4–6 1. Ca²⁺ still enters through L-type channels, but the SR's release channels (RyR2) fail to open. What happens to contraction? A. It is much weaker, because the SR's large Ca²⁺ store is not released B. It is normal, because the entering Ca²⁺ is enough on its own C. It is stronger, because more Ca²⁺ stays in the extracellular fluid D. It becomes sustained, because Ca²⁺ can no longer be pumped away Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The entering Ca²⁺ is only the trigger. Most of the Ca²⁺ that activates the filaments comes from the SR, so without RyR2 release the rise in Ca²⁺ is far too small. 2. SERCA2a pumps work more slowly than normal. What is the most likely immediate effect? A. The action potential upstroke becomes slower B. Gap junctions close, so cells contract one at a time C. The heart can now be tetanised by rapid stimuli D. Relaxation is delayed because Ca²⁺ in the cell stays high Show answer and explanation Answer: D. SERCA2a is the main route for removing Ca²⁺ after a beat. If it slows, Ca²⁺ stays bound to troponin for longer and relaxation is delayed. Over later beats, less Ca²⁺ is stored for release. 3. Why can't a rapid series of stimuli make cardiac muscle contract continuously? A. Cardiac muscle stores no calcium in its SR B. Gap junctions block stimuli that arrive too quickly C. Its long refractory period lasts most of the contraction D. The vagus nerve relaxes the muscle between beats Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The cell cannot fire again until its contraction is nearly over, so contractions cannot add up into tetanus. This guarantees relaxation and filling between beats. 7. How do the sympathetic and vagal nerves change cardiac muscle? Sympathetic stimulation prepares the heart for exercise or stress: stronger beats, faster relaxation and a faster rate. Noradrenaline (released by sympathetic nerve endings) and adrenaline (released by the adrenal medulla) both act on β₁ receptors on heart cells: β₁ receptor → G protein → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP rises → PKA is switched on Here cAMP (cyclic AMP) is a messenger molecule inside the cell. PKA (protein kinase A) is an enzyme that switches other proteins on or off by adding phosphate to them. PKA acts on the Ca²⁺ machinery in two ways: Stronger contraction. L-type channels let in more Ca²⁺, so the SR releases more, and more myosin heads pull on actin. Faster relaxation. PKA disables phospholamban, a protein that normally acts as a brake on SERCA2a. With the brake off, Ca²⁺ is pumped back into the SR faster. Caffeine and theophylline (xanthines) work further along the same pathway. The enzyme phosphodiesterase normally breaks cAMP down. Xanthines block that enzyme, so cAMP builds up and contraction gets stronger. Vagal stimulation works the other way. Acetylcholine released by the vagus nerve slows the SA node (a slower heart rate) and weakens atrial contraction. Its direct effect on the strength of the ventricles is small, because few vagal fibres reach ventricular muscle. Three words for three effects: Term Word parts What it describes Positive effect Inotropy ino = fibre Strength of contraction Stronger contraction Lusitropy lusi = loosening Speed of relaxation Faster relaxation Chronotropy chrono = time Heart rate Faster heart rate Important distinction: positive inotropy means the muscle contracts more strongly at the same filling. A ventricle that contracts harder simply because it holds more blood is showing the Frank–Starling effect, not increased inotropy. ( → Cardiac Output, §4) Source note: The lecture slide says adrenaline comes from sympathetic nerve endings and noradrenaline from the adrenals. It is the other way round: sympathetic nerve endings release mainly noradrenaline, and the adrenal medulla releases mainly adrenaline. Both act on β₁ receptors. The lecture also lists a negative inotropic effect of vagal stimulation; this effect is mainly on the atria. 8. Why these properties matter for pumping Each property of heart muscle supports one part of the pumping cycle: Gap junctions make each chamber contract as one, giving a strong, coordinated squeeze. The long refractory period guarantees that the ventricles relax and refill between beats. Calcium handling sets the strength of each beat. More Ca²⁺ means more myosin heads pulling, a stronger contraction and a bigger stroke volume. ( → Cardiac Output, §7) Fast relaxation matters most at high heart rates. Diastole shortens most as the rate rises, so the ventricles must relax quickly to have time to fill. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §10) Contraction is not the same as ejection. The ventricles start contracting a moment before blood leaves: pressure must first rise above the pressure in the aorta or pulmonary artery. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §4) Source note: Lecture connection: Circulation 1 (cardiac muscle cannot be tetanised; contractility through β₁ receptors and cAMP; xanthines; vagal effect) and the ECG lecture (ST segment and QT interval). The cellular detail (intercalated discs, phases of the action potential, CICR, SERCA2a and NCX) is standard physiology added as foundation. 9. Understanding Check 1. How does β₁ stimulation make heart muscle both contract more strongly and relax faster? A. The plateau is shortened, so less Ca²⁺ is needed for each beat B. More Ca²⁺ is released, and SERCA2a is freed to pump it back faster C. Gap junctions open wider, so the cells contract and relax together D. Vagal fibres are switched off, which removes their braking effect Show answer and explanation Answer: B. PKA increases Ca²⁺ entry and release, giving a stronger beat. It also disables phospholamban, the brake on SERCA2a, so Ca²⁺ is pumped back faster and the muscle relaxes sooner. 2. According to the lecture, why can caffeine and theophylline make heart muscle contract more strongly? A. They block the breakdown of cAMP, so cAMP builds up B. They open fast Na⁺ channels, so the upstroke is larger C. They block β₁ receptors, so noradrenaline acts longer D. They slow SERCA2a, so more Ca²⁺ stays in the cell Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Xanthines block phosphodiesterase, the enzyme that breaks down cAMP. More cAMP means more PKA activity and stronger contraction. 3. A ventricle contracts more strongly because it was filled with more blood. Is this a positive inotropic effect? A. Yes, because any stronger contraction counts as inotropy B. Yes, because extra filling releases more Ca²⁺ from the SR C. No, because inotropy only describes the heart rate D. No, because inotropy means stronger at the same filling Show answer and explanation Answer: D. A stronger beat caused by more filling is the Frank–Starling effect. Inotropy is a change in the muscle's own strength at the same filling. 4. Strong vagal stimulation mainly affects the heart by: A. Weakening ventricular contraction more than heart rate B. Blocking the gap junctions between ventricular cells C. Slowing the SA node and weakening atrial contraction D. Shortening the refractory period of the ventricles Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Acetylcholine slows the SA node and weakens the atria. Few vagal fibres reach ventricular muscle, so the direct effect on ventricular strength is small. 5. Why does fast relaxation matter more at high heart rates? A. Diastole shortens most, so filling must happen faster B. Systole lengthens, so contraction must end sooner C. The refractory period disappears at high heart rates D. Ca²⁺ stops entering the cells when the rate is high Show answer and explanation Answer: A. As the rate rises, diastole is cut far more than systole. A ventricle that relaxes slowly has too little time left to fill. 10. Chapter Essentials The chain of events: electrical signal → Ca²⁺ enters → the SR releases more Ca²⁺ → myosin pulls on actin → Ca²⁺ is removed → the muscle relaxes. Intercalated discs: gap junctions pass current from cell to cell; desmosomes and fascia adherens hold the cells together and pass on their pull. The cells act as one unit (a functional syncytium). The atria and ventricles are separate units. Fibrous tissue insulates them, and the AV node is the only normal electrical route between them. The ventricular action potential: phase 0 is Na⁺ rushing in; phase 2, the plateau, is Ca²⁺ in balanced by K⁺ out; phase 3 is K⁺ out. On the ECG, the plateau is the ST segment. CICR: a little Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels opens RyR2, and the SR releases much more. Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, and myosin pulls on actin. Relaxation needs energy: SERCA2a pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR, and NCX pushes it out of the cell. No tetanus: the long refractory period lasts most of the contraction, so the ventricles always relax and refill. Sympathetic (β₁ → cAMP → PKA): stronger contraction and faster relaxation (phospholamban released). Xanthines raise cAMP by blocking its breakdown. Vagal stimulation slows the SA node and weakens the atria. Inotropy is strength at the same filling; lusitropy is speed of relaxation; chronotropy is heart rate.","cardiac-conduction":"Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity 1. Why does the heart need a conducting system? Pumping works only if the chambers contract in the right order and at the right moment: the atria before the ventricles, so the atria can top up the ventricles first; all parts of each ventricle together, so pressure builds fast enough to eject blood. That gives the heart two electrical jobs: start each beat, and deliver the signal in the right order. The specialised conducting system does both. Unlike skeletal muscle, the heart does not need a nerve impulse for every contraction. Specialised heart cells fire on their own; this is automaticity. The nerves only speed this built-in rhythm up or slow it down ( §7). As the lecture summarises, depolarisation spreads from the SA node to the AV node, then down the bundle of His to the rest of the heart, and contraction starts after depolarisation. 2. The pathway of cardiac electrical conduction SA node → atrial muscle → AV node → bundle of His → right and left bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular muscle Structure Where it is and what it does SA node (sinoatrial node) In the wall of the right atrium, where the superior vena cava joins it. Starts each beat. Internodal pathways Carry the signal across the right atrium to the AV node. Bachmann's bundle also carries it to the left atrium. AV node (atrioventricular node) Low in the right atrium, in the wall between the two atria, just behind the tricuspid valve. Holds the signal back briefly. Bundle of His Carries the signal from the AV node through the fibrous barrier into the wall between the ventricles. Right and left bundle branches Run down each side of the wall between the ventricles. Purkinje fibres Spread through the inner walls of both ventricles and hand the signal to the muscle. Figure 1. Specialised cardiac conducting system. Image credit Image: OpenStax College · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. What are the internodal pathways? The lecture names three: anterior (including Bachmann's bundle), middle (Wenckebach) and posterior (Thorel). They are routes the signal prefers, not insulated wires; the signal also spreads through ordinary atrial muscle. Bachmann's bundle matters most because it carries the signal across to the left atrium, so both atria contract almost together. Source note: The three internodal tracts are named as in the lecture. Most anatomists now describe them as preferred routes through atrial muscle rather than separate insulated bundles. 3. Why is the SA node the normal pacemaker? Because it reaches threshold first. A working ventricular muscle cell sits at a steady −90 mV until a neighbour excites it. An SA node cell never sits still. After each beat, its voltage immediately starts creeping upwards by itself until it reaches threshold and fires. This slow climb is the pacemaker potential, also called spontaneous diastolic depolarisation: spontaneous (by itself), diastolic (between beats), depolarisation (becoming less negative). The three phases of the SA node action potential: Phase 4: the slow climb. At its most negative, about −60 mV, the cell opens special channels that let positive ions, mainly Na⁺, leak in. This leak is the funny current (I_f), and its channels are called HCN channels. It is called \"funny\" because most channels open when a cell becomes less negative, but these open when it becomes more negative. Later in the climb, Ca²⁺ channels start to open too, and the outward K⁺ leak fades. The voltage rises steadily towards threshold, about −40 mV. Phase 0: the upstroke. At threshold, L-type Ca²⁺ channels open and Ca²⁺ rushes in. This upstroke is slower than a ventricular cell's, which is driven by fast Na⁺ channels. The SA node never gets negative enough to use its fast Na⁺ channels. Phase 3: repolarisation. The Ca²⁺ channels close, K⁺ flows out, and the voltage falls back to about −60 mV. The climb then starts again. There is no phase 1 or 2: an SA node cell has no plateau. Figure 2. Pacemaker versus ventricular action potentials. Image credit Image: Jonker, Barnett, Boink & Christoffels, Cells (2024), Figure 1 · CC BY 4.0 · unmodified. Source note: Voltages are typical approximate values (Guyton and Hall). The lecture covers the SA node's location and its role as pacemaker; the ionic detail is foundation. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why does an SA node cell fire again without any nerve signal? A. The vagus nerve releases acetylcholine that triggers each new beat B. Its voltage creeps up by itself after each beat until it reaches threshold C. Neighbouring atrial muscle cells excite it at the end of each beat D. Its resting voltage stays at −90 mV until stretch opens Na⁺ channels Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The SA node has no steady resting voltage. The funny current and later Ca²⁺ currents push the voltage up after every beat until it reaches threshold, so it fires again on its own. 2. Why is the funny current called \"funny\"? A. It flows only when the heart rate is unusually high B. It is carried by Ca²⁺ instead of Na⁺ like other currents C. Its channels open when the cell becomes more negative D. It flows outwards even though it makes the cell less negative Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Most channels open as a cell becomes less negative. HCN channels open as it becomes more negative, just after a beat, and let positive ions in, which starts the next climb. 3. Why is the SA node's upstroke slower than a ventricular cell's? A. It must wait for the AV node to release the signal first B. Its gap junctions slow the entry of Na⁺ from its neighbours C. It starts from −90 mV, so it has further to climb D. It is driven by Ca²⁺ through L-type channels, not fast Na⁺ Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The SA node never gets negative enough to use fast Na⁺ channels, so its upstroke relies on slower L-type Ca²⁺ channels. 4. If other conducting cells can fire spontaneously, why doesn't each set its own rhythm? The lecture puts it simply: all parts of the conducting system can fire on their own, but they are controlled by the SA node, the pacemaker. The reason is speed. The SA node climbs to threshold fastest. Each beat it starts spreads through the conducting system and fires the slower cells before they reach threshold on their own. That resets them, so their own rhythm never gets a chance. The fastest pacemaker wins; the others still have automaticity, but they are always beaten to it. Being driven repeatedly at a faster rate also temporarily suppresses the slower cells' own firing. This is overdrive suppression, and it is why there can be a short pause before a backup pacemaker takes over. What if the SA node fails? The next fastest pacemaker takes over and produces an escape rhythm: Pacemaker Typical rate SA node 60–100 beats/min (normal resting rhythm) AV junction about 40–60 beats/min Ventricles ( Purkinje fibres) about 20–40 beats/min An escape rhythm keeps the ventricles beating, but more slowly, so it may not pump enough blood. 5. Why does the AV node delay electrical conduction? The AV node conducts slowly. Its cells are small, they have few gap junctions, and like the SA node their upstroke is driven by Ca²⁺. The signal takes about a tenth of a second to get through. Why is that delay useful? It gives the atria time to finish contracting before the ventricles start. The atrial kick pushes the last portion of blood into the ventricles while the mitral and tricuspid valves are still open. Most filling, about 70% in the lecture, has already happened passively; the kick only tops it up. You can see the delay on the ECG. The lecture describes the PR interval, normally 0.12–0.20 s, as the time from the start of atrial depolarisation to the start of ventricular depolarisation. Most of that time is the signal crawling through the AV node. ( → ECG, §4) Why can't the signal simply spread from the atria to the ventricles? A ring of fibrous tissue between them does not conduct. The AV node and bundle of His are the only normal route across. Because the AV node can only pass impulses so fast, it also protects the ventricles from being driven by a very fast atrial rhythm. 6. Why is ventricular conduction so rapid? After the AV node, the signal enters the His–Purkinje system and speeds up dramatically: Tissue Approximate conduction speed AV node about 0.05 m/s Ordinary ventricular muscle about 0.3–0.5 m/s Purkinje fibres about 2–4 m/s Purkinje fibres are fast because they are wide, packed with gap junctions, and use fast Na⁺ channels for their upstroke. They spread the signal over the inner walls of both ventricles in a few hundredths of a second, so the whole ventricular muscle is activated within about 0.1 s, the width of the QRS complex. The order is roughly: the wall between the ventricles first, then the inner walls near the apex, then outwards through the muscle to the outer surface, and the base last. The details belong in the ECG chapter. Slow in one place, fast in another. The two speeds do two different jobs: Slow AV conduction gets the timing between chambers right: the atria finish before the ventricles start. Fast His–Purkinje conduction gets the coordination within the ventricles right: all the muscle contracts together, so pressure builds quickly. Follow one heartbeat's signal What it shows: a simple heart outline with the SA node, AV node, bundle of His, bundle branches and Purkinje fibres labelled. Below it, an ECG strip. Controls: Play sends a glowing signal along the pathway, and the ECG strip draws in step with it: the atria light up with the P wave; the signal pauses at the AV node, labelled \"AV delay\", along the flat PR segment; it then races down the His bundle and the Purkinje fibres, and both ventricles light up together with the QRS complex. Step buttons let students stop at each stage. Schematic timing. OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO Cardiac Conduction and ECG — Alila Medical Media Trace SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje fibres, and notice why atrial activation precedes ventricular activation. ECG details are supplementary. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation. Watch video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted. Source note: Conduction speeds are approximate textbook values (Guyton and Hall). The lecture gives the PR interval (0.12–0.20 s). Understanding Check: Sections 4–6 1. Every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. Why doesn't the AV junction set its own rhythm? A. Its own firing is blocked by the fibrous skeleton B. Each SA impulse fires it before it reaches threshold C. It only fires when the vagus nerve stimulates it D. It fires faster, but its beats are too weak to count Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The SA node's impulse arrives and fires the AV junction before it can reach threshold by itself, resetting it every beat. The fastest pacemaker wins. 2. The SA node stops firing, and the ventricles keep beating at about 45 beats/min. What is most likely setting this rhythm? A. The SA node, firing more slowly because of vagal tone B. The Purkinje fibres in the ventricle walls C. A backup pacemaker in the AV junction D. Bachmann's bundle in the wall of the atria Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The AV junction's own rate is about 40–60 beats/min, so it is the next pacemaker in line. A ventricular escape rhythm is usually slower, about 20–40 beats/min. 3. On the ECG, which interval mostly reflects the AV-node delay? A. The QT interval B. The QRS duration C. The ST segment D. The PR interval Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The PR interval runs from the start of atrial depolarisation to the start of ventricular depolarisation. Most of that time is the signal crossing the AV node. 4. Why must conduction be slow in the AV node but fast in the Purkinje fibres? A. Slow AV conduction lets the atria finish first; fast Purkinje conduction makes the ventricles act together B. Slow AV conduction saves energy; fast Purkinje conduction lets each ventricle contract twice in every beat C. Slow AV conduction prevents tetanus; fast Purkinje conduction shortens the ventricular refractory period D. Slow AV conduction lets the SA node recover; fast Purkinje conduction raises the overall heart rate Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The AV delay gets the timing between atria and ventricles right. The fast Purkinje network makes all the ventricular muscle contract together. 7. How does the autonomic nervous system influence pacemaker activity? The key rule: the faster the phase 4 climb reaches threshold, the sooner the next beat. The nerves change heart rate by changing that climb. At rest, the vagus is already braking the heart. An SA node with no nerve input fires at about 100 beats/min. At rest, the vagus nerve releases acetylcholine all the time ( vagal tone), holding the rate down to about 60–80. The lecture's evidence: heart rate is controlled mainly by this constant vagal discharge, so cutting the vagus (vagotomy) or blocking it with atropine makes the heart rate rise. Sympathetic stimulation speeds the climb. Noradrenaline acts on β₁ receptors and raises cAMP inside the SA node cells. More funny current and more Ca²⁺ current flow, the phase 4 climb gets steeper, threshold comes sooner, and the heart rate rises. Conduction through the AV node also speeds up, so the PR interval shortens. Vagal stimulation slows the climb. Acetylcholine acts on M₂ receptors. It lowers cAMP, so there is less funny current. It also opens extra K⁺ channels, so the cell starts each climb lower (more negative) and climbs more slowly. Threshold comes later, and the heart rate falls. AV conduction slows, so the PR interval lengthens. How nerves set the heart rate What it shows: the SA node voltage over 3 seconds, with a dashed threshold line. Controls: four buttons: Strong vagal (starts lower, shallow climb, about 41 beats/min); Rest (vagal tone, about 75 beats/min); No nerve input (about 100 beats/min); Sympathetic (steep climb, about 150 beats/min). The trace redraws for each, the beats-per-minute readout updates, and a one-line explanation names what changed (the slope, the starting voltage, or both). Schematic. Two words for two effects: Chronotropy ( chrono = time): an effect on heart rate. Dromotropy ( dromo = running): an effect on conduction speed, especially through the AV node. Sympathetic stimulation is positively chronotropic and dromotropic; vagal stimulation is negatively chronotropic and dromotropic. Source note: The intrinsic SA node rate of about 100 beats/min is a standard textbook value. The lecture (Circulation 2) states that heart rate is controlled mainly by tonic vagal discharge, and that vagotomy and atropine raise it. 8. Clinical significance of conduction disturbances Electrical problems come in two kinds: the pacemaker fails, or the signal is delayed or blocked on its way. The SA node fails. It fires too slowly or pauses. A backup pacemaker may take over with a slower escape rhythm ( §4). The signal is blocked at the AV node (heart block). Think of it as three levels of trouble: First degree: every impulse gets through, but late. The PR interval is longer than 0.20 s. Second degree: some impulses don't get through. Some atrial beats are not followed by a ventricular beat; those beats are \"dropped\". Third degree (complete): no impulses get through. The atria keep beating at the SA node's rate, and the ventricles beat independently at a slow escape rate. In complete block, the atria sometimes contract while the tricuspid valve is shut. Blood is pushed back into the neck veins, giving the giant \"a\" waves the lecture describes in the JVP. ( → Pulses and JVP) Both kinds of problem reduce pumping: the heart rate may be too slow, and the atria and ventricles no longer work in step. Understanding Check 1. According to the lecture, atropine (which blocks vagal acetylcholine) raises the resting heart rate. What does this show? A. Atropine directly speeds up the funny current in the SA node B. Sympathetic nerves cannot act on the heart at rest C. At rest, the vagus nerve continuously slows the SA node D. The SA node needs acetylcholine before it can fire Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Removing the vagal brake lets the SA node rise towards its own rate of about 100 beats/min. So at rest, constant vagal tone is holding the rate down. 2. How does noradrenaline make the SA node fire faster? A. It makes the cell rest at −90 mV, closer to the Na⁺ threshold B. It steepens the slow climb of phase 4, so threshold comes sooner C. It adds a plateau, which shortens the time between beats D. It slows the AV node, so the SA node has less to wait for Show answer and explanation Answer: B. β₁ stimulation raises cAMP, which increases the funny current and Ca²⁺ current. The climb gets steeper, so each beat starts sooner. 3. Vagal acetylcholine opens extra K⁺ channels in the SA node. Why does this slow the heart? A. K⁺ entering the cell makes phase 0 much larger and slower B. K⁺ blocks the gap junctions between the SA node and the atria C. The plateau lengthens, so the refractory period lasts longer D. The cell starts lower and climbs more slowly, so threshold comes later Show answer and explanation Answer: D. K⁺ leaving the cell pulls the voltage down, so each climb starts more negative. With less funny current the climb is also shallower, so it takes longer to reach threshold. 4. In complete heart block, the atria beat at 75 per minute and the ventricles at 35 per minute. Why? A. The ventricles follow their own slower escape pacemaker B. The SA node fires twice for every ventricular beat C. The AV node lets only every other impulse through D. The vagus nerve slows the ventricles but not the atria Show answer and explanation Answer: A. In complete block no atrial impulse reaches the ventricles. A ventricular escape pacemaker takes over at its own slow rate, independent of the atria. Letting every other impulse through would be second-degree block. 9. Chapter Essentials Pathway: SA node → atria → AV node → bundle of His → bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular muscle. Automaticity: specialised cells fire on their own; the nerves only change the rate. The SA node sits where the superior vena cava joins the right atrium. It is the pacemaker because it reaches threshold first. The SA node action potential: phase 4 is a slow climb, mainly the funny current (Na⁺ in, through channels that open when the cell is most negative); phase 0 is Ca²⁺ in; phase 3 is K⁺ out. There is no plateau. The fastest pacemaker wins. If the SA node fails, the AV junction (about 40–60/min) or the ventricles (about 20–40/min) take over with an escape rhythm. The AV node sits just behind the tricuspid valve. It conducts slowly (about 0.05 m/s), so the atria finish contracting first. On the ECG this is the PR interval (0.12–0.20 s). Purkinje fibres conduct fast (2–4 m/s), so both ventricles contract together, within about 0.1 s (the QRS). Vagal tone: the SA node's own rate is about 100/min. At rest the vagus holds it at 60–80, which is why atropine or vagotomy raises the heart rate. Nerves change the phase 4 climb. Sympathetic (β₁, cAMP) makes it steeper and the rate faster. Vagal (M₂, more K⁺, less funny current) starts it lower and makes it shallower, so the rate is slower. Heart block: first degree, late; second degree, some dropped; third degree, none get through, with the atria and ventricles beating independently.","cardiac-cycle":"The Cardiac Cycle 1. One heartbeat: what the atria and ventricles are doing The cardiac cycle is everything that happens in the heart during one heartbeat: the atria and ventricles each contract and relax once, in a fixed order. Systole means contraction and diastole means relaxation. On their own, the words are incomplete. Always ask systole of which chambers? The atria and ventricles are often doing different things, though for a large part of each beat they are both relaxed. Why do the atria go first? Each beat starts with an electrical impulse from the SA node in the right atrium. The impulse spreads across the atria first, then pauses briefly at the AV node before reaching the ventricles. So the atria are activated, and contract, just before the ventricles. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §5) Follow one beat: Atrial systole. At the end of ventricular diastole, the atria contract and push a final portion of blood into ventricles that are already nearly full. Ventricular systole. The ventricles contract and eject blood. Meanwhile the atria relax and begin refilling with blood returning from the veins. Both chambers in diastole. The ventricles now relax too, so both are relaxed together. For a brief moment the ventricles still cannot fill, because their pressure is higher than atrial pressure. As they relax further, their pressure falls below atrial pressure and blood flows in passively. Atrial systole again, and the next beat begins. How long does each part take? At about 75 beats/min, one cycle lasts 60 ÷ 75 = 0.8 s. Part of the cycle Duration Where it comes from Ventricular systole ≈ 0.3 s Typical resting value Ventricular diastole ≈ 0.5 s 0.8 − 0.3 Atrial systole ≈ 0.1 s Last part of ventricular diastole Both chambers relaxed ≈ 0.4 s 0.5 − 0.1, half of every beat These are rounded teaching values; the lecture gives no phase durations. That shared 0.4 s matters. After a short pause while ventricular pressure is still falling, it is when most ventricular filling happens. This is why atrial contraction is only a top-up. It is also why very fast heart rates, which shorten this period most, can limit filling ( §10). One heartbeat at 75 beats/min: what the atria and ventricles are doing Two aligned bars across one 0.8 s cycle. Atria: systole for the first 0.1 s, then diastole. Ventricles: systole from 0.1 s to 0.4 s, then diastole. From 0.4 s to 0.8 s both are relaxed: first a short pause while the ventricle is still relaxing with no filling, then passive filling. Proportions approximate, at 75 beats/min. What drives each step? Every phase follows one chain: Electrical signal → contraction or relaxation → pressure change → valves open or close → blood moves and volume changes The electrical signal starts the chain but never moves blood or valves directly. Each link takes a moment, so the steps happen in sequence, not all at once. The next section explains the key link: how pressure moves the valves. 2. The one rule every valve follows The heart has four valves, in two pairs: AV (atrioventricular) valves sit between each atrium and its ventricle: the mitral valve on the left and the tricuspid valve on the right. Semilunar valves sit between each ventricle and its artery: the aortic valve on the left and the pulmonary valve on the right. All four obey one rule. A valve opens when pressure upstream (in the chamber blood is coming from) is higher than pressure downstream (where blood is going). It closes when that difference reverses. How can pressure alone do this? Each valve is made of thin flaps called cusps, shaped to open in one direction only. Higher pressure upstream pushes the cusps apart, and blood flows through. When downstream pressure becomes higher, blood starts to move backwards and pushes the cusps together until they seal. No nerve or electrical signal is involved. ( → Introduction, §3: valves keep flow one-way) What about the papillary muscles? The AV valve cusps are tethered by chordae tendineae to papillary muscles in the ventricular wall. These muscles contract with the ventricle, but they do not open or close the valve. They hold the cusps in place so that rising ventricular pressure cannot push them inside out into the atrium. Now apply the rule to the left heart: left atrium → mitral valve → left ventricle → aortic valve → aorta. Mitral valve opens P LA > P LV Blood enters the ventricle Aortic valve opens P LV > P aorta Blood leaves the ventricle Here P means pressure, LA is left atrium and LV is left ventricle. The two valves respond to different pressure differences, so there are moments when both are closed at once. Those moments explain two phases you will meet in Sections 4 and 6, when the ventricle's pressure changes but its volume does not. One refinement comes later: moving blood carries momentum, so flow and valve closure can lag slightly behind a change in pressure ( §6). The right heart follows the same rule at lower pressures, through the tricuspid and pulmonary valves. We follow the left heart first and return to the right in Section 10. 3. Filling: how blood enters the relaxed ventricle Blood keeps returning to the heart all the time, even while the ventricles are contracting and their AV valves are shut. During that time the relaxed atria act as reservoirs, collecting the returning blood, so atrial pressure slowly rises. When the ventricle relaxes, its pressure falls. As soon as ventricular pressure drops below atrial pressure, the mitral valve opens and the stored blood rushes in. No contraction is needed for this: blood simply moves down the pressure difference. Why is filling fast at first, then slow? At first the difference is large: the atrium is full and pressurised, and the ventricle has only just relaxed. So filling is rapid. As blood enters, ventricular pressure rises only a little, because the relaxed ventricle stretches easily. It stays low, as the lecture notes, but it creeps closer to atrial pressure. With a smaller difference, inflow slows. This slower period is diastasis. Throughout passive filling, blood flows from the veins through the open atrium into the ventricle, so the atrium is acting as a conduit. Why do the atria contract at all? Near the end of diastole, the atria contract and push in a final volume, the atrial kick, with the atrium acting as a booster pump. The lecture describes filling as about 70% passive and 30% atrial. These are teaching proportions: at rest the atrial share is often 10–30%. The kick matters more in two situations: A stiff ventricle resists passive stretching, so less blood enters passively and the active push makes up more of the total. A fast heart rate shortens diastole, leaving less time for passive filling, so the final push contributes a larger share. When filling ends, the ventricle holds its end-diastolic volume (EDV), about 130 mL in the lecture's example. This is the volume it starts its next contraction with ( §7). Ventricular filling: why it is fast, then slow, then topped up Left atrial and left ventricular pressure across ventricular diastole, with ventricular volume beneath. When the mitral valve opens the pressure gap is large and volume rises steeply (rapid filling). The gap then narrows and volume rises slowly (diastasis). Atrial contraction makes the a wave and adds a final step of volume up to EDV. Ventricular pressure stays low throughout. Schematic; pressures not to scale. What is happening electrically during filling? The ventricles repolarised near the end of their contraction ( §5), before filling began. During rapid filling and diastasis, no excitation wave is spreading through the working heart muscle, and none is needed: the pressure difference alone moves the blood. The SA node is not idle, though. Its pacemaker cells are slowly depolarising towards threshold. When they fire, the impulse spreads across the atria (the P wave on an ECG) and atrial contraction follows. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §3) Understanding Check — Section 3 A patient's atria stop contracting effectively, but ventricular relaxation is normal. Why does most ventricular filling continue? A. Venous blood is pushed through the closed AV valves by contracting veins B. Ventricular contraction draws blood back from the atria during systole C. The SA node keeps triggering small atrial contractions during diastole D. Atrial pressure exceeds relaxed ventricular pressure, so blood flows passively Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Filling needs only a pressure difference across an open AV valve. Atrial systole adds a final top-up. Losing it reduces filling but does not stop it. 4. Isovolumetric contraction: contracting, but no blood leaves We left the ventricle full, holding its EDV, with the atria just finished contracting. After the AV nodal pause, the impulse races through the His–Purkinje system, activating the ventricular muscle almost all at once. On an ECG, this ventricular activation is the QRS complex (more in §9). ( → Cardiac Conduction, §6) Why does force lag slightly behind activation? Electrical activation first has to release calcium inside each muscle cell; only then can the cell develop force. So ventricular pressure starts rising a moment after the QRS. ( → Cardiac Muscle, §4) Why does the mitral valve close so quickly? Atrial pressure is only a few mmHg, so ventricular pressure overtakes it almost as soon as contraction begins. The pressure difference across the mitral and tricuspid valves reverses and they close. This closure contributes to the first heart sound ( S1). But ventricular pressure is still far below aortic pressure, so the aortic valve stays closed. Now both valves are closed, and the ventricle is a sealed chamber. Why does pressure climb so steeply? Blood cannot be compressed. With no way in or out, the volume cannot change, so all the force the muscle develops goes into raising pressure. The muscle fibres build tension but barely shorten, as the lecture notes. Pressure therefore climbs steeply while volume stays fixed at EDV. This is isovolumetric contraction ( iso = same, volumetric = volume). It is the start of ventricular systole, not yet ejection. Mitral closed Aortic closed Pressure ↑ Volume = EDV Meanwhile, the atria have relaxed and are again collecting venous blood behind closed AV valves. As ventricular pressure rises, the closed AV valves are the only part of the sealed chamber that can give a little. As the lecture puts it, they bulge back into the atrium. This causes a small bump in atrial pressure, the c wave. The atrium is not contracting; it is being pushed on. What ends this phase? Ventricular pressure must exceed pressure in its outflow artery. The lecture uses about 80 mmHg for aortic diastolic pressure and about 10 mmHg for pulmonary arterial diastolic pressure. These are typical reference values, not fixed thresholds in every person. Contraction is not the same as ejection: the ventricle must first overcome the pressure already in the artery. Source note: The lecture says closure of the AV valves \"initiates systole\". More precisely, ventricular contraction starts first, and the rising pressure then closes the AV valves. Valve closure marks the start of isovolumetric contraction rather than causing it. Isovolumetric contraction in three steps Step 1: ventricular pressure rises above atrial pressure, so the mitral and tricuspid valves close. Step 2: all four valves are closed; ventricular pressure rises while volume stays the same. Step 3: ventricular pressure exceeds arterial pressure, so the aortic and pulmonary valves open and ejection begins. The atria stay relaxed and keep filling throughout. Understanding Check — Section 4 1. Just after the mitral valve closes, LV pressure climbs steeply but LV volume does not change. Why? A. Both the mitral and aortic valves are closed, so blood has no exit B. The ventricular muscle has not yet been depolarised by the impulse C. Blood leaves the ventricle as fast as it enters from the atrium D. The aortic valve is open, but aortic pressure prevents any outflow Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Contraction is already under way and raising pressure, but with both valves shut there is nowhere for blood to go. 2. A person's aortic diastolic pressure is higher than usual. Ventricular activation and contractility are unchanged. What happens to isovolumetric contraction? A. It shortens, because the aortic valve opens at a lower LV pressure B. It stays the same, because valve opening depends on electrical timing C. It lengthens, because LV pressure must climb higher to open the valve D. It disappears, because the mitral valve stays open until ejection Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The aortic valve opens only when LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure. A higher starting aortic pressure means the ventricle must build more pressure first, so, all else equal, this phase lasts longer. 5. Ejection: why blood finally leaves As soon as LV pressure rises above aortic pressure, the aortic valve opens (on the right, the pulmonary valve opens the same way). Blood flows into the artery and ventricular volume begins to fall. Isovolumetric contraction is over; ejection has begun. Why is ejection rapid at first? At the moment the valve opens, the muscle is contracting strongly and the pressure difference pushing blood out is at its largest. Blood accelerates out quickly: rapid ejection. Ventricular pressure keeps rising for a while, peaking at about 120 mmHg in the lecture's example, even though volume is already falling. Pressure and volume measure different things and need not move together. On the volume curve, this is the steep part of the fall. Why does it then slow down? Two things shrink the pressure difference at once: The muscle weakens. As contraction passes its peak, ventricular force and pressure fall. The aorta fills. Blood entering the aorta stretches its elastic wall and raises aortic pressure. With the valve open, LV and aortic pressures now rise and fall almost together. ( → Blood Pressure, §2) With a smaller difference, outflow slows: reduced ejection, the gentler part of the volume fall. Late in this phase, ventricular pressure can dip slightly below aortic pressure while blood still flows forward. Moving blood carries momentum and takes time to stop. Source note: The lecture describes ejection as \"initially slow, then rapid\". Standard physiology, including the volume curve on every Wiggers diagram, shows the reverse: rapid ejection first, then reduced ejection. We teach the standard order. Meanwhile, the atria stay relaxed behind closed AV valves. Early in ejection, atrial pressure briefly dips, the x descent. The atrium is finishing its relaxation, and the contracting ventricle pulls the AV valve ring downward, enlarging the atrium. After that, returning venous blood fills the atrium steadily and its pressure rises again. So while the ventricles empty, the atria store the blood that will rush in once the ventricles relax. Electrically, the ventricles were activated before ejection began, and no new impulse is needed to keep it going. During the later part of ejection the ventricular muscle repolarises (the T wave on an ECG), which sets up the relaxation that follows. Repolarisation does not switch contraction off instantly; force declines over the following moments. When ejection ends, some blood remains: the end-systolic volume (ESV). Section 7 shows how EDV and ESV give the stroke volume. Understanding Check — Section 5 During early ejection, ventricular volume is falling, yet ventricular pressure is still rising. How can both happen at once? Reveal explanation Volume falls because the aortic valve is open and blood is leaving. Pressure rises because the muscle is still increasing its force faster than blood escapes. Ejection does not require pressure to be falling. 6. Closing the outflow valves and isovolumetric relaxation Why does the aortic valve close? Late in ejection, blood was still coasting forward on momentum, even though ventricular pressure had dipped below aortic pressure. That forward flow now slows to a stop and briefly reverses. The tiny backflow pushes the aortic cusps together and they seal (the rule from §2). The lecture calls this short interval, from the end of forward ejection to semilunar valve closure, protodiastole. Source note: The lecture times protodiastole from the moment \"ventricular pressure starts falling\". In our account, pressure begins to fall during reduced ejection, while blood is still leaving. We therefore date protodiastole from the end of forward flow, as most physiology texts do. Either way, it ends when the semilunar valves close. Aortic valve closure contributes the aortic component of the second heart sound ( S2). The pulmonary valve closes the same way, slightly later ( §10). On the aortic pressure trace, closure leaves a small dip and rebound, the dicrotic notch. The dip is the brief backflow; the rebound is the stretched aortic wall recoiling against the now-closed valve. It is not a second contraction. Why doesn't filling restart straight away? The aortic valve is now shut, but ventricular pressure is still above atrial pressure, so the mitral valve is also shut. Both valves are closed again, and the ventricle is once more a sealed chamber. Why does pressure fall so steeply? This is Section 4 in reverse. The muscle relaxes as calcium is pumped out of the cytoplasm, so its tension drops. Volume cannot change, so the falling tension shows up entirely as falling pressure. Pressure drops steeply while volume stays at ESV. This is isovolumetric relaxation. ( → Cardiac Muscle, §5) Mitral closed Aortic closed Pressure ↓ Volume = ESV Meanwhile, the atria have kept filling behind closed AV valves since the x descent ( §5). Atrial pressure reaches its peak now: the v wave. No atrial contraction is involved; it is simply blood accumulating. When ventricular pressure finally falls below atrial pressure, the mitral valve opens. The stored atrial blood rushes in, atrial pressure drops (the y descent), and rapid filling begins. We are back at Section 3. Electrically, ventricular repolarisation (the T wave) happened during late ejection, before most of this relaxation. The T wave, aortic valve closure and mitral valve opening are three separate moments, in that order, not one event. Understanding Check — Section 6 1. The aortic valve has just closed. Ventricular pressure is falling quickly, yet the mitral valve is still shut. Why? A. Atrial pressure is falling faster than ventricular pressure at this moment B. Ventricular pressure is still higher than left atrial pressure at this moment C. The mitral valve opens only when the next P wave triggers atrial systole D. Ventricular repolarisation must finish before either valve is able to move Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The mitral valve opens only when LV pressure falls below LA pressure. Until then, the ventricle relaxes with both valves closed. 2. The atrium is not contracting, yet its pressure rises steadily during ventricular systole. Which wave is this, and why does it happen? Reveal explanation The v wave. Venous blood keeps entering the atrium while the AV valve is closed, so blood accumulates and pressure rises. When the AV valve opens, that stored blood flows into the ventricle. 7. How much blood does the ventricle pump? A ventricle never empties completely. EDV is the volume just before contraction; ESV is what remains after ejection. The difference is the stroke volume (SV), the volume ejected in one beat. Stroke volume SV = EDV − ESV Example: 130 mL − 50 mL = 80 mL Why is some blood always left behind? Ejection stops when the pressure difference and the blood's momentum run out, not when the chamber is empty. The remaining ESV is a reserve: a stronger contraction can eject some of it. The ejection fraction (EF) is the share of EDV that is ejected: Ejection fraction EF = SV EDV × 100 % Example: (80 ÷ 130) × 100 ≈ 61.5% The lecture uses EDV ≈ 130 mL, SV ≈ 70–90 mL and EF ≈ 65% as typical values; small differences between rounded examples are not contradictions. EF is the fraction of that ventricle's end-diastolic blood ejected, not a fraction of total blood volume. Because it compares what is ejected with what was available, EF is widely used clinically as a measure of how well the ventricle contracts. A low EF suggests weakened contraction. ( → Heart Failure) Two ways to raise stroke volume. Since SV = EDV − ESV, the ventricle can eject more by: filling more (a larger EDV), or emptying more (a smaller ESV), for example through stronger contraction. The Cardiac Output chapter explains what controls each. ( → Cardiac Output, §3) Now trace the volume through one beat. It rises during filling and reaches EDV after atrial systole. It stays flat during isovolumetric contraction, then falls during ejection, steeply then gradually. It stays flat at ESV during isovolumetric relaxation, then rises again when the mitral valve opens. Stroke volume and ejection fraction in one picture A ventricle filled to its end-diastolic volume of 130 mL. After ejection, 50 mL remains (end-systolic volume). The 80 mL that left is the stroke volume, which is 61.5% of 130 mL: the ejection fraction. Understanding Check — Section 7 EDV stays at 140 mL, but a drug lowers ESV from 70 mL to 50 mL. What happens to SV and EF? Reveal explanation Before: SV = 140 − 70 = 70 mL; EF = 70 ÷ 140 = 50%. After: SV = 140 − 50 = 90 mL; EF = 90 ÷ 140 ≈ 64.3%. Emptying more completely raises both SV and EF, even with the same filling. 8. The whole cycle on one map Use this after reading the explanation, to rebuild each step from its cause. Start with atrial systole, where the heartbeat begins. What changes? Immediate consequence Next phase SA node fires; atria depolarise Atria contract; final filling to EDV Atrial systole Ventricles depolarise; ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure Mitral valve closes ( S1) Isovolumetric contraction Ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure Aortic valve opens Rapid, then reduced ejection Forward flow stops and briefly reverses ( protodiastole) Aortic valve closes ( S2) Isovolumetric relaxation Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure Mitral valve opens Rapid filling, then diastasis SA node fires again Next atrial systole Next cycle Compare the two isovolumetric phases Isovolumetric contraction Isovolumetric relaxation Muscle Developing force Relaxing Mitral and aortic valves Both closed Both closed Ventricular pressure Rising Falling Ventricular volume Constant at EDV Constant at ESV Atria Relaxed, filling; c wave Relaxed, full; v wave peaks Ends when Aortic valve opens Mitral valve opens Volume is constant in both because neither valve is open. Pressure moves in opposite directions because the muscle is building force in one and relaxing in the other. 9. Reading the Wiggers diagram one trace at a time A Wiggers diagram lines up everything you have just learned on one time axis: the ECG, atrial, ventricular and aortic pressures, ventricular volume and heart sounds. It looks crowded, but every curve follows the events above. We will build it one trace at a time for the left heart. How to read it. Time runs from left to right, and every trace shares the same clock. A vertical line drawn anywhere cuts all the traces at the same instant: it tells you what the ECG, pressures, volume and sounds are doing at that moment. The key insight: valves move where pressure curves cross. By the rule in Section 2, a valve opens or closes exactly when the pressures on its two sides swap order. So on the diagram: The mitral valve closes and opens where the ventricular and atrial pressure curves cross. The aortic valve opens and closes where the ventricular and aortic pressure curves cross. Find those four crossings and you have found the four valve events. Between them, the volume curve tells you whether blood is entering, leaving or neither. The Wiggers diagram, built one step at a time (left heart) One heartbeat plus the start of the next, with the ECG, left atrial, left ventricular and aortic pressures, left ventricular volume and heart sounds on one time axis. The four valve events are where pressure curves cross: mitral closure and opening where the ventricular and atrial curves cross, aortic opening and closure where the ventricular and aortic curves cross. Labels show the P, QRS and T waves; the a, c and v waves and x and y descents; the dicrotic notch; EDV and ESV; and S1 and S2. Schematic; values approximate. Step 1: Atrial systole. The SA node fires and the atria depolarise: the P wave. Shortly afterwards the atria contract, producing the a wave on the atrial pressure trace and topping up the ventricle to EDV. P wave → atrial contraction → a wave → EDV: related, but in sequence, not simultaneous. (In a stiff ventricle, this final push can produce an extra sound, S4.) Step 2: Ventricular contraction begins. After the AV nodal delay, ventricular activation gives the QRS complex. Ventricular pressure then rises and crosses atrial pressure: the mitral valve closes ( S1), and the bulging valve makes the c wave. With both valves shut, the volume trace is flat: isovolumetric contraction. Step 3: Ejection. Ventricular pressure crosses aortic pressure and the aortic valve opens. From here the LV and aortic pressure traces run almost together, because they are connected through the open valve. The volume trace falls steeply ( rapid ejection), then more gently (reduced ejection). Meanwhile atrial pressure dips in the x descent, then begins to climb. Step 4: Relaxation. The T wave appears during reduced ejection as the ventricles repolarise. Forward flow stops and briefly reverses, the aortic valve closes ( S2), and the dicrotic notch marks the aortic trace. Ventricular pressure drops steeply while volume stays flat at ESV; atrial pressure reaches the v wave. Step 5: Filling resumes. Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure; the mitral valve opens, atrial pressure falls ( y descent) and the volume trace rises again. (Rapid inflow can sometimes produce an extra sound, S3.) Read it in four passes Find QRS and mitral closure. QRS represents ventricular depolarisation. Pressure then rises, the mitral valve closes and volume stays at EDV: isovolumetric contraction. Find aortic opening. Left ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure. The volume curve now falls as ejection begins. Find aortic closure. Pressure falls below aortic pressure, producing valve closure and S2. A small dicrotic notch appears on the aortic pressure trace around this event. Ventricular volume then stays at ESV during isovolumetric relaxation. Find mitral reopening. Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure; the mitral valve opens, and the volume curve rises as filling resumes. Why are there a, c and v waves in atrial pressure? Wave Cause a wave Atrial contraction increases atrial pressure. c wave Early ventricular contraction pushes the closed AV valve towards the atrium. x descent Atrial relaxation, then the AV valve ring pulled down during ejection. v wave Venous blood continues entering the atrium while the AV valve is closed. y descent AV valve opens and stored atrial blood empties into the ventricle. Related: full JVP waves and descents → Pulses and JVP, §4 · ECG timing → ECG, §5 · S1 and S2 → Heart Sounds, §2 · S3 and S4 → Heart Sounds, §4 Watch the mechanism SHORT ANIMATION · OPTIONAL Cardiac cycle and Wiggers traces Observe when each valve closes, when volume stops changing and when ejection starts. Play animation DrJanaOfficial; diagram derived from Xavax · Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0. OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO The Cardiac Cycle — Alila Medical Media Identify the four valve transitions and compare the two isovolumetric phases. Watch video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not rehosted. Understanding Check — Section 9 1. On a Wiggers diagram, ventricular pressure rises sharply while ventricular volume stays flat. Which event ends this phase? A. The mitral valve opens as ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure B. The T wave appears and the ventricle begins its electrical recovery C. The aortic valve closes, leaving the dicrotic notch on aortic pressure D. The aortic valve opens as ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The flat volume with rising pressure is isovolumetric contraction. It ends when LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure and blood can leave. 2. The T wave has begun, yet the aortic valve is still open and blood is still leaving. Is this possible? Reveal explanation Yes. Repolarisation happens during reduced ejection. Electrical recovery starts the decline in force, but force and forward flow do not stop instantly. The aortic valve closes only when flow reverses at the end of ejection. 10. Right versus left heart, S2 splitting and heart rate Same rules, lower pressures. Both ventricles are activated in the same beat and follow the same valve rule. The right ventricle pumps into the low-resistance pulmonary circulation, reaching about 25 mmHg; the left reaches about 120 mmHg. Even so, over time both pump almost exactly the same volume per minute. The two circuits are connected in series, so whatever the right ventricle sends through the lungs must come back to the left. Higher pressure does not mean more blood. ( → Introduction, §2) Why the right ventricle ejects first. The lecture notes that the left ventricle starts contracting slightly before the right, yet right ventricular ejection begins first. Both points are correct. The right ventricle only has to exceed about 10 mmHg in the pulmonary artery, versus about 80 mmHg in the aorta, so its isovolumetric phase (contracting with all valves shut, before ejection) is much shorter and the pulmonary valve opens first. The SA node does not send separate beats to each side. Why S2 has two parts. S2 is made of A2 (aortic closure) and P2 (pulmonary closure). A2 normally comes first. During expiration they are close together and often sound single. During inspiration, P2 moves later and the gap widens: physiological splitting of S2. Two effects delay P2: More right-heart filling. Inspiration makes chest pressure more negative, drawing more venous blood into the right heart. The right ventricle fills more and takes longer to eject. Lower pulmonary impedance. The expanding lungs lower the impedance (opposition to flow) of the pulmonary vessels. Blood keeps flowing forward into the pulmonary artery for longer after right ventricular pressure peaks, so the pulmonary valve closes later. A2 may also move slightly earlier. The expanded lung vessels hold more blood for a few beats, so left ventricular filling dips briefly. Source note: The lecture says the valves close \"at the same time\" in expiration. In practice a small gap remains but is usually too short to hear; the teaching point is the inspiratory widening. Mechanism 2 corresponds to the lecture's \"decrease in the impedance of the pulmonary vasculature\". The lecture also lists \"contracting lungs exert a force on the vessels\"; this is not a standard explanation of splitting, so we have not taught it. Related: listening for splitting → Heart Sounds, §3 What happens when heart rate rises? At 75 beats/min, one cycle lasts 0.8 s; at 150 beats/min, only 0.4 s. Both phases shorten, but not equally. Why does diastole lose most of the time? How long systole lasts is tied to how long the ventricular action potential lasts. Faster contraction and repolarisation shorten it a little, giving quicker ejection, as the lecture notes. But diastole is simply whatever time is left before the next beat, so it absorbs most of the cut. That matters, because diastole is when the ventricles fill. At very high rates, or when the ventricle relaxes poorly, there may not be enough time to fill. EDV then falls, and stroke volume falls with it. A faster heart rate does not automatically mean less stroke volume, though. During exercise, greater venous return, faster relaxation and stronger contraction can maintain or even raise it. The heart also cannot be driven into a sustained, tetanic contraction, because its long refractory period forces relaxation between beats. Cycle length = 60 ÷ heart rate. At 75 beats/min it is 0.80 s (800 ms). This tool calculates total cycle length only; it does not model the exact split between systole and diastole. Related: cardiac output and rate → Cardiac Output, §2 Coronary blood flow. The left ventricle's own muscle receives most of its blood flow during diastole. During systole the contracting muscle squeezes the vessels running through it, especially the deeper ones. So marked tachycardia hits twice: it shortens the time for coronary perfusion just as the faster-working heart needs more oxygen. ( → Coronary Circulation, §3 and §5) Understanding Check — Section 10 During inspiration, P2 occurs later than during expiration. Which explanation is best? A. More right-heart filling and lower pulmonary impedance prolong RV ejection B. Inspiration slows conduction in the right bundle, delaying RV activation C. Pulmonary arterial pressure rises on inspiration, so RV ejection starts late D. Less blood returns to the right atrium, so the tricuspid valve closes later Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Both inspiratory effects let the right ventricle keep ejecting for longer, so the pulmonary valve closes later and the A2–P2 gap widens. 11. Understanding Check Predict first, then reveal. Each scenario changes one thing. Work out what happens in the rest of the heart before opening the answer. Scenario 1: Contracting, but no blood leaving. The left ventricle has just started contracting. Its pressure is rising, but its volume is not changing. What are the valves and the left atrium doing? Reveal explanation This is isovolumetric contraction. The mitral valve has just shut, and the aortic valve has not opened yet, because ventricular pressure is still below aortic pressure. With both shut, blood can't get in or out. Meanwhile the left atrium is relaxed and filling with blood from the lungs. Contracting and ejecting are not the same thing. Scenario 2: Relaxing, but no blood entering. Ejection has ended and the aortic valve has shut. Ventricular pressure is falling, but volume is not changing. Why is no blood coming in? Reveal explanation This is isovolumetric relaxation. Ventricular pressure is still higher than atrial pressure, so the mitral valve stays shut. The atrium is full, at its v wave. Once ventricular pressure drops below atrial pressure, the mitral valve opens and blood rushes in. Scenario 3: The atria stop contracting properly. Does ventricular filling stop? Reveal explanation No. Most filling is passive: blood flows in whenever atrial pressure is higher than ventricular pressure. Only the final top-up from atrial contraction is lost. That loss matters most when the ventricle is stiff or the heart rate is fast. Scenario 4: Heart rate doubles. The rate rises from 75 to 150 beats/min. What may happen to filling, stroke volume and blood flow to the heart muscle? Reveal explanation Each beat now lasts 0.4 s instead of 0.8 s, and most of the lost time comes out of diastole. So there is less time to fill the ventricle and less time for blood to flow into the left ventricle's own muscle. Stroke volume may fall, but not always: during exercise, more venous return, faster relaxation and stronger contraction can protect it. Final questions (answer and explanation shown straight after each response) Q1. A patient's ventricle becomes stiff and relaxes poorly. Atrial contraction is normal. What happens to ventricular filling? A. Passive filling rises, so the atrial kick becomes unnecessary for filling B. Filling stops completely, because the mitral valve can no longer open C. Passive filling falls, so the atrial kick supplies a larger share of filling D. Filling is unchanged, because filling depends only on the SA node firing Show answer and explanation Answer: C. A stiff ventricle doesn't stretch easily. As blood enters, its pressure rises quickly and soon matches atrial pressure, so passive inflow stops early. The atria's final push then makes up a bigger part of the filling. This is why losing atrial contraction hurts these patients most. Q2. During diastasis, before the next P wave, what is happening electrically? A. SA nodal cells are slowly depolarising towards their next threshold B. A slow excitation wave is spreading through the ventricular muscle C. The AV node is firing repeatedly to sustain passive ventricular filling D. Purkinje fibres are repolarising the atria in preparation for systole Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Passive filling needs no electrical impulse; the pressure difference moves the blood. Meanwhile the SA node's pacemaker cells slowly drift towards threshold. When they fire, the P wave and atrial contraction follow. Q3. The left ventricle starts contracting slightly before the right, yet the pulmonary valve opens before the aortic valve. Why? A. The right ventricle contracts more strongly than the left ventricle does B. The impulse reaches the right ventricle first by a faster route C. The pulmonary valve has thinner cusps that open at any pressure D. The right ventricle only has to exceed a much lower arterial pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The right ventricle only needs to beat about 10 mmHg in the pulmonary artery; the left must beat about 80 mmHg in the aorta. The right ventricle reaches its target sooner, so its valve opens first. Q4. Ventricles X and Y eject the same stroke volume, but X starts with a larger end-diastolic volume. Which conclusion follows? A. Both have the same ejection fraction, because their stroke volumes match B. X has the lower ejection fraction, because SV is a smaller share of its EDV C. X has the lower end-systolic volume, because it started with more blood D. Both have the same end-systolic volume, because they ejected equal volumes Show answer and explanation Answer: B. EF = SV ÷ EDV. If SV is the same but EDV is bigger, the fraction is smaller. X also has the larger ESV, since ESV = EDV − SV. Q5. Late in ejection, left ventricular pressure falls slightly below aortic pressure, yet blood keeps leaving for a moment. Why? A. The left atrium contracts and pushes blood on through the ventricle B. The aortic cusps actively propel blood forward as they begin closing C. Blood already moving forward carries momentum that takes time to stop D. The mitral valve opens and adds atrial pressure behind the outflow Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Moving blood can't stop instantly. It slows down, then briefly flows backwards, and that backflow is what shuts the aortic valve. Q6. An arrhythmia raises a resting patient's heart rate from 75 to 150 beats/min. Which change is most likely? A. Systole and diastole both halve, so filling time is proportionally kept B. Systole shortens most, so ejection is incomplete but filling is protected C. Coronary perfusion time rises, because the heart relaxes more often D. Diastole shortens most, so filling and coronary perfusion time both fall Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Systole can only shorten a little, so diastole loses most of the time. At rest, without the boosts that exercise brings, less filling time can lower EDV and stroke volume. The left ventricle's muscle also gets less time to receive blood. Q7. Which pairing of an atrial pressure wave with its cause is correct? A. c wave: the closed AV valve bulging back towards the atrium B. v wave: the atrium contracting against a closed AV valve C. a wave: venous blood filling the atrium while the AV valve is shut D. y descent: the atrium relaxing while the AV valve stays closed Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The c wave comes from the contracting ventricle pushing the shut valve back. The a wave is the atrium contracting, the v wave is the atrium filling up, and the y descent happens when the AV valve opens and the atrium empties. Q8. End-diastolic volume is 150 mL and end-systolic volume is 60 mL. What are the stroke volume and ejection fraction? A. 90 mL and 40% B. 90 mL and 60% C. 60 mL and 60% D. 60 mL and 40% Show answer and explanation Answer: B. SV = 150 − 60 = 90 mL. EF = 90 ÷ 150 × 100 = 60%. 12. Chapter Essentials The atria and ventricles take turns. The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. While the ventricles contract, the atria relax and refill. For about 0.4 s of a 0.8 s beat, both are relaxed together. One chain drives every phase: electrical signal → contraction or relaxation → pressure change → valves open or close → blood moves and volume changes. Valves are passive. Each opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front, and shuts when that reverses. Papillary muscles stop the AV valves turning inside out; they do not open them. Filling is mostly passive: rapid filling, then slower diastasis, then the atrial kick (lecture: 70% passive, 30% atrial; at rest often 10–30% atrial). The kick matters more with a stiff ventricle or a fast heart rate. Isovolumetric contraction: all four valves are shut, so the contracting ventricle's pressure shoots up while its volume stays at EDV. Closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves contributes to S1. Ejection starts only when ventricular pressure beats arterial pressure: about 80 mmHg in the aorta, 10 mmHg in the pulmonary artery. It is rapid first, then reduced, as the muscle weakens and the aorta fills. Isovolumetric relaxation: forward flow briefly reverses and shuts the aortic and pulmonary valves (S2, dicrotic notch). With all valves shut, pressure falls while volume stays at ESV. It ends when the mitral and tricuspid valves open. Volumes: SV = EDV − ESV and EF = SV ÷ EDV × 100%. Stroke volume rises by filling more (bigger EDV) or emptying more (smaller ESV). Atrial pressure waves: a = atria contract; c = shut AV valves bulge back; x = atria relax and the valve ring is pulled down; v = atria fill behind shut valves; y = AV valves open and the atria empty. Electrical comes before mechanical: the P wave before atrial contraction, the QRS before ventricular contraction, and the T wave during reduced ejection, before the outflow valves shut. On a Wiggers diagram, every valve event is a point where two pressure curves cross. Right heart and heart rate: the right heart follows the same rules at lower pressures, so the right ventricle ejects first; on inspiration P2 is delayed and S2 splitting widens. At faster heart rates diastole loses most of the time, so very high rates cut filling and coronary perfusion time.","heart-sounds":"Heart Sounds and Murmurs 1. Why does the heart produce sounds? With each beat, a healthy heart makes two main sounds, heard through a stethoscope as \"lub-dub\". Each one is made by a pair of valves shutting. Sound Valves that shut When it happens S1, \"lub\" Mitral and tricuspid, the inlet valves between the atria and the ventricles As the ventricles start to contract S2, \"dub\" Aortic and pulmonary, the outlet valves into the aorta and the pulmonary artery As the ventricles stop ejecting and start to relax Why does a shutting valve make a sound? The lecture puts it simply: the sounds are made by the valves closing. But what you hear is not the leaflets clapping together. When a valve snaps shut, the blood moving against it stops suddenly. That sudden stop makes the blood, the tightened valve leaflets and the nearby heart and artery walls vibrate. It's the same reason a water pipe bangs when a tap is turned off quickly. The vibrations travel through the chest wall to the stethoscope. Why is there no sound when a valve opens? A normal valve opens gently as pressure builds behind it, and blood simply starts to flow smoothly. Nothing is stopped suddenly, so there is nothing to hear. What the sounds tell you about timing. S1 marks the start of ventricular contraction, and S2 marks its end. So: S1 → S2 is ventricular systole: the ventricles contract and eject. S2 → next S1 is ventricular diastole: the ventricles relax and fill. At a resting heart rate, diastole is the longer phase. So the silence after \"dub\" is longer than the gap between \"lub\" and \"dub\". That is how you tell S1 from S2 when listening. At fast heart rates the two gaps become similar, so clinicians feel the carotid pulse while listening: the pulse comes just after S1. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §1) Watch and listen to one heartbeat A marker moves across one heartbeat. At S1 the mitral and tricuspid valves light up as they shut; at S2 the aortic and pulmonary valves light up. The span from S1 to S2 is ventricular systole; the longer span after S2 is ventricular diastole. Play gives a lub-dub in time with the marker, and a heart-rate control (60–150 beats/min) shows diastole shrinking much more than systole as the rate rises. Schematic sounds and timings, not a recording. Hear a real heart. Listen for lub, dub, then the longer pause. REAL HEART · AMPLIFIED RECORDING · ABOUT 19 SECONDS Normal S1–S2 sequence A real chest, recorded through a stethoscope. Headphones work best. Listen Open on Wikimedia Commons Original recording: James Heilman, MD ( CC0); amplified version: Natural Philo ( CC BY-SA 3.0). 2. Why does S1 occur before S2? Both sounds follow the valve rule from the cardiac cycle. A valve opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front of it. It shuts the moment that difference reverses. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §2) Follow one beat. The left and right sides do the same thing at the same time; the right side just works at lower pressures. S1: the inlet valves shut as contraction begins. The electrical signal spreads through the ventricles (the QRS complex on the ECG), and the ventricles start to contract. Within moments, pressure in each ventricle rises above the pressure in its atrium. Blood starts to push back towards the atria, and that push snaps the mitral and tricuspid valves shut: S1. The outlet valves are still shut as well, so for a moment the ventricles squeeze with every valve closed. This is isovolumetric contraction. S1 therefore marks the start of ventricular systole. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §4) S2: the outlet valves shut as ejection ends. The ventricles keep ejecting until their force fades. Their pressure then falls below the pressure in the aorta and pulmonary artery. Blood briefly starts to flow back towards the ventricles, and this snaps the aortic and pulmonary valves shut: S2. The ventricles now relax with every valve closed again. This is isovolumetric relaxation. As the lecture puts it, S2 marks the end of ventricular systole, which is the beginning of diastole. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §6) In one line: S1 is when the ventricle overtakes the atrium; S2 is when the artery overtakes the ventricle. How long are they, and why do they sound different? The lecture gives about 0.15 s for S1 and 0.12 s for S2. S1 is the longer, lower \"lub\". S2 is the shorter, sharper \"dub\": the aortic and pulmonary valves, and the stretched artery walls behind them, are tighter, so they vibrate at a higher pitch. Do both valves in a pair shut at exactly the same moment? Almost, but not quite. The lecture says they close at about the same time. For S1 the gap is too small to hear, so S1 sounds single. For S2 the gap can widen enough to hear as two parts. That is S2 splitting, the subject of the next section. Related: where S1 and S2 sit on the full timeline → Cardiac Cycle, §9 3. Why does S2 sometimes split with inspiration? Why S2 has two parts. S2 is made of A2 (aortic closure) and P2 (pulmonary closure). A2 normally comes first. During expiration they are close together and often sound single. During inspiration, P2 moves later and the gap widens: physiological splitting of S2. Two effects delay P2: More right-heart filling. Inspiration makes chest pressure more negative, drawing more venous blood into the right heart. The right ventricle fills more and takes longer to eject. Lower pulmonary impedance. The expanding lungs lower the impedance (opposition to flow) of the pulmonary vessels. Blood keeps flowing forward into the pulmonary artery for longer after right ventricular pressure peaks, so the pulmonary valve closes later. A2 may also move slightly earlier. The expanded lung vessels hold more blood for a few beats, so left ventricular filling dips briefly. These two effects are what the lecture means by \"venous return\" and \"reduces pulmonary resistance… thus longer time for blood flow\". Why \"physiological\"? Because it is normal. It happens in healthy people with every breath in, and it fades with every breath out. What matters is the change with breathing: the gap widens on inspiration and narrows on expiration. A split that stays wide during expiration as well is not normal. That is clinical material beyond this lecture. Where and how to hear it. P2 is a soft sound, so listen at the pulmonary area: the second left intercostal space, beside the sternum (see §8). Ask the person to breathe slowly. On a breath in, the single \"dub\" becomes a quick \"da-dub\". On a breath out, it merges back into one. Why S2 splits on inspiration Two timelines. In expiration, A2 (aortic valve closing) and P2 (pulmonary valve closing) are almost together. In inspiration, A2 comes slightly earlier and P2 clearly later, so the gap widens. Schematic, not to scale. Hear it. A simulator recording at the pulmonary area. The simulator breathes quickly, so S2 changes from one sound to two and back every few beats. Listen for the \"dub\" becoming a short \"da-dub\". (Recording: University of Michigan Heart Sound and Murmur Library, \"split S2, transient\", CC BY-SA 4.0. Headphones recommended.) Recording: split S2 that comes and goes with breathing, at the pulmonary area. Source note: The lecture says the aortic and pulmonary valves close \"at the same time\" in expiration. In practice a small gap remains but is usually too short to hear; the teaching point is the widening on inspiration. Mechanism 2 corresponds to the lecture's \"decrease in the impedance of the pulmonary vasculature\". The lecture also lists \"contracting lungs exert a force on the vessels\"; this is not a standard explanation of splitting, so we have not taught it. Related: the full timing picture → Cardiac Cycle, §10 · breathing and venous return → Venous Return, §5 Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Listening at the second left intercostal space, you hear S2 as two sounds when the person breathes in and as one sound when they breathe out. What does this most likely show? A. Delayed closure of the mitral valve because more blood fills the left heart B. Early closure of the pulmonary valve because less blood returns to the right heart C. Normal widening of the A2–P2 gap as the right ventricle ejects for longer D. A stiff right ventricle that delays the start of right ventricular ejection Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Inspiration fills the right ventricle more and lowers the opposition to flow in the lungs. Both effects let the right ventricle keep ejecting for longer, so P2 comes later. The mitral valve has nothing to do with S2, and the gap closing again on expiration shows this is normal, not disease. 2. At the instant S2 is heard over the aortic area, which pressure change has just happened? A. Left ventricular pressure has just risen above left atrial pressure B. Left ventricular pressure has just fallen below left atrial pressure C. Left ventricular pressure has just risen above aortic pressure D. Left ventricular pressure has just fallen below aortic pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The aortic valve shuts when the artery overtakes the ventricle. A is the moment of S1 (the mitral valve shuts). C is the aortic valve opening, which makes no sound. B is the mitral valve opening at the end of isovolumetric relaxation, which is also silent. 3. During inspiration, why does blood keep flowing into the pulmonary artery after right ventricular pressure has peaked? A. The tricuspid valve stays open later during inspiration B. The expanded lung vessels offer less opposition to flow C. The pulmonary valve has fewer cusps than the aortic valve D. Right ventricular contraction starts later on inspiration Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Expanded lungs lower the impedance of the pulmonary vessels, so forward flow continues for longer and the pulmonary valve shuts later. The tricuspid valve must be shut while the right ventricle ejects. Both outflow valves have three cusps. Inspiration does not delay the electrical activation of the right ventricle. 4. What causes S3 and S4? S1 and S2 are valves shutting. S3 and S4 are different: no valve shuts. They are filling sounds, made in diastole as blood rushes into the ventricle and is then suddenly slowed. The lecture says the same: both are due to ventricular filling. What separates them is what drives the rush, and that puts them at opposite ends of diastole. Recall how a ventricle fills ( → Cardiac Cycle, §3): Rapid filling. The mitral and tricuspid valves open, and blood stored in the atria pours into the ventricles. Diastasis. Filling slows to a trickle. Atrial kick. The atria contract and push in the last portion of blood. S3: at the end of rapid filling, early in diastole. About 0.1–0.2 s after S2, the rush of early filling reaches the limit of how far the ventricle wall will easily stretch. The inflowing blood is suddenly slowed, and the ventricle wall and blood vibrate. The result is a low, dull sound just after S2: \"lub-dub-dum\". Many students remember the rhythm as Ken-TUC-ky. When it is normal: in children, young adults, athletes and pregnant women. Their ventricles relax quickly and fill very fast. When it suggests a problem: with age, early filling normally slows and S3 disappears. In an older adult, an S3 usually means the ventricle is being overfilled or is enlarged and failing. ( → Heart Failure) S4: during the atrial kick, late in diastole. Just before S1, the atria contract and push their blood into the ventricles. If the ventricle is stiff, for example thickened, it resists this final push. The blood is stopped abruptly and the wall vibrates. The result is a low sound just before S1: \"da-lub-dub\", or TEN-nes-see. It depends entirely on atrial contraction. It comes just after the P wave and goes with the a wave in the atria. So it is absent in atrial fibrillation, where the atria quiver instead of contracting together. When it suggests a problem: an S4 you can actually hear with a stethoscope in an adult usually means a stiff ventricle. S3 S4 When Early diastole, just after S2 Late diastole, just before S1 What drives the rush Passive rapid filling Atrial contraction (the atrial kick) Rhythm lub-dub- dum ( Ken-TUC-ky) da -lub-dub ( TEN-nes-see) Heard in healthy people? Often, in the young Rarely heard with a stethoscope In an older adult it usually means An overfilled or failing ventricle A stiff ventricle Needs atrial contraction? No Yes, so it is lost in atrial fibrillation How to listen. Both are very low-pitched. Use the bell of the stethoscope, pressed lightly, at the apex, with the person turned onto their left side. This brings the apex closer to the chest wall. Where S3 and S4 sit in filling Ventricular volume through one diastole: a steep rise (rapid filling), a flat stretch (diastasis), then a small final rise (atrial kick). S3 is marked where the steep rise suddenly levels off; S4 is marked on the atrial kick; S2 and the next S1 are marked at either end. Hear S3 and Hear S4 play the matching recording while the marker lights up. Recordings: University of Michigan Heart Sound and Murmur Library, CC BY-SA 4.0. Headphones recommended. Source note: The lecture lists both S3 and S4 as normal sounds due to rapid ventricular filling. We agree that both are filling sounds. Standard physiology texts (e.g. Guyton and Hall) separate them by cause: S3 comes from rapid passive filling, S4 from atrial contraction. They also note that S3 is often normal in young people, while an audible S4 in an adult usually points to a stiff ventricle. We teach that distinction because it explains why S4 disappears in atrial fibrillation. Related: S3 and S4 on the Wiggers diagram → Cardiac Cycle, §9 5. Why do heart sounds change in loudness? Each sound is made by a valve snapping shut. So anything that makes the snap harder makes the sound louder. For S1 and S2, two different things control how hard the snap is. S1 depends on how wide open the mitral and tricuspid valves are when contraction starts. The lecture links S1's loudness to how the ventricle fills. Think of a door: one that is already nearly shut closes quietly, but one flung shut from wide open bangs. Slow heart rate → soft S1. Diastole is long, so the ventricles finish filling well before they contract. As the inflow slows, the cusps of the mitral and tricuspid valves drift up towards each other. The lecture calls these \"floating valves\". When the ventricles finally contract, the cusps only have a short way to go, so they close gently. Fast heart rate → loud S1. Diastole is short, so the ventricles contract while blood is still rushing in and the cusps are still wide apart. Rising ventricular pressure slams them shut from fully open. A stronger contraction also gives a louder S1, for example during exercise. Ventricular pressure rises faster, so the cusps are driven shut harder. Why a slow heart has a soft S1 Two panels of the mitral valve at the instant the ventricle starts to contract. Slow heart: the cusps are already close together and travel a short distance (soft S1). Fast heart: the cusps are still wide open and travel a long distance (loud S1). Play animates both closing at the same moment, with a quiet and a loud lub. Schematic. S2 depends on the pressure in the artery behind the valve. The aortic and pulmonary valves are pushed shut by the pressure in the aorta and pulmonary artery. The higher that pressure, the harder and quicker the valve is driven shut. This is the lecture's \"brisk closure\". High blood pressure in the aorta (systemic hypertension) → loud A2. High pressure in the pulmonary artery ( pulmonary hypertension) → loud P2. It is heard best at the pulmonary area, the second left intercostal space. What a stethoscope picks up also depends on the route to the chest wall. Sounds are quieter through a thick chest wall, fluid around the heart, or over-inflated lungs. A badly damaged, stiff valve may also barely move, so its closing sound becomes softer even though the disease is severe. So loudness alone never tells you how serious a problem is. It is one clue to put alongside timing and where the sound is heard. Related: heart rate and the length of diastole → Cardiac Cycle, §10 · contractility → Cardiac Output, §7 Understanding Check: Sections 4–5 1. A patient's S4 disappears when they develop atrial fibrillation. What is the best explanation? A. The ventricle becomes less stiff once the heart rate rises B. The mitral valve no longer opens fully during diastole C. Rapid filling ends before the atria have time to empty D. The atria no longer contract together to push the last blood in Show answer and explanation Answer: D. S4 is made when the atrial kick pushes blood into a stiff ventricle. In atrial fibrillation the atria quiver instead of contracting together, so there is no kick and no S4. The ventricle is just as stiff as before. 2. An S3 is heard in a healthy 19-year-old athlete. Which explanation fits best? A. Atrial contraction is pushing blood into a stiff left ventricle B. Fast early filling of a ventricle that relaxes quickly is suddenly slowed C. The aortic and pulmonary valves are closing at clearly different times D. The mitral valve is closing late because the heart rate is very slow Show answer and explanation Answer: B. S3 comes at the end of rapid filling. Young, quickly relaxing ventricles fill very fast, so the sudden slowing can be heard, and that is normal. A describes S4. C describes split S2. D mixes up S3 with S1. 3. In a person with a slow resting heart rate, S1 is soft. What is the best explanation? A. The ventricle contracts less forcefully because it is overfilled B. The aortic valve shuts gently because aortic pressure is low C. The mitral and tricuspid cusps have nearly floated shut already D. The atria contract weakly, so less blood crosses the valves Show answer and explanation Answer: C. A long diastole lets filling finish, and the cusps drift close together before contraction starts. They only have a short way to travel, so they close softly. A fuller ventricle actually contracts more strongly. The aortic valve makes S2, not S1. 4. A loud P2 at the second left intercostal space most likely points to: A. Raised pressure in the pulmonary artery B. Raised pressure in the right atrium C. Faster flow through the tricuspid valve D. Lower resistance in the lung vessels Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The higher the pressure behind the pulmonary valve, the harder it is driven shut, so P2 gets louder. D is what happens on inspiration: it delays P2 rather than making it louder. 6. What makes a murmur? Normal blood flow is silent. Blood normally moves in smooth layers, sliding past one another. This is laminar flow, the lecture's \"streamlined\" flow. Nothing tumbles or collides, so there is nothing to hear. A murmur is the sound of blood flow that has become turbulent: swirling and tumbling instead of sliding in layers. The swirls shake the heart and vessel walls for as long as the turbulence lasts. So a murmur is a longer whooshing sound, not a short thud like S1 or S2. What makes flow turbulent? Flow tips into turbulence when it is fast, when the blood is thin (low viscosity), or when it is forced through a sudden narrowing. The lecture sums this up with the Reynolds number: the higher it is, the more likely turbulence becomes. ( → Haemodynamics, §5) The heart can make flow turbulent in three ways. These follow the lecture's list. A narrowed valve ( stenosis). When the valve should be open, it opens only partly. Blood is squeezed through the small opening as a fast jet, which becomes turbulent beyond the valve. This murmur is made by forward flow. A leaking valve ( regurgitation, the lecture's \"incompetence\"). When the valve should be shut, it does not close fully. Blood is driven backwards through the gap as a fast jet. This is the lecture's \"backflow\". Fast flow through normal valves (a \"hyperdynamic\" circulation). The valves are healthy, but blood moves fast enough, or is thin enough, to become turbulent anyway. The lecture's example is anaemia, where thin blood and a raised cardiac output combine. Exercise, fever and pregnancy can do the same. These flow murmurs do not mean the valves are damaged. Three ways to make a murmur Four panels. Normal: a fully open valve with smooth, parallel arrows (laminar: silent). Stenosis: a narrowed opening with a fast jet that breaks into swirls beyond it (forward jet). Regurgitation: a valve that should be shut, with a thin jet leaking backwards into swirls (backward jet). High flow: a normal open valve with many long, fast arrows becoming swirls (normal valve, fast flow). In every murmur there is turbulence somewhere; the questions are where it is and which way the blood is going. The key question for any murmur: which way is the abnormal blood flowing, and is that happening in systole or diastole? That decides when you hear it, which is the subject of §7. Murmur or bruit? Both come from the same mechanism. A murmur is heard over the heart; a bruit is heard over a blood vessel ( §9). Related: anaemia and viscosity → Haemodynamics, §7 7. Predict a murmur's timing without memorising a list The lecture's tip is to group the valves that close together. The aortic and pulmonary valves behave as one pair, and the mitral and tricuspid valves as the other. Then you only need to know what each pair should be doing in each phase: During Mitral and tricuspid (inlet valves) Aortic and pulmonary (outlet valves) Ventricular systole (S1 → S2) Should be shut Should be open, for ejection Ventricular diastole (S2 → S1) Should be open, for filling Should be shut For any faulty valve, ask one question: when is the faulty valve supposed to be open (for stenosis) or shut (for a leak)? That phase is when the abnormal flow happens, so that is when you hear the murmur. Systolic murmurs (between S1 and S2) Aortic or pulmonary stenosis. These valves should be open while the ventricles eject. A narrowed opening squeezes forward flow into a fast jet, so the murmur is systolic. Mitral or tricuspid regurgitation. These valves should be shut while the ventricles contract. If they leak, the contracting ventricle drives blood back into the atrium. The leak starts as soon as contraction starts and lasts until relaxation, so the murmur can fill all of systole. Diastolic murmurs (between S2 and the next S1) Mitral or tricuspid stenosis. These valves should be open while the ventricles fill. A narrowed opening obstructs filling, so the murmur is diastolic. Aortic or pulmonary regurgitation. These valves should be shut while the ventricles relax. The high pressure in the artery drives blood back into the ventricle, starting right after S2, so the murmur is diastolic. At a glance Valve pair Stenosis (forward jet) Regurgitation (backward jet) Aortic / pulmonary Systolic Diastolic Mitral / tricuspid Diastolic Systolic The pattern is a cross: each lesion falls in the opposite phase to its partner. You never need to memorise it. If you forget, ask the question again. Murmur predictor Pick a valve (aortic, pulmonary, mitral, tricuspid) and a fault (narrowed or leaking). The tool then shows, one step at a time, when that valve should be open or shut, which way the abnormal jet flows, the phase on an S1–S2–S1 timeline, and the answer: systolic or diastolic. Hear an example plays a recording typical of that lesion where one exists. Recordings: University of Michigan Heart Sound and Murmur Library, CC BY-SA 4.0. OPTIONAL VIDEO · ABOUT 4 MINUTES Watch it all together Alila Medical Media, Heart Sounds and Heart Murmurs. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video. Understanding Check: Sections 6–7 1. A valve leaks blood from the aorta back into the left ventricle. When is the murmur heard? A. During systole, between S1 and S2, as the ventricle ejects B. During diastole, starting just after S2, as the ventricle relaxes C. Late in diastole only, when the atria push blood into the ventricle D. Throughout the cycle, because aortic pressure is always higher Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The aortic valve should be shut while the ventricle relaxes. Aortic pressure is far above ventricular pressure then, so blood leaks back from the moment the valve closes (S2). In systole the valve is open anyway, so there is no backflow to hear. 2. Why does mitral stenosis cause a diastolic murmur, while mitral regurgitation causes a systolic one? A. Stenosis raises aortic pressure in diastole; a leak lowers left atrial pressure in systole B. Stenosis is heard at the apex only in diastole; a leak is heard at the base only in systole C. Stenosis blocks filling when the valve should be open; a leak lets blood back when it should be shut D. Stenosis delays mitral closure into diastole; a leak moves aortic closure earlier into systole Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The mitral valve should be open during filling (diastole), so a narrowed one causes a diastolic murmur. It should be shut during contraction (systole), so a leaking one causes a systolic murmur. 3. A patient with severe anaemia has a soft systolic murmur, but all four valves are normal on a scan. What is the most likely cause? A. The mitral valve leaks because the left ventricle is overfilled B. The aortic valve narrows because red cells clump on its cusps C. Low blood viscosity delays closure of the pulmonary valve D. Thin blood and a high cardiac output make ejection flow turbulent Show answer and explanation Answer: D. This is a flow murmur: normal valves, but blood that is thin and fast enough to become turbulent during ejection. It does not mean the valves are damaged. 8. Where do we listen to each heart valve? The lecture says each valve is best heard over its surface marking: the spot on the chest wall where that valve's sound comes through most clearly. These four spots are the auscultation areas: Area Where on the chest Aortic Second right intercostal space, beside the sternum Pulmonary Second left intercostal space, beside the sternum Tricuspid Lower left border of the sternum (fourth or fifth intercostal space) Mitral The apex: fifth left intercostal space, in line with the middle of the clavicle Figure 1. Where to place the stethoscope for each valve. Image credit Rib cage drawing from Gray's Anatomy of the Human Body (1918), public domain; listening-point labels added. Supplied by the DOMiNANCE Breakdowns project. Why aren't these spots directly over the valves? The four valves sit close together behind the sternum, packed into a small area. Each valve's sound travels in the direction its blood flows, and comes out where that chamber or vessel lies closest to the chest wall: The aortic valve sits behind the left side of the sternum. Its sound is carried up the aorta, which comes nearest the surface at the right second intercostal space. The mitral valve also sits behind the sternum. Its sound is carried down through the left ventricle to the apex, where the ventricle touches the chest wall. A practical tip for telling S1 from S2: S1 is loudest at the apex, where the mitral sound comes out. S2 is loudest at the base, the aortic and pulmonary areas at the top of the heart. Moving the stethoscope between the two and hearing which sound gets louder tells you which is which. Two recordings of the same normal heart sounds: at the apex, where S1 is louder, and at the base, where S2 is louder. Bell or diaphragm? The diaphragm, pressed firmly, picks up higher-pitched sounds: S1, S2 and most murmurs. The bell, resting lightly, picks up low-pitched sounds such as S3, S4 and the rumble of mitral stenosis. Source note: The lecture slide gives \"AT PM\" beside the listening areas. We could not confirm what this abbreviation means, so we present the four standard areas without it. Valve surface positions follow standard anatomy texts. 9. Why can bruits or murmurs arise elsewhere? Turbulence is not limited to the heart valves. Wherever blood is forced through a narrowing, takes a short cut, or flows unusually fast, it can become turbulent and make a sound ( §6). Heard over a vessel, this sound is called a bruit. The lecture lists these examples: Condition Why the flow becomes turbulent When it is heard Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA): the fetal channel between the aorta and pulmonary artery stays open after birth Aortic pressure is higher than pulmonary pressure in both systole and diastole, so blood crosses all the time Continuous, through S2 Ventricular septal defect (VSD): a hole between the ventricles When the ventricles contract, left ventricular pressure far exceeds right, so blood jets through the hole Systolic Arteriovenous (AV) fistula: a direct channel from an artery to a vein High-pressure arterial blood rushes into a low-pressure vein, skipping the capillaries Continuous bruit over the site Aneurysm: a ballooned section of artery The sudden widening disturbs smooth flow (not every aneurysm makes a sound) A bruit over the aneurysm Goitre (enlarged thyroid) with high blood flow Increased blood flow through the gland A bruit over the thyroid Anaemia Thin, fast blood (see §6) A soft systolic flow murmur The rule is the same everywhere: find the pressure difference that drives the abnormal flow, and when it exists. That tells you when the sound is heard. The PDA murmur is continuous for exactly this reason: the pressure difference between the aorta and pulmonary artery never reverses. Related: the ductus arteriosus and why it should close after birth → Fetal Circulation, §4 · Korotkoff sounds in blood-pressure measurement → Blood Pressure, §7 10. Understanding Check Questions on the whole chapter. The earlier checks cover Sections 1–7 in detail. 1. A patient has a patent ductus arteriosus, an open channel joining the aorta to the pulmonary artery. Why is its murmur continuous through systole and diastole? A. The ductus closes in systole and reopens in diastole each beat B. Aortic pressure stays above pulmonary pressure through the whole cycle C. Pulmonary pressure stays above aortic pressure through the whole cycle D. Both the aortic and pulmonary valves leak during every diastole Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Flow crosses the ductus whenever aortic pressure is higher than pulmonary pressure, and in this patient that is true throughout the cycle. Because the driving difference never reverses, the turbulence and the sound never stop. 2. Why is the mitral valve best heard at the apex rather than directly over the valve? A. The mitral valve actually lies beneath the fifth left intercostal space B. The sternum blocks all sound, so no valve can be heard over it C. Its sound is carried to where the left ventricle meets the chest wall D. The apex is the only area where the bell can be applied lightly Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The mitral valve sits behind the sternum, but its sound travels with the blood into the left ventricle and comes out at the apex, where the ventricle touches the chest wall. 3. A 70-year-old has a stiff, thickened left ventricle. Which extra sound would you most expect, and why? A. An S4 just before S1, from the atrial kick B. An S3 just after S2, from rapid early filling C. A loud S1 at the apex, from floating cusps D. A split S2 on expiration, from late P2 Show answer and explanation Answer: A. A stiff ventricle resists the final push of blood from the atria, which makes S4. An S3 points to an overfilled or failing ventricle. Floating cusps make S1 soft, not loud. A split that persists on expiration is not explained by stiffness. 4. Listening at the apex, you hear \"lub-dub\" with a long pause after \"dub\". Then you move to the second right intercostal space, and the second sound becomes the louder one. What have you confirmed? A. The second sound is S1, because S1 is loudest over the aortic area B. The first sound is S1, because S1 is loudest at the apex, S2 at the base C. The patient has aortic regurgitation, because S2 is loud at the base D. The second sound is split, because it changes when you move position Show answer and explanation Answer: B. S1 is loudest at the apex and S2 at the base. Together with the long pause after S2 (diastole), this confirms which sound is which. A louder S2 at the base is normal, not a sign of disease. 11. Chapter Essentials S1 (\"lub\"): the mitral and tricuspid valves shutting as the ventricles start to contract. It marks the start of ventricular systole. S2 (\"dub\"): the aortic and pulmonary valves shutting as ejection ends. It marks the start of ventricular diastole. The sound is not the leaflets clapping: it is the vibration caused when moving blood is suddenly stopped. Normal valves open silently. S1 → S2 is systole; S2 → S1 is diastole. At rest the pause after S2 is longer. S1 is loudest at the apex, S2 at the base. Physiological splitting of S2: on inspiration, more right-heart filling and lower pulmonary impedance delay P2, so A2 and P2 separate. They merge again on expiration. S3: early diastole, at the end of rapid filling. Often normal in the young; in older adults it suggests an overfilled or failing ventricle. S4: late diastole, from the atrial kick into a stiff ventricle. It needs atrial contraction, so it is absent in atrial fibrillation. Loudness: a slow heart gives a soft S1 (the cusps have floated nearly shut). High aortic or pulmonary pressure gives a loud A2 or P2. Loudness alone does not measure severity. Murmurs are turbulent flow: from a narrowed valve (forward jet), a leaking valve (backward jet), or fast, thin blood through normal valves (flow murmur, e.g. anaemia). Timing rule: ask when the faulty valve should be open (stenosis) or shut (leak). Aortic/pulmonary stenosis and mitral/tricuspid regurgitation are systolic. Mitral/tricuspid stenosis and aortic/pulmonary regurgitation are diastolic. Auscultation areas: aortic at the second right intercostal space; pulmonary at the second left; tricuspid at the lower left sternal border; mitral at the apex. These are where each valve's sound comes out, not where the valves sit. Bruits follow the same rule as murmurs: find the pressure difference driving the abnormal flow, and when it exists. A PDA is continuous because aortic pressure stays above pulmonary pressure all the time.","ecg":"Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms 1. What does an ECG actually record? Each heartbeat starts with an electrical signal that spreads through the heart muscle. As the muscle cells depolarise (switch on) and then repolarise (reset), they create small voltage differences. These spread through the body's tissues, all the way to the skin. Electrodes on the skin pick them up, and the machine draws them over time. That drawing is the electrocardiogram (ECG, or EKG). Electrical activation → Contraction → Pressure changes → Blood movement An ECG records electricity, not pumping. It does not measure how strongly the heart contracts, the pressure it makes or how much blood it ejects. A QRS complex means the ventricles have been switched on electrically, not that they have pumped blood. Usually contraction follows. But the electrical signal can carry on even when the heart muscle pumps little or no blood. So a normal-looking ECG does not prove the heart is pumping well. OPTIONAL VIDEO · BIOVFX ECG SERIES, PART 1 OF 3 (ECG or EKG) Rhythm Strips part 1 BioVFX. An introduction to ECG recordings, ECG paper and rhythm strips, and how to measure time using the boxes on the paper. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © BioVFX, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation on this page does not depend on this video. 2. Why can the tracing move upwards or downwards? First, two words that are easy to mix up: An electrode is the sticker on the skin. A lead is one view of the heart's electrical activity, worked out from the voltage between electrodes. Think of each lead as a camera pointed at the heart from one direction. Every lead watches the same heartbeat, but from a different angle, so each draws a slightly different shape. At any moment, the electrical wave spreading through the heart has an overall direction and size. We draw it as an arrow, the electrical vector. What a lead records depends on which way that arrow points compared with the lead: The wave of depolarisation travels… The lead draws… Towards the lead's positive end An upward deflection Away from it A downward deflection Across it, at right angles A small or up-and-down (biphasic) deflection Example: in a normal heart, the ventricles depolarise mostly downwards and to the patient's left. Lead II looks at the heart from that direction, so its QRS is usually upright. aVR looks from the right shoulder, so the same wave travels away from it, and its QRS points down. The up and down are electrical, not movement. An upward wave does not mean the heart moved up. It means the electrical wave was heading towards that lead. Lead view A heart outline with one lead's positive end marked. Drag or tap to turn the depolarisation arrow. A small trace beside it updates live: upward when the arrow points towards the lead, downward when it points away, and a small up-and-down deflection when it points across. Buttons switch between Lead I, Lead II and aVR. Default: Lead II with the normal ventricular direction (upright QRS). Why is the normal T wave upright, if it is \"the opposite\" of depolarisation? The rule in the table is for depolarisation. Repolarisation (the cells resetting) gives the opposite sign: a resetting wave travelling towards a lead draws a downward deflection. But in the ventricles, the last cells to switch on (the outer layer) are the first to reset. So repolarisation travels in the reverse direction, from outside to inside. Opposite sign plus opposite direction cancel out, so the T wave usually points the same way as the QRS. Source note: The lecture does not explain T-wave direction. The outer-layer-first order of ventricular repolarisation is standard textbook physiology (Guyton and Hall). Understanding Check: Sections 1–2 1. What is the difference between an ECG electrode and an ECG lead? A. An electrode is a skin contact; a lead is a view worked out from electrodes B. An electrode records voltage; a lead records the strength of contraction C. An electrode is placed on the chest; a lead is always placed on a limb D. An electrode is one view of the heart; a lead is the wire to the machine Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Electrodes are the physical contacts. Leads are views calculated from the voltages between them, so ten electrodes can give twelve leads. 2. In one lead, the wave of ventricular depolarisation travels away from the positive end. What does that lead draw? A. An upward QRS, because the ventricles are contracting B. A downward QRS, because the wave is moving away C. A flat line, because no wave is heading towards it D. A taller T wave, because repolarisation comes first Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Depolarisation moving towards a lead draws an upward deflection, and moving away draws a downward one. The deflection shows direction, not contraction. 3. A patient's ECG shows regular, normal-looking QRS complexes, but no pulse can be felt. How is this possible? A. The QRS complexes must be interference from the electrodes B. The ECG measures blood flow, so a pulse must be present C. The ECG records electrical activity, which can occur without effective pumping D. The pulse is always delayed by several seconds after each QRS Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The ECG shows the electrical signal only. The muscle can fail to pump effectively even while that signal continues, so you must also check the pulse and blood pressure. 3. Why can ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads? A standard 12-lead ECG uses 10 electrodes: one on each limb and six on the chest. The machine combines their voltages to give twelve views: Lead group How many Names Viewing plane Standard limb leads (bipolar) 3 I, II, III Frontal (front-on) Augmented limb leads 3 aVR, aVL, aVF Frontal Chest leads 6 V1–V6 Horizontal (a slice across the chest) The right-leg electrode is not used to make a view. It works as an earth that reduces electrical interference. That is why there are ten electrodes but only nine are used to make the twelve views. The standard limb leads: I, II and III Each one measures the voltage between two limb electrodes: Lead Negative end Positive end I Right arm Left arm II Right arm Left leg III Left arm Left leg Together they form a triangle around the heart, Einthoven's triangle. Because the three leads share the same three electrodes, their voltages at any instant are linked by Einthoven's law: Einthoven's law V II = V I + V III So if Lead I reads +0.5 mV and Lead III reads +0.3 mV at the same instant, Lead II must read +0.8 mV. The augmented limb leads: aVR, aVL and aVF Each uses one limb electrode as its positive end, compared with an average of the other two limb electrodes: aVR looks from the right arm. aVL looks from the left arm. aVF looks from the left foot. The lecture calls these leads unipolar, because only one electrode forms the positive end. Normal ventricular depolarisation travels away from the right shoulder, so the QRS in aVR is usually negative. The chest leads: V1–V6 Each chest electrode is a positive end, compared with an average of the limb electrodes. Together they wrap around the heart from right to left. Electrode Position V1 4th intercostal space, right edge of the sternum V2 4th intercostal space, left edge of the sternum V3 Midway between V2 and V4 V4 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line V5 Anterior axillary line, at the level of V4 V6 Midaxillary line, at the level of V4 Place V4 before V3, because V3 sits midway between V2 and V4. A misplaced electrode changes the shapes and can make a normal ECG look abnormal. Where the limb and chest leads look from RA LA LL I II III aVR aVL aVF Limb leads (front view) 4th ICS 5th ICS MCL AAL MAL V1 V2 V3 V4 V5 V6 Chest leads (front view) Patient’s right is on your left. Arrowheads mark each lead’s positive end. ICS = intercostal space; MCL = midclavicular line; AAL = anterior axillary line; MAL = midaxillary line. Source note: The lecture names the three lead groups (bipolar, augmented unipolar, chest unipolar). Electrode positions and the right-leg electrode's role are standard clinical teaching. 4. Waves, segments and intervals: what is the difference? The ECG trace is made of three kinds of parts. The words are not interchangeable: Wave: a bump up or down from the baseline (P, QRS, T). Segment: the flat stretch between two waves (PR segment, ST segment). Interval: a span that includes at least one wave (PR interval, QT interval). One normal beat: waves, segments and intervals Wave Segment Interval P QRS T PR segment ST segment PR interval 0.12–0.20 s QRS 0.06–0.10 s QT interval 0.2–0.4 s, rate-dependent OPTIONAL VIDEO · BIOVFX ECG SERIES, PART 2 OF 3 (ECG or EKG) Wave patterns. Part 2 BioVFX. Upward and downward deflections, and how to identify the P, Q, R, S and T waves. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © BioVFX, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation on this page does not depend on this video. P wave: the atria are switched on. The signal starts in the SA node and spreads across both atria. The P wave is that spread through the atrial muscle; the SA node itself is too small to show. The atria contract just after it. Normal duration: 0.08–0.10 s. PR interval: from the atria switching on to the ventricles switching on. It runs from the start of the P wave to the start of the QRS. Normal: 0.12–0.20 s. It includes the spread across the atria, the hold-up in the AV node and the run down the His– Purkinje fibres. So the PR interval is not just the AV delay. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §6) The PR segment is shorter. It runs from the end of the P wave to the start of the QRS, when the signal is passing through the AV node and His–Purkinje fibres. OPTIONAL VIDEO · BIOVFX ECG SERIES, PART 3 OF 3 (ECG or EKG) PR interval part 3 BioVFX. The PR interval, measured from the start of the P wave to the start of the QRS complex, normally about 120–200 ms. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © BioVFX, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation on this page does not depend on this video. QRS complex: the ventricles are switched on. The ventricles have far more muscle than the atria, so the QRS is much bigger than the P wave. Normal duration: 0.06–0.10 s in the lecture; clinically, anything under 0.12 s is accepted as normal. Q: a downward deflection before the first upward one R: the upward deflection S: a downward deflection after the R Not every lead shows all three. Where is the atria resetting? It happens at the same time as the QRS, and its small signal is hidden inside the much bigger one. ST segment: the whole ventricle is switched on. It runs from the end of the QRS (the J point) to the start of the T wave. All the ventricular cells are in the plateau of their action potential at the same voltage, so there is no voltage difference for the electrodes to pick up, and the line is flat. Flat does not mean resting: this is when the ventricles are contracting hardest. The lecture stresses that the ST segment can rise or fall when the heart muscle is short of oxygen (ischaemia). T wave: the ventricles reset. Resetting spreads more slowly than switching on, so the T wave is longer and lower than the QRS. U wave: sometimes seen. A small wave after the T. The lecture describes it as the last remnants of ventricular repolarisation; its exact source is still debated. QT interval: the ventricles switch on and reset. It runs from the start of the QRS to the end of the T wave, roughly one ventricular action potential. It is 0.2–0.4 s, and it shortens as heart rate rises, so there is no single normal QT value. Part What happens Normal (lecture) P wave Atria switch on 0.08–0.10 s PR interval Start of P to start of QRS 0.12–0.20 s QRS complex Ventricles switch on 0.06–0.10 s ST segment Whole ventricle switched on (plateau) Flat at the baseline T wave Ventricles reset Longer than QRS QT interval Ventricles switch on and reset 0.2–0.4 s, depending on heart rate Source note: Values are the lecture's. \"Under 0.12 s\" for the QRS is the usual clinical cut-off. Doctors correct the QT for heart rate (the QTc, often with Bazett's formula, QT ÷ √RR); that is beyond this lecture. 5. Connect the ECG to mechanical events Each electrical event comes just before the mechanical event it triggers: ECG Electrical event What follows mechanically P wave Atria switch on Atria contract and top up the ventricles PR interval The signal is held in the AV node The atria finish emptying before the ventricles contract QRS Ventricles switch on Ventricles contract; S1 is heard soon after ST segment Whole ventricle switched on Ventricles eject blood T wave Ventricles reset Ventricles relax; S2 comes near the end of the T wave The Wiggers diagram puts these electrical events above the pressure and volume curves of the same beat. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §9) Understanding Check: Sections 3–5 1. At the same instant, Lead I reads +0.6 mV and Lead III reads +0.4 mV. What does Lead II read? A. +0.2 mV, because Lead II equals Lead I minus Lead III B. +1.0 mV, because Lead II equals Lead I plus Lead III C. +0.5 mV, because Lead II is the average of the other two D. +2.4 mV, because Lead II is the product of the other two Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Einthoven's law: Lead II = Lead I + Lead III, compared at the same instant. 2. A student measures from the end of the P wave to the start of the QRS and calls it the PR interval. What has the student left out? A. The QRS complex, which the interval also includes B. The AV delay, which happens after the QRS starts C. The P wave, which the interval includes from its start D. The T wave, which ends the interval Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The PR interval starts at the beginning of the P wave. What the student measured is the PR segment. 3. Why is the ST segment flat, even though the ventricles are contracting hard? A. The ventricles are electrically silent and fully at rest B. The atria are resetting and cancel out the ventricles C. All ventricular cells are at the same voltage, so no difference is picked up D. The electrodes stop recording while blood is ejected Show answer and explanation Answer: C. During the plateau, every ventricular cell sits at about the same voltage. With no difference between regions, the lead draws a flat line. 4. Why is the QRS complex much bigger than the P wave? A. The ventricles have far more muscle to switch on B. The QRS includes the atria resetting as well C. The ventricles are closer to the electrodes D. The AV node amplifies the signal Show answer and explanation Answer: A. More muscle switching on gives a bigger total signal. The atria resetting is hidden inside the QRS, but it is tiny and not the reason. 6. How do ECG paper squares represent time and voltage? ECG paper is a grid of small squares (1 mm) grouped into large squares (5 small squares, outlined in bold). In the lecture's standard settings, the paper moves at 25 mm/s, and 10 mm of height = 1 mV. Reading the ECG paper 1 mV 10 mm tall 1 large square = 0.20 s 25 small squares = 1 second 1 small square 0.04 s · 0.1 mV Standard settings: paper speed 25 mm/s, and 10 mm of height = 1 mV. One small square = 0.04 s across and 0.1 mV up; one large square = 0.20 s and 0.5 mV. Across the paper is time. The paper moves 25 small squares every second. So: 1 small square = 1 ÷ 25 = 0.04 s 1 large square = 5 × 0.04 = 0.20 s 5 large squares = 1 second Up the paper is voltage. 10 small squares = 1 mV. So: 1 small square = 0.1 mV 1 large square = 0.5 mV Don't mix the two. A taller QRS is a bigger voltage, not a longer one. To measure how long something lasts, count squares across. Try it: a PR interval spans 4 small squares. How long is it? 4 × 0.04 = 0.16 s, which is normal (0.12–0.20 s). In large squares, a normal PR interval is 3 to 5 small squares (0.12–0.20 s), and a normal QRS is under 3 small squares (under 0.12 s). 7. Calculate heart rate from the tracing The idea in one sentence: work out how long one beat takes, then see how many of those beats fit into a minute. Step 1: count the small squares between two R waves. Step 2: change squares to seconds. Multiply by 0.04. This is the RR interval, the time for one beat. Step 3: change to beats per minute. Divide 60 by the RR interval. The lecture's example: 22 small squares. Step 1: 22 small squares Step 2: 22 × 0.04 = 0.88 s per beat Step 3: 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ 68 beats/min The quicker way, and where 1500 comes from. In one minute the paper moves 25 mm/s × 60 s = 1500 mm, which is 1500 small squares. If one beat takes 22 squares, the number of beats in a minute is: 1500 ÷ 22 ≈ 68 beats/min. Same answer, one step. Counting large squares works the same way: 1500 small squares = 300 large squares, so 300 ÷ 4.4 large squares ≈ 68. General relationship HR = 60 RR in seconds 25 mm/s · regular rhythm HR = 1500 N small squares Or 300 ÷ number of large squares between consecutive R waves. No calculator? Use the countdown. Find an R wave that sits on a thick line. For each thick line after it, say the next number in the countdown. Stop where the next R wave lands: Next R wave lands after… 1 large square 2 3 4 5 6 Heart rate (beats/min) 300 150 100 75 60 50 Each number is just 300 ÷ the number of large squares. If the R wave falls between two lines, the rate lies between those two numbers. For the lecture's strip (4.4 large squares), it lands between 75 and 60, nearer 75: about 68. Rate ruler A strip of ECG paper with two R waves. A slider sets the gap between them, from 10 to 40 small squares. Underneath, the three steps update live: squares → seconds (× 0.04) → beats/min (60 ÷ RR), with the 1500 shortcut and the countdown range beside them. It starts at the lecture's 22 squares, 68 beats/min. A second example: 15 small squares. 15 × 0.04 = 0.6 s; 60 ÷ 0.6 = 100 beats/min. Check: 1500 ÷ 15 = 100, or 3 large squares on the countdown = 100. ✓ If the rhythm is irregular, the gaps between R waves vary, so one gap gives the rate for one beat only. Instead, count the QRS complexes in a 6-second strip (30 large squares) and multiply by 10, because 6 s is one tenth of a minute. Eight complexes in 6 s ≈ 80 beats/min. Three common mistakes Dividing 1500 by large squares. Use 1500 for small squares and 300 for large squares. Using the 1500 rule on an irregular rhythm. Count complexes in 6 s × 10 instead. Forgetting the paper speed. At 50 mm/s each small square is 0.02 s, so the rules become 3000 ÷ small squares and 600 ÷ large squares. Source note: The 1500 and 300 rules and the 22-square example are from the lecture. The countdown, the 6-second method and the 50 mm/s adjustment are standard supplementary teaching. 8. Basic rhythm assessment (supplementary) The lecture focuses on ECG basics, not rhythm diagnosis. But four questions turn the trace into a first look at the rhythm: Rate: is it between about 60 and 100 beats/min at rest? Regularity: are the RR intervals equal? P waves: is there one before every QRS, and do they all look the same? Intervals: is the PR interval 0.12–0.20 s, and the QRS narrow? Normal sinus rhythm answers yes to all four. The PR interval also shows whether the signal is getting through the AV node: What you see Meaning PR longer than 0.20 s, but every P is followed by a QRS First-degree heart block: delayed, but always gets through Some P waves are not followed by a QRS Second-degree heart block: some signals are dropped P waves and QRS complexes at their own separate rates Third-degree (complete) heart block: nothing gets through The mechanisms are explained in Cardiac Conduction. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §8) Understanding Check: Sections 6–8 1. At 25 mm/s, two R waves are 20 small squares apart. What is the heart rate? A. 50 beats/min, because 1000 ÷ 20 = 50 B. 60 beats/min, because 1200 ÷ 20 = 60 C. 75 beats/min, because 1500 ÷ 20 = 75 D. 80 beats/min, because 1600 ÷ 20 = 80 Show answer and explanation Answer: C. At 25 mm/s, 1500 small squares pass in a minute, so 1500 ÷ 20 = 75. Check: 20 × 0.04 = 0.8 s, and 60 ÷ 0.8 = 75. 2. On a regular strip, each R wave lands 3 large squares after the one before. What is the heart rate? A. About 75 beats/min B. About 100 beats/min C. About 150 beats/min D. About 300 beats/min Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The countdown runs 300, 150, 100, so 3 large squares is 100 beats/min (300 ÷ 3). 3. Two QRS complexes look identical, but one is twice as tall. What does the extra height tell you? A. That beat lasted twice as long B. That beat had a bigger voltage C. That beat came at twice the rate D. That beat had a longer PR interval Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Height is voltage; width is time. A taller complex is a bigger signal, not a longer one. 4. An irregular rhythm shows 9 QRS complexes in a 6-second strip. What is the approximate rate? A. About 54 beats/min, from 9 × 6 B. About 75 beats/min, from 1500 ÷ 20 squares C. About 90 beats/min, from 9 × 10 D. About 150 beats/min, from 9 ÷ 6 × 100 Show answer and explanation Answer: C. A 6-second strip is one tenth of a minute, so multiply by 10. For an irregular rhythm this is more reliable than measuring one RR interval. 5. Every P wave is followed by a QRS, but the PR interval is 0.28 s. What does this show? A. Complete heart block: no signals get through B. Second-degree heart block: some signals are dropped C. First-degree heart block: signals are delayed but all get through D. A normal rhythm, because every P has a QRS Show answer and explanation Answer: C. A PR interval over 0.20 s means the signal is slow through the AV node, but since every P is followed by a QRS, none are dropped. 9. Chapter Essentials The ECG records electricity, not pumping. A normal trace does not prove the heart is ejecting blood. Electrode = skin contact; lead = a view. Ten electrodes give twelve leads; the right-leg electrode is an earth. Direction rule: depolarisation towards a lead draws an upward deflection; away draws a downward one; across draws a small up-and-down one. The T wave is usually upright like the QRS: a resetting wave draws the opposite way to a switching-on wave, but the ventricles reset in the reverse order, so the two reversals cancel out. Leads: I, II and III each compare two limb electrodes (Einthoven's triangle, II = I + III); aVR, aVL and aVF each look from one limb; V1–V6 look across the chest in a horizontal slice. P = atria switch on (0.08–0.10 s); QRS = ventricles switch on (0.06–0.10 s); T = ventricles reset. PR interval = start of P to start of QRS (0.12–0.20 s), including the AV delay. The PR segment starts at the end of P. ST segment = whole ventricle switched on (plateau): every cell sits at the same voltage, so the line is flat even though the muscle is active. Ischaemia can raise or lower it. QT interval = start of QRS to end of T (0.2–0.4 s), shorter at higher heart rates. Paper: 25 mm/s and 10 mm/mV. Small square = 0.04 s and 0.1 mV; large square = 0.20 s and 0.5 mV. Rate (regular rhythm): 60 ÷ RR (s), 1500 ÷ small squares or 300 ÷ large squares. Irregular: QRS complexes in 6 s × 10. Heart block: first degree = PR over 0.20 s, every P conducted; second degree = some P waves dropped; third degree = P and QRS independent.","cardiac-output":"Cardiac Output and the Frank–Starling Law 1. What is cardiac output, and why does it matter? Cardiac output (CO) is the volume of blood one ventricle pumps out each minute. It is how much blood the heart delivers to the body, not how much it ejects in one beat. It depends on two things: Heart rate (HR): beats per minute. Stroke volume (SV): the blood ejected by one ventricle in one beat. Cardiac output CO = HR × SV Example: 75 beats/min × 70 mL/beat = 5,250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min Why multiply? Each beat sends out one stroke volume. 75 beats of 70 mL each is 75 × 70 mL in a minute. At rest, cardiac output is about 5–5.5 L/min in an adult. That is roughly the whole blood volume pumped round once a minute. It varies with body size and activity. Why must the right and left ventricles pump the same amount? The two circulations are joined in series. Everything the right ventricle pumps into the lungs comes back to the left ventricle. If the right ventricle pumped even slightly more each minute than the left, blood would pile up in the lungs. So over time the two outputs must be equal, even though the left ventricle works at a much higher pressure. ( → Introduction, §2) Cardiac index: allowing for body size A bigger person needs more blood flow than a smaller one. Cardiac index (CI) divides cardiac output by body surface area (BSA), so people of different sizes can be compared fairly. Cardiac index CI = CO BSA For CO = 5.25 L/min and BSA = 1.75 m²: CI = 3.0 L/min/m². 2. Why can changing heart rate alter cardiac output? From CO = HR × SV, a faster heart rate raises cardiac output only if stroke volume holds up. Sympathetic nerves usually raise both. They speed the SA node (positive chronotropy) and make each contraction stronger (positive inotropy). So in exercise, HR and SV rise together, and CO rises a lot. But a very fast heart can pump less. The ventricles fill during diastole. As heart rate rises, diastole shortens much more than systole. At very high rates there is too little time to fill, so each beat ejects less. Example: 75 beats/min × 70 mL = 5.25 L/min. 130 beats/min × 35 mL = 4.55 L/min. The heart is beating faster but pumping less. HR ↑ More beats per minute SV ↓ markedly Less blood each beat CO may ↓ Depends on their product Rule: never predict cardiac output from heart rate alone. Always ask what happened to stroke volume. Understanding Check: Sections 1–2 1. A heart beats 80 times a minute and ejects 60 mL each beat. What is the cardiac output? A. 1.3 L/min, because 80 ÷ 60 = 1.3 B. 4.8 L/min, because 80 × 60 mL = 4,800 mL/min C. 14.0 L/min, because 80 + 60 = 140 D. 48.0 L/min, because 80 × 60 = 48,000 mL/min Show answer and explanation Answer: B. CO = HR × SV = 80 × 60 mL = 4,800 mL/min = 4.8 L/min. 2. Why must right and left ventricular outputs be equal over time? A. Both ventricles generate exactly the same pressure B. The two circulations are in series, so unequal outputs would pile up blood C. The septum lets blood mix until both ventricles hold the same volume D. The same nerve fires both ventricles at the same strength Show answer and explanation Answer: B. All blood leaving the right ventricle returns to the left. Unequal outputs would move blood steadily from one circulation into the other. 3. Heart rate rises from 75 to 130 beats/min, while stroke volume falls from 70 to 35 mL. What happens to cardiac output? A. It rises, because the heart is beating faster B. It stays the same, because the two changes cancel out C. It falls, because SV fell proportionally more than HR rose D. It cannot change, because CO depends only on venous return Show answer and explanation Answer: C. 75 × 70 = 5.25 L/min; 130 × 35 = 4.55 L/min. Shorter filling time cut SV by half, which outweighed the faster rate. 3. What determines stroke volume? Stroke volume is what the ventricle starts with minus what it leaves behind: SV = EDV − ESV (end-diastolic volume minus end-systolic volume). ( → Cardiac Cycle, §7) Three things decide it: Factor The question it answers If it rises (others unchanged) Preload How stretched is the ventricle before it contracts? SV rises (within limits) Afterload How hard is it to push the blood out? SV falls Contractility How strongly can the muscle contract at the same stretch? SV rises Preload: the starting stretch Preload is how stretched the ventricular muscle is at the end of filling, just before it contracts. The more blood that fills the ventricle, the more it stretches. So end-diastolic volume (EDV) is the usual way to estimate preload. Volume and pressure are not the same. A stiff ventricle can have a high filling pressure while holding little blood, so filling pressure can mislead. Afterload: the load against ejection Afterload is what the ventricle must push against to eject blood. For the left ventricle, it is mainly the pressure in the aorta: the aortic valve only opens once ventricular pressure exceeds it. A higher aortic pressure means more of each contraction is spent just reaching that pressure, so less blood is ejected and more is left behind (higher ESV). Contractility: how strong the muscle is at a given stretch Contractility ( inotropy) is how forcefully the muscle contracts at the same stretch and the same load. It depends mainly on how much calcium reaches the contractile proteins in each beat. ( → Cardiac Muscle, §4) Key distinction: a ventricle that contracts harder because it was stretched more is using the Frank–Starling mechanism. That is not a rise in contractility. A rise in contractility means a stronger contraction without extra stretch. 4. Frank–Starling law: why does more filling produce more ejection? The law: within normal limits, the more the ventricle is filled, the more it ejects. Why does stretched muscle contract harder? Two reasons: Better overlap of the filaments. At normal resting volumes, the heart's sarcomeres are shorter than their best length. Stretching them brings actin and myosin closer to the length where the most cross-bridges can form. The muscle becomes more sensitive to calcium. Stretch makes the contractile proteins respond more strongly to the same amount of calcium. Venous return ↑ → EDV and fibre stretch ↑ → Force generated ↑ → SV ↑ What is it for? Matching output to input, beat by beat. If more blood returns to a ventricle, it stretches more and ejects more, so blood doesn't pile up. This is also what keeps the two ventricles in step: if the right ventricle sends a little extra to the lungs, the left ventricle fills a little more and pumps it out. No nerve signal is needed. It is built into the muscle. Does more filling always mean more output? No. At high filling, the curve flattens: extra volume adds less and less SV. A badly failing ventricle may sit on a flat curve, where extra filling mainly raises pressure behind the heart and causes congestion. Source note: The lecture's curve shows a dashed falling limb at extreme stretch. That is a teaching extension: an intact human heart does not normally work on a falling limb. 5. How do we read Frank–Starling curves? A Frank–Starling curve plots stroke volume (or cardiac output) against filling (EDV). There are two ways the point can move. Frank–Starling curves at a glance More contractility Normal Less contractility 1 2 Filling (end-diastolic volume) → Stroke volume → 1 Same curve: more filling gives a bigger stroke volume (the Frank–Starling law). 2 Same filling, higher curve: stronger contraction (contractility) gives more. Schematic, not patient measurements; afterload held constant. Frank–Starling explorer Replaces the static curves. Three curves: low, normal and high contractility. A slider sets EDV, and the dot slides along the current curve. Buttons switch contractility, and the dot jumps up or down to another curve at the same EDV. A readout shows EDV, SV and CO (at 70 beats/min), and one sentence says which kind of change just happened. Moving along the same curve: a change in preload Someone who has been standing lies down. Blood stops pooling in the legs and returns to the heart, so EDV rises. The point moves right and up along the same curve: more filling, more SV. Contractility has not changed. Jumping to another curve: a change in contractility Two beats have the same EDV, but before the second one, sympathetic nerves raise calcium entry. The second beat ejects more without any extra stretch: the point jumps to a higher curve. A weakened heart muscle sits on a lower curve. Careful: a higher afterload also lowers the curve. So a lower curve doesn't prove that contractility has fallen, unless afterload is the same. Two lecture terms: heterometric and homometric Term Meaning (break it down) Example Heterometric regulation hetero = different, metric = length: output changes because the starting length changes More venous return → more stretch → more SV (Frank–Starling) Homometric regulation homo = same: output changes at the same starting length Sympathetic stimulation → stronger beat at the same EDV Understanding Check: Sections 3–5 1. Two beats have the same EDV, but the second ejects more blood after sympathetic stimulation. What changed? A. Preload rose, moving the beat along the same curve B. Contractility rose, shifting the beat to a higher curve C. Afterload rose, making ejection easier for the ventricle D. Venous return rose, stretching the fibres more Show answer and explanation Answer: B. With EDV unchanged, the stretch is the same. A bigger SV at the same stretch means stronger contraction: a higher curve (homometric regulation). 2. Why does a stretched ventricle contract more forcefully? A. Stretch opens more gap junctions between the cells B. Stretch triggers a sympathetic reflex from the atria C. Stretch brings filaments to better overlap and raises calcium sensitivity D. Stretch lowers aortic pressure so ejection is easier Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Normal resting sarcomeres are shorter than optimal, so stretch improves actin–myosin overlap, and the proteins also respond more strongly to calcium. 3. Aortic pressure suddenly rises, with no change in filling or contractility. What happens to the next beat? A. SV rises, because the ventricle contracts against more force B. SV is unchanged, because preload and contractility are the same C. SV falls and more blood is left behind in the ventricle D. SV falls because the ventricle fills less during diastole Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Higher afterload means more of the contraction is spent reaching aortic pressure, so less is ejected and ESV rises. 4. Someone who has stood still for a long time lies down. Where does their heart move on the Frank–Starling graph? A. Along the same curve, to the right and up B. To a higher curve, at the same EDV C. Along the same curve, to the left and down D. To a lower curve, at the same EDV Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Lying down returns pooled blood to the heart, raising EDV. More filling moves the point along the same curve: preload changed, not contractility. 6. What raises or lowers end-diastolic volume? EDV depends on how much blood comes back, how long the ventricle has to fill and how easily it stretches. Raises EDV Why More blood volume More blood available to return Veins tightening (venous tone ↑) Squeezes blood out of the veins towards the heart Skeletal muscle pump Contracting muscles squeeze veins; valves keep blood moving towards the heart Breathing (respiratory pump) Breathing in lowers chest pressure and draws blood in Strong atrial contraction Tops up the ventricle at the end of filling Longer filling time (slower heart rate) More time for blood to enter Lowers EDV Why Blood loss Less blood to return Standing still Gravity pools blood in the leg veins Fluid around the heart ( tamponade) Pressure from outside stops the ventricle expanding A stiff ventricle It resists stretching, so it fills less A very fast heart rate Too little time to fill The lecture also lists heart attack (myocardial infarction) and endomyocardial fibrosis (EMF). Both damage or stiffen the ventricle wall, so filling and pumping are affected, and the effect on EDV depends on the disease and its stage. 7. What changes contractility? Sympathetic stimulation is the main way to raise contractility. Sympathetic nerves release noradrenaline onto the heart, and the adrenal medulla releases adrenaline into the blood. Both act on β₁ receptors on heart muscle cells: β₁ receptor → Gs / adenylyl cyclase → cAMP / PKA ↑ → Ca²⁺ cycling ↑ → Stronger contraction In plain terms, the signal ends up letting more calcium in through L-type channels and more calcium out of the sarcoplasmic reticulum. More calcium means more cross-bridges and a stronger beat (positive inotropy). The same signal also speeds the pumping of calcium back into storage, so the muscle relaxes faster too (positive lusitropy), which helps the ventricle refill at fast heart rates. Caffeine and theophylline ( xanthines), which the lecture lists, slow the breakdown of cAMP, so they can strengthen contraction. In everyday doses the effect is small. The vagus nerve has little direct effect on ventricular strength. It mainly slows heart rate and AV conduction. Vagal nerve endings reach the atria and the nodes, but hardly reach the ventricular muscle. What weakens contraction? The lecture lists too much CO₂ (hypercapnia), too little oxygen (hypoxia), acidosis, some drugs and heart failure. Low oxygen and acid both interfere with the cell's energy supply and its calcium handling, so each beat is weaker. Source note: One lecture slide reverses the usual sources. Most of the noradrenaline reaching the heart comes from sympathetic nerves, while circulating adrenaline comes mainly from the adrenal medulla. 8. How do preload, afterload and inotropy differ in real situations? More venous return Preload ↑ Often moves along the curve → SV ↑ Higher aortic pressure Afterload ↑ Ejection becomes harder → SV may ↓ β₁ stimulation Contractility ↑ More force at same EDV → SV ↑ Three patients show why each factor must be thought about separately: Blood loss: heart rate rises, but there is so little blood returning (low preload) that SV and CO can still fall. Aortic stenosis: even a strong contraction has to force blood through a narrow valve, a high load against ejection. Heart failure: the curve is flat, so extra filling raises pressure and congestion more than SV. 9. What happens to cardiac output during exercise and other states? In exercise, everything pushes cardiac output up at once: Sympathetic nerves raise heart rate and contractility. Contracting muscles and deeper breathing push more blood back to the heart ( more venous return), so preload rises. Blood vessels in active muscle widen, lowering resistance so blood flows out more easily. So both HR and SV rise, and CO can increase several-fold. Source note: One lecture slide shows a downward arrow for venous return during exercise. The lecture's own diagrams, and standard physiology, show that the muscle and breathing pumps increase venous return in exercise. The lecture lists other states that raise cardiac output: eating, a hot environment, pregnancy, anxiety and excitement, adrenaline-like drugs and an overactive thyroid (thyrotoxicosis). Each works through different mechanisms. Blood loss (link to shock): after a large bleed, venous return and preload fall. Reflex sympathetic activity raises heart rate and tightens blood vessels to hold up blood pressure for a while, but if SV falls far enough, CO and tissue blood flow still fall. ( → Shock) Understanding Check: Sections 6–9 1. Which change would lower end-diastolic volume? A. Contracting leg muscles while walking briskly B. A slower heart rate that lengthens diastole C. Fluid around the heart pressing on the ventricles D. Veins tightening under sympathetic stimulation Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Fluid in the pericardium (tamponade) presses on the heart from outside, so the ventricles can't expand and fill. The other three all increase filling. 2. How does sympathetic stimulation make each contraction stronger? A. It stretches the ventricle so filaments overlap better B. It raises cAMP, so more calcium reaches the filaments C. It slows the heart, giving more time for filling D. It lowers aortic pressure, making ejection easier Show answer and explanation Answer: B. β₁ stimulation raises cAMP, which increases calcium entry and release. More calcium means more cross-bridges and a stronger beat at the same stretch. 3. A patient in severe heart failure is given a large volume of fluid. Why might their SV barely rise? A. Their ventricle sits on a flat part of a low curve B. Fluid lowers their heart rate and so their output C. Extra fluid raises their contractility too much D. Fluid reduces afterload, so the ventricle empties early Show answer and explanation Answer: A. On a flat, low curve, extra filling adds little SV but raises the pressure behind the heart, worsening congestion. 4. Why does dynamic exercise increase venous return? A. Gravity drains blood from the legs more easily during exercise B. The vagus nerve opens the venous valves during exercise C. Lower heart rate gives the veins more time to empty D. Contracting muscles and deep breathing pump blood back to the heart Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Muscle contractions squeeze the veins (valves keep the flow heading to the heart), and deeper breathing draws blood into the chest. 5. After a large bleed, a patient's heart rate is 130 beats/min, yet their tissues are poorly perfused. What is the best explanation? A. A high heart rate always reduces cardiac output B. Low preload cut SV so much that faster beating could not make up for it C. Sympathetic stimulation weakens contraction after blood loss D. The extra beats raise afterload, so no blood is ejected Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Blood loss reduces venous return and EDV, so SV falls. Since CO = HR × SV, a large enough fall in SV beats the rise in HR. 10. Chapter Essentials CO = HR × SV, about 5–5.5 L/min at rest. Right and left outputs must be equal over time (in series). Cardiac index = CO ÷ body surface area (L/min/m²). Heart rate alone doesn't predict CO. Very fast rates shorten filling and can lower SV enough to drop CO. SV = EDV − ESV, set by preload, afterload and contractility. Preload = the stretch before contraction (estimated by EDV). Afterload = what the ventricle pushes against (mainly aortic pressure). Contractility = strength at the same stretch. Frank–Starling: more filling → more stretch → better filament overlap and calcium sensitivity → more SV. It matches output to input and keeps the two ventricles in step. Along the curve = preload change (heterometric). To a new curve = contractility change (homometric). Afterload also shifts curves. EDV rises with more blood volume, venous tone, muscle and breathing pumps, atrial contraction and longer filling. It falls with blood loss, standing, tamponade, stiff ventricles and very fast rates. Sympathetic β₁ → cAMP → more calcium → stronger contraction and faster relaxation. The vagus mainly slows the heart and barely weakens the ventricles. Hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis, some drugs and heart failure weaken contraction. Exercise raises HR, contractility and venous return together. Blood loss lowers preload, and tachycardia may not save CO.","co-measurement":"Measuring Cardiac Output and the Work of the Heart 1. Why measure cardiac output instead of calculating it from heart rate alone? CO = HR × SV, but at the bedside stroke volume can't be seen or felt. A pulse tells you the heart rate, not how much blood each beat pushes out. So we need a way to measure blood flow directly. The lecture's method is Fick's principle, which works out flow from how much oxygen the body uses. ( → Cardiac Output, §1) 2. Fick's principle: working out flow from oxygen use The idea, with a delivery van. Vans carry parcels to a town. Each van arrives with 20 parcels and leaves with 15, so it drops off 5. The town takes in 250 parcels a minute. How many vans pass through each minute? 250 ÷ 5 = 50 vans a minute. Blood works the same way: Oxygen used by the body each minute (V̇O₂) is the parcels delivered. Oxygen dropped off by each mL of blood (arterial − mixed venous content) is the parcels per van. Blood flow (cardiac output) is the number of vans. Arterial O₂ content O₂ carried into tissues − Mixed venous O₂ content O₂ remaining after tissues → O₂ extracted Amount removed from each unit of blood Fick's oxygen principle V̇O₂ = CO × ( CₐO₂ − CᵥO₂) Fick cardiac output CO = V̇O₂ ( CₐO₂ − CᵥO₂) The lecture's example, in three steps: Oxygen used: 250 mL O₂/min Oxygen dropped off per mL of blood: 0.20 − 0.15 = 0.05 mL O₂ per mL of blood Flow: 250 ÷ 0.05 = 5,000 mL blood/min = 5.0 L/min Check the units: (mL O₂/min) ÷ (mL O₂ per mL blood) = mL blood/min. The oxygen cancels and leaves blood flow. Fick calculator Three sliders: oxygen use (150–1,000 mL/min), arterial O₂ content (0.10–0.22 mL/mL) and mixed venous O₂ content (0.05–0.18 mL/mL; it cannot exceed arterial). The three steps update live, with the answer in L/min. A small bar shows the oxygen dropped off per mL. It starts at the lecture's 250, 0.20 and 0.15 → 5.0 L/min. One line notes the pattern: when tissues take more oxygen from each mL, the same oxygen use needs less flow. Content, not saturation. Fick needs the amount of oxygen in each mL of blood (content). Saturation only tells you what percentage of haemoglobin is carrying oxygen. Someone with anaemia can be 98% saturated but carry much less oxygen per mL, because there is less haemoglobin. Using saturation in place of content gives the wrong answer. 3. Where do the oxygen measurements come from? Arterial content: from any systemic artery, since arterial blood is the same everywhere. Mixed venous content: from the pulmonary artery, through a catheter. Why the pulmonary artery? Veins from different organs carry very different amounts of oxygen. The kidney takes little oxygen out, while working muscle takes a lot. Their blood only becomes fully mixed after passing through the right heart. A sample from one arm vein shows only what the arm used. Why not use a finger pulse oximeter? It measures arterial saturation only. It can't measure venous content or blood flow. Direct versus estimated Fick. A true (direct) Fick measures oxygen use at the time. Some clinics use a predicted value instead, which adds error. Either way, the method assumes a steady state: oxygen use and flow must be stable while the samples are taken. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Oxygen use is 250 mL/min. Arterial content is 0.20 and mixed venous content 0.15 mL O₂ per mL blood. What is the cardiac output? A. 1.25 L/min, from 250 × 0.05 ÷ 10 B. 5.0 L/min, from 250 ÷ 0.05 C. 12.5 L/min, from 250 ÷ 0.02 D. 50.0 L/min, from 250 ÷ 0.005 Show answer and explanation Answer: B. 0.20 − 0.15 = 0.05 mL O₂ per mL blood. 250 ÷ 0.05 = 5,000 mL/min = 5.0 L/min. 2. Why is mixed venous blood taken from the pulmonary artery, not an arm vein? A. Arm veins contain no measurable oxygen at all B. Pulmonary artery blood has been oxygenated by the lungs C. Blood from every organ has mixed by the time it reaches there D. The pulmonary artery is the easiest vessel to reach Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Organs extract different amounts of oxygen. Only after the right heart is the venous blood from the whole body mixed. Pulmonary artery blood has not yet passed through the lungs. 3. A patient with severe anaemia has 98% arterial saturation. Why can't 98% be used in the Fick formula? A. Saturation over 95% always makes the formula invalid B. Fick needs oxygen content, which is low when haemoglobin is low C. Anaemia stops oxygen from leaving the blood at the tissues D. Saturation is only measured in venous blood, not arterial Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Saturation is a percentage of haemoglobin. With little haemoglobin, each mL carries less oxygen even at 98%. 4. Oxygen use stays at 250 mL/min, but the tissues now take out 0.10 mL O₂ from each mL of blood instead of 0.05. What does this mean for cardiac output? A. It has doubled, to 10 L/min B. It is unchanged, at 5 L/min C. It has halved, to 2.5 L/min D. It cannot be worked out without heart rate Show answer and explanation Answer: C. 250 ÷ 0.10 = 2,500 mL/min. When each mL gives up more oxygen, less flow is needed for the same oxygen use. This is what happens when cardiac output falls. 4. Other ways to measure flow: indicator dilution (supplementary) The same \"what goes in must come out\" idea underlies other methods. Dye dilution. Inject a known amount of dye into a vein, then measure its concentration downstream over time. Fast flow dilutes the dye more and washes it past quickly, so the concentration curve is low and short. Slow flow gives a higher, longer curve. From the dose and the curve, flow can be worked out. Thermodilution. The same idea, using cold saline instead of dye. A pulmonary artery catheter injects it, and a temperature sensor (thermistor) downstream records how much and for how long the blood cools. Method What is measured Main pitfall Fick Oxygen use, and arterial and mixed venous oxygen content Needs a steady state and good samples Dye dilution Dye concentration over time Dye coming round a second time (recirculation) Thermodilution Blood temperature over time Leaky valves and shunts distort the curve Source note: The lecture covers Fick only. Dye dilution and thermodilution are standard supplementary methods that use the same mass-balance idea. 5. What does it mean for the heart to perform work? The heart doesn't just move blood. It pushes it against pressure, and that takes work. The lecture's simple formula: Approximate external stroke work SW ≈ SV × P P is the pressure the ventricle pumps against, usually mean arterial pressure. This is an estimate: the exact value is the area of the pressure–volume loop. Example: SV = 70 mL, mean arterial pressure = 100 mmHg → stroke work ≈ 70 × 100 = 7,000 mmHg·mL (about 0.93 J) per beat. Why does the left ventricle do more work than the right? Both pump the same volume, but the left pumps against about 100 mmHg (aorta) and the right against about 15 mmHg (pulmonary artery). Same volume against higher pressure means more work. That is why the left ventricular wall is so much thicker. ( → Introduction, §4) 6. Visualising stroke work on a pressure–volume loop Figure 1. Ventricular pressure–volume loop — enclosed area represents external stroke work Stroke work = area inside 1 2 3 4 ESV EDV Volume → Pressure → 1 Filling 2 Isovolumetric contraction 3 Ejection 4 Isovolumetric relaxation Original teaching schematic, not measured patient data. The ventricle fills at low pressure, generates pressure with constant volume, ejects blood as volume falls, and then relaxes at approximately constant end-systolic volume. Follow the loop round: Bottom: the ventricle fills at low pressure (volume rises). Right side: it contracts with all valves shut, so pressure rises at constant volume. Top: it ejects (volume falls at high pressure). Left side: it relaxes with all valves shut, so pressure falls at constant volume. The area inside the loop is the stroke work. A taller loop (higher pressure) or a wider loop (bigger SV) means more work. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §8) The loop shows only the outside work of pushing blood. The muscle also uses energy to pump calcium, keep ion gradients and hold tension, so stroke work is not the same as the heart's total oxygen use. 7. Why does pressure loading increase the heart's oxygen demand? The lecture lists three main things that set how much oxygen the heart muscle uses: Wall tension, which rises with the pressure the ventricle must develop Contractility: a stronger beat costs more energy Heart rate: more beats per minute Pressure work costs more oxygen than volume work. Raising pressure means building and holding high wall tension, which is expensive. Pumping more volume at the same pressure costs less. The lecture's examples: Aortic stenosis (pressure load): the ventricle must generate very high pressure to force blood through a narrow valve, so oxygen demand is high. Aortic regurgitation (volume load): the ventricle pumps a bigger volume each beat, but at near-normal pressure, so oxygen demand rises less. These are general patterns. Individual patients vary with severity and coronary supply. Why must coronary flow rise in exercise? Even at rest, the heart muscle extracts most of the oxygen from its blood (about 70%). There is little left to take, so when demand rises, the only way to get more oxygen is more coronary flow. If narrowed coronaries can't deliver it, the muscle runs short of oxygen and chest pain ( angina) results. ( → Coronary Circulation) Careful: Fick uses the whole body's oxygen use to measure cardiac output. The heart's own oxygen use is a separate, much smaller quantity. Understanding Check: Sections 4–7 1. In dye dilution, what does a low, short concentration curve downstream tell you? A. Flow is fast, so the dye is diluted and washed past quickly B. Flow is slow, so the dye stays in the vessel for longer C. The dye was injected into the wrong vessel entirely D. The heart rate is too slow for the method to work Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Fast flow spreads the same dose through more blood and carries it past quickly, giving a lower, shorter curve. 2. The two ventricles eject the same volume. Why does the left do more work? A. It ejects blood more slowly than the right does B. It pumps against a much higher pressure C. It has to pump blood through the lungs D. Its valves are smaller, so it ejects less Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Stroke work ≈ SV × pressure. The same SV against about 100 mmHg instead of about 15 mmHg means far more work. 3. On a pressure–volume loop, what does the enclosed area represent? A. The heart's total oxygen use B. The stroke volume C. The external stroke work of one beat D. The time for one cardiac cycle Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The area is the outside work of pushing blood against pressure. Total oxygen use is larger, and SV is the loop's width. 4. Which places the greatest oxygen demand on the left ventricle? A. Pumping a larger volume at normal pressure B. A slower heart rate with normal pressure C. A smaller stroke volume at normal pressure D. Pumping a normal volume against a very high pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: D. High pressure means high wall tension, the most oxygen-expensive kind of work. That is why aortic stenosis raises demand more than regurgitation. 5. Why must coronary blood flow rise when the heart works harder? A. Heart muscle already takes out most of the oxygen it receives B. Coronary arteries narrow during exercise C. Blood carries less oxygen during exercise D. Heart rate falls, so each beat needs more flow Show answer and explanation Answer: A. With extraction already near its maximum, the only way to get more oxygen is to deliver more blood. 8. Chapter Essentials Fick's principle: oxygen used per minute = blood flow × oxygen dropped off per mL. So CO = V̇O₂ ÷ (CaO₂ − CvO₂). The lecture's example: 250 ÷ (0.20 − 0.15) = 5,000 mL/min = 5.0 L/min. Use content, not saturation: content depends on haemoglobin. Samples: arterial from any artery; mixed venous from the pulmonary artery. Fick needs a steady state and accurate samples. Indicator dilution (supplementary): a known dose of dye or cold saline, measured downstream. Faster flow gives a lower, shorter curve. Stroke work ≈ SV × pressure. On a pressure–volume loop it is the area inside. The left ventricle does more work than the right: same volume, higher pressure. Heart muscle oxygen demand rises with wall tension, contractility and heart rate. Pressure work costs more than volume work. The heart already extracts most of its oxygen, so more demand needs more coronary flow.","hemodynamics":"Blood Flow and Haemodynamics 1. What propels blood around the circulation? The heart creates pressure, and blood flows from high pressure to low. Each beat pushes blood into the arteries at high pressure. The pressure falls step by step through arteries → arterioles → capillaries → venules → veins, back to the heart at nearly zero. Three helpers keep blood moving: Elastic recoil of large arteries. In systole the aorta stretches like a balloon. In diastole it springs back and keeps pushing blood forward, so flow continues between beats. The skeletal muscle pump. Contracting muscles squeeze the veins. Valves in the veins let blood go only towards the heart. The respiratory pump. Breathing in lowers pressure in the chest and draws blood into the large veins. These help, but the heart is the main pressure source. 2. What actually determines flow? Flow depends on the pressure difference, not the pressure. Picture two water tanks joined by a pipe. Water flows only if one tank is higher than the other, and faster the bigger the difference. Raise both tanks equally and nothing changes. Pressure, flow and resistance Q = Δ P R R = Δ P Q Q = blood volume per time; ΔP = upstream − downstream pressure; R = vascular resistance. Q = flow (volume per minute) ΔP = pressure at the start minus pressure at the end R = resistance: how hard the vessels make it for blood to pass Example: a pressure drop of 80 mmHg across a resistance of 20 mmHg·min/L gives 80 ÷ 20 = 4 L/min. So high pressure alone doesn't mean high flow. A blocked artery can have high pressure upstream and almost no flow through it. 3. Why do different vessels have different roles? Vessel What its wall is like Its job Large arteries Thick and elastic Stretch in systole, recoil in diastole (keep flow going) Arterioles Thick smooth-muscle layer Control resistance, and so flow into each organ Capillaries One cell thick Exchange gases, nutrients and fluid Veins Thin, stretchy, with valves Hold most of the blood; return it to the heart Arterioles are \"resistance vessels\" because their muscle can narrow or widen them quickly, and a small change in radius changes resistance hugely ( §6). Veins are \"capacitance vessels\" ( capacity = how much they hold) because they stretch easily. They can take in a lot of extra blood with little rise in pressure. When sympathetic nerves tighten them, that stored blood is pushed back to the heart ( §10). Capillary structure and lymph are covered in their own chapters. ( → Microcirculation) ( → Lymphatics) Source note: One slide gives capillary gaps and fenestrations in millimetres. They are in fact nanometres to micrometres wide. 4. Why is blood velocity lowest in capillaries? Flow and velocity are different things. Flow (Q) = how much blood passes per minute (L/min). Velocity (v) = how fast it moves (cm/s). Continuity relation Q = v A v = Q A A = total cross-sectional area of the vessels considered. A = the total cross-sectional area of all the vessels at that level. Think of a motorway. The same number of cars per minute has to pass every point. Where the road is one lane, the cars must go fast. Where it splits into hundreds of lanes, each car can go slowly. Each capillary is tiny, but there are billions of them side by side. Their total area is far bigger than the aorta's, so blood moves through them most slowly. This gives time for exchange. Blood speeds up again as the capillaries join into fewer, larger veins. Understanding Check: Sections 1–4 1. Why does blood keep flowing in the arteries during ventricular diastole? A. The ventricles keep ejecting a little blood in diastole B. The stretched aorta recoils and keeps pushing blood forward C. Valves in the arteries pump blood towards the tissues D. The veins pull blood out of the arteries by suction Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The aorta stretches in systole and springs back in diastole, keeping the pressure up and the blood moving. 2. Pressure falls by 90 mmHg across a vascular bed with a resistance of 30 mmHg·min/L. What is the flow? A. 0.3 L/min, from 30 ÷ 90 B. 3 L/min, from 90 ÷ 30 C. 60 L/min, from 90 − 30 D. 2,700 L/min, from 90 × 30 Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Q = ΔP ÷ R = 90 ÷ 30 = 3 L/min. 3. Each capillary is very narrow, yet blood moves slowest in capillaries. Why? A. Capillary walls create a lot of friction B. Capillaries have the highest blood pressure C. Their total cross-sectional area is enormous D. Red cells block the capillaries completely Show answer and explanation Answer: C. v = Q ÷ A. The same flow spread across a huge total area moves slowly, like traffic spread across many lanes. 4. Why are veins called capacitance vessels? A. They can hold a lot of extra blood with little rise in pressure B. Their muscle controls resistance to blood flow C. They are the main site of oxygen exchange D. They have the highest pressure in the circulation Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Veins stretch easily, so they store large volumes at low pressure. Arterioles are the resistance vessels. 5. Laminar flow, turbulence and Reynolds number Laminar flow means blood moves in smooth layers ( lamina = thin layer). The layer touching the wall is held back by friction and moves slowest. Each layer inward slides faster, and the centre is fastest. Figure 1. Idealised laminar blood-flow profile Vessel wall Arrow length = speed: slowest at the wall, fastest in the centre. The ideal no-slip profile explains the lecturer's arrows. Actual blood contains cells and flowing vessels branch, pulse and deform, so the profile is not always exactly parabolic. Laminar flow is silent. When flow becomes turbulent, swirling in eddies, it makes noise: murmurs in the heart, bruits in arteries. ( → Heart Sounds, §6) What makes turbulence more likely? The Reynolds number (Re) sums it up: Reynolds number Re = ρ v D η ρ = density; v = velocity; D = diameter; η = dynamic viscosity. Turbulence is more likely when: velocity (v) is high, as through a narrowed valve or artery; the vessel is wide (D); viscosity (η) is low, as in anaemia, where thinner blood flows more easily. A high Re makes turbulence more likely, not certain. Branching and sudden narrowing also matter. Don't confuse R with Re. R is resistance; Re is the Reynolds number, a pure number with no units. Two examples: Anaemia: thinner blood (lower viscosity) and a faster circulation can cause a flow murmur with normal valves. A narrowed artery: blood speeds up through the narrow part and becomes turbulent beyond it, causing a bruit. 6. Why is vessel radius such a powerful determinant of flow? The Poiseuille equation describes smooth flow through a straight tube: Hagen–Poiseuille equation Q = π Δ P r 4 8 η L r = vessel radius; L = vessel length; η = viscosity. Ideal tube resistance R = 8 η L π r 4 The key fact: resistance depends on radius to the fourth power (R ∝ 1/r⁴). What does \"to the fourth power\" mean? Multiply the change by itself four times. Halve the radius, and resistance rises by 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 16 times. So with the same pressure difference, flow falls to 1/16. Anyone who has tried to drink through a very thin straw knows the feeling. Radius halved Resistance ×16 At unchanged ΔP: flow falls to 1/16 Radius doubled Resistance ÷16 At unchanged ΔP: flow increases sixteenfold Radius dial A slider sets the vessel's radius from 50% to 150% of normal, and the vessel drawing narrows or widens. It shows the radius change, r⁴ worked out step by step, resistance (×) and flow (% of normal, at the same pressure difference), with flow bars for normal and new. It starts at 100%, and presets mark 80% (flow falls to about 41%) and 50% (about 6%). One line: \"A 20% narrowing more than halves flow.\" Why this matters: arterioles control flow by changing their radius only slightly. A 20% narrowing more than halves flow. This is how the body directs blood to the organs that need it ( §8). Caution: Poiseuille assumes a rigid, straight tube and a simple fluid. Blood vessels stretch and branch, and blood contains cells, so the numbers are a guide, not exact predictions. A fast jet doesn't mean more flow. Blood speeds up through a narrowing (v = Q ÷ A), yet the narrowing raises resistance, so total flow can fall. Speed and flow are different things. Understanding Check: Sections 5–6 1. Which change makes turbulent flow most likely? A. Lower velocity and higher blood viscosity B. Higher velocity and lower blood viscosity C. A narrower vessel with slower flow D. Thicker blood in a smooth, straight vessel Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Re = ρvD ÷ η. Faster flow and thinner blood both raise Re. 2. A vessel's radius is halved, with pressure, length and viscosity unchanged. What happens to flow? A. It halves B. It falls to a quarter C. It falls to an eighth D. It falls to a sixteenth Show answer and explanation Answer: D. R ∝ 1/r⁴. Half the radius gives 2⁴ = 16 times the resistance, so flow falls to 1/16. 3. Why can anaemia cause a heart murmur with normal valves? A. Thinner blood and faster flow make turbulence more likely B. Thicker blood increases friction against the valves C. Low oxygen damages the valve leaflets directly D. The red cells clump together and strike the valves Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Lower viscosity and a higher cardiac output both raise the Reynolds number, so flow across normal valves can become turbulent. 4. Blood speeds up as it passes through a narrowed artery. Has total flow through the artery increased? A. Yes, because faster blood always means more flow B. Yes, because the narrowing lowers resistance C. No: speed rises because the area falls, while flow may drop D. No: blood actually slows down in a narrowing Show answer and explanation Answer: C. v = Q ÷ A, so speed rises where area falls. The narrowing also raises resistance, so total flow can fall. 7. Viscosity, haematocrit and blood cells Viscosity is how thick or sticky a fluid is: honey is more viscous than water. Thicker blood flows less easily, so resistance rises. Blood viscosity depends mainly on the haematocrit, the percentage of blood made up of red cells. It also depends on plasma proteins, temperature and how flexible the red cells are. Polycythaemia (too many red cells): thicker blood → more resistance → more work for the heart. Anaemia (too few red cells): thinner blood, but each mL carries less oxygen, so the heart pumps more blood to compensate. That also means more work, for a different reason. Stiff or misshapen red cells (sickle cell disease, hereditary spherocytosis) flow less easily through small vessels. Source note: One slide suggests viscosity has little effect in small vessels. In very narrow vessels, red cells line up in the centre and blood behaves as if it were thinner. This is a real effect, but it depends on vessel size and does not hold everywhere. 8. Why are arterioles the main controllable resistance vessels? Vessels are arranged in two ways: In series (one after another, like the arteries then arterioles then capillaries): the resistances add up. In parallel (side by side, like the many capillaries in a bed, or the organs off the aorta): each extra path lowers the total resistance, because blood has more routes. This solves a puzzle. Each capillary is narrower than an arteriole, so why aren't capillaries the main resistance? Because there are so many of them in parallel that their combined resistance is lower. The arterioles, with their muscular walls, are where the biggest pressure drop happens and where resistance can be adjusted. In practice: when an organ's arterioles constrict, its blood flow falls; when they dilate, its flow rises. The heart's output is the same, but it is shared out differently. 9. How can blood flow be measured? Method What it detects How it gives flow Electromagnetic flowmeter Voltage produced by blood moving through a magnetic field Voltage rises with flow Doppler ultrasound The change in pitch of sound bouncing off moving red cells Gives velocity; vessel area is needed to get flow Plethysmography Swelling of a limb when venous outflow is briefly blocked The rate of swelling equals the arterial inflow Doppler measures speed, not volume. To get flow, multiply velocity by the vessel's cross-sectional area (Q = vA). The lecture also gives an arm-to-tongue circulation time of about 15 seconds: a substance injected into an arm vein is tasted on the tongue about 15 s later. 10. Why can veins hold more blood than arteries? Compliance is how much a vessel's volume increases for each rise in pressure: Compliance C = Δ V Δ P Veins are far more compliant than arteries: they balloon out with little pressure. So most of the blood volume sits in the veins (about two-thirds at rest), ready to be called on. Why does tightening the veins help cardiac output? Sympathetic nerves make the veins less stretchy. The stored blood is squeezed towards the heart, so venous return, EDV and, through Frank–Starling, stroke volume all rise. ( → Cardiac Output, §4) Understanding Check: Sections 7–10 1. How do polycythaemia and anaemia both increase the heart's work? A. Both make the blood thicker and harder to pump B. Polycythaemia thickens blood; anaemia makes the heart pump more blood C. Both lower blood pressure, so the heart beats faster D. Polycythaemia thins blood; anaemia thickens it Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Thicker blood raises resistance. Anaemic blood is thinner, but carries less oxygen, so cardiac output must rise. 2. Capillaries are narrower than arterioles. Why are arterioles still the main resistance vessels? A. Capillaries have no walls, so they offer no resistance B. Arterioles are longer than all the capillaries put together C. Capillaries are in parallel in huge numbers, and arterioles can adjust their radius D. Blood flows faster through capillaries than through arterioles Show answer and explanation Answer: C. So many parallel capillaries share the flow that their total resistance is low. Arterioles have muscle to change their radius. 3. What does Doppler ultrasound measure directly? A. The volume of blood flowing per minute B. The pressure difference along the vessel C. The speed of the moving blood cells D. The viscosity of the blood Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Doppler measures velocity from the pitch change. Flow needs the vessel's area as well (Q = vA). 4. Sympathetic nerves tighten the veins. What happens to stroke volume? A. It rises, because more blood returns to fill the heart B. It falls, because resistance to flow increases C. It is unchanged, because veins don't affect the heart D. It falls, because the heart beats faster Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Less stretchy veins push stored blood to the heart. More filling means more stroke volume (Frank–Starling). 11. Chapter Essentials Flow follows the pressure difference: Q = ΔP ÷ R. High pressure alone doesn't mean high flow. Elastic arteries recoil in diastole and keep blood moving between beats. Vessel roles: elastic arteries (recoil), arterioles (resistance), capillaries (exchange), veins (capacity). Velocity ≠ flow: v = Q ÷ A. The capillaries' huge total area makes blood slowest there. Laminar flow is silent and fastest at the centre. Turbulent flow is noisy (murmurs, bruits). Reynolds number Re = ρvD ÷ η. High velocity, wide vessels and thin blood favour turbulence. Re is not resistance. R ∝ 1/r⁴: halve the radius, and resistance rises 16-fold. A 20% narrowing more than halves flow. Viscosity rises with haematocrit. Polycythaemia raises cardiac work because thicker blood raises resistance; anaemia raises it because each mL carries less oxygen, so the heart must pump more blood. Arterioles are the adjustable resistance. Parallel paths lower total resistance; series resistances add. Doppler measures velocity; flow = velocity × area. Compliance C = ΔV ÷ ΔP. Veins hold most of the blood, and tightening them raises venous return and stroke volume.","blood-pressure":"Arterial Blood Pressure 1. What exactly is arterial blood pressure? Blood pressure is the push of blood against the artery walls. It rises and falls with every heartbeat, so we report two numbers: What it is Systolic (SBP) The highest pressure, reached as the ventricle ejects Diastolic (DBP) The lowest pressure, just before the next ejection 120/70 mmHg means systolic 120, diastolic 70. These are the lecture's example values. Diagnosing high blood pressure uses clinical guidelines, not one fixed number. Why does pressure swing up and down? Each ejection pushes extra blood into the arteries, so pressure rises. Between beats, blood keeps draining into the small vessels, so pressure falls until the next beat. 2. Why doesn't blood stop moving during diastole? In diastole the aortic valve is shut and no blood is being ejected, yet blood keeps flowing through the arteries. The reason is the Windkessel effect (German for \"air chamber\", after the air-filled chamber old fire pumps used to smooth out their spurts): Systole: blood is ejected faster than it can drain away, so the elastic aorta stretches, storing some of the energy. Diastole: the aorta springs back, pushing the stored blood onwards. Figure 1. Windkessel mechanism — elastic arterial energy storage and recoil Systole: aortic valve open LV Blood is ejected faster than it can run off, so the aorta stretches and stores energy in its elastic wall. Diastole: aortic valve shut LV No blood leaves the heart, but the wall recoils and keeps pushing blood onwards, so flow continues and pressure falls slowly. Original schematic, not to scale. Elastic recoil turns the heart's pulsed output into a steadier flow through the tissues. So flow is smooth and continuous in the tissues even though the heart pumps in spurts. Pressure still falls in diastole; the recoil just slows the fall. A stiff aorta can't stretch much. The same stroke volume then pushes systolic pressure higher, and with less recoil, diastolic pressure falls lower. That is why older people often have high systolic pressure with a wide pulse pressure. ( → Pulses and JVP, §1) 3. Why do systolic and diastolic pressure depend on more than heart rate? Blood pressure is set by how much blood the heart pumps and how hard it is for that blood to drain away: Mean pressure and flow MAP − CVP ≈ CO × SVR MAP = mean arterial pressure; CVP = central venous pressure; SVR = systemic vascular resistance. Because CVP is near zero, this is usually written MAP ≈ CO × SVR (systemic vascular resistance). It is the same rule as Q = ΔP ÷ R, rearranged. ( → Haemodynamics, §2) So pressure rises if cardiac output rises or if the arterioles tighten (SVR rises). Heart rate alone doesn't tell you what BP will do. Blood pressure is not the same as blood flow. After a bleed, the arterioles tighten hard (high SVR), which can hold BP near normal even while cardiac output, and tissue blood flow, are falling. A normal BP doesn't prove the tissues are getting enough blood. ( → Shock) Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why does blood keep flowing through the arteries in diastole, with the aortic valve shut? A. The ventricle keeps ejecting a small amount in diastole B. The aorta stretched in systole recoils and pushes blood onwards C. The veins draw blood out of the arteries by suction D. The aortic valve leaks slightly in every healthy heart Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The Windkessel effect: energy stored in the stretched aortic wall is released as it springs back. 2. An older person's aorta has become stiff. What happens to their pulse pressure? A. It widens, because systolic rises and diastolic falls B. It narrows, because the aorta can't stretch C. It stays the same, because stroke volume is unchanged D. It falls to zero, because there is no recoil Show answer and explanation Answer: A. A stiff aorta can't absorb the stroke volume, so systolic rises. With less recoil, diastolic falls. The gap between them widens. 3. A patient who has lost a lot of blood has a BP of 110/80 but cold, poorly perfused hands. How can BP be near normal? A. Blood loss raises cardiac output, keeping pressure up B. Arterioles tighten (SVR rises), holding pressure up as flow falls C. The heart pumps more blood with each beat after a bleed D. Pressure and flow always change together, so the flow must be normal Show answer and explanation Answer: B. MAP ≈ CO × SVR. A large rise in SVR can hold MAP up even while CO, and tissue flow, falls. 4. What is pulse pressure? Pulse pressure is the difference between the two numbers: Pulse pressure PP = SBP − DBP For 120/70 mmHg: PP = 50 mmHg. It is the size of the pressure rise with each beat, and it is what you feel as the strength of the pulse. ( → Pulses and JVP, §2) Two things widen it: A bigger stroke volume: more blood pushed in per beat. A stiffer aorta: less stretch, so the same volume raises pressure more. So a wide pulse pressure doesn't by itself prove a big stroke volume. It may be a stiff aorta. 5. What is mean arterial pressure, and why isn't it simply the midpoint? Mean arterial pressure (MAP) is the average pressure over a whole beat. It is the pressure that actually drives blood into the tissues. Why isn't it halfway between 120 and 70? At a resting heart rate, the heart spends about one-third of each beat in systole and two-thirds in diastole. The pressure sits near diastolic for longer, so the average is pulled towards it. Approximate mean arterial pressure MAP ≈ DBP + 1 3 ( SBP − DBP) At ordinary resting heart rates: MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓ pulse pressure. For 120/70: pulse pressure = 50, and ⅓ × 50 ≈ 17, so MAP ≈ 70 + 17 = about 87 mmHg, lower than the midpoint of 95. This is an estimate. At fast heart rates diastole shortens, the beat is closer to half systole and half diastole, and the ⅓ rule becomes less accurate. 6. Why does measured BP depend on whether the arm is above or below the heart? Blood is a column of fluid, so gravity adds pressure below the heart and takes it away above. For blood, the change is about 0.77 mmHg per centimetre of height. ( → Gravity) Hydrostatic pressure change Δ P = ρ g h ρ = fluid density; g = gravitational acceleration; h = vertical height difference. Arm 20 cm below heart About +15–16 mmHg Hydrostatic contribution makes the reading higher Arm 20 cm above heart About −15–16 mmHg Hydrostatic contribution makes the reading lower Rule: measure with the cuff at heart level. Rest the arm on a support: if the patient holds it up with their own muscles, the effort itself raises BP. Understanding Check: Sections 4–6 1. A BP is 130/70 mmHg. What is the approximate mean arterial pressure? A. 60 mmHg, from 130 − 70 B. 90 mmHg, from 70 + ⅓ × 60 C. 100 mmHg, from the midpoint of 130 and 70 D. 110 mmHg, from 130 − ⅓ × 60 Show answer and explanation Answer: B. PP = 60 mmHg; MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓PP = 70 + 20 = 90 mmHg. 2. Why is MAP closer to diastolic than to systolic pressure at rest? A. Diastolic pressure is the more important number clinically B. The heart spends about two-thirds of each beat in diastole C. Systolic pressure is measured less accurately D. The aorta's recoil raises diastolic pressure to match systolic Show answer and explanation Answer: B. MAP is a time average. More time is spent at the lower, diastolic pressure, so the average is pulled towards it. 3. The cuff is on an arm hanging 20 cm below heart level. How will the reading be affected? A. About 15 mmHg too low B. Unchanged, because gravity doesn't affect arteries C. About 15 mmHg too high D. About 77 mmHg too high Show answer and explanation Answer: C. About 0.77 mmHg per cm × 20 cm ≈ 15 mmHg added by gravity below the heart. 4. Two people have the same stroke volume, but one has a wider pulse pressure. What is the most likely reason? A. Their aorta is stiffer B. Their heart rate is slower C. Their arterioles are more dilated D. Their blood is thinner Show answer and explanation Answer: A. With SV the same, a stiffer aorta stretches less, so the same volume raises pressure more and the pulse pressure widens. 7. How does a cuff and stethoscope reveal systolic and diastolic pressure? The cuff squeezes the brachial artery in the upper arm. A stethoscope over the artery, just below the cuff, listens for Korotkoff sounds: taps made by blood forcing its way through the squeezed artery. The whole method is a contest: is the cuff squeezing harder than the blood is pushing? 1. Cuff above systolic (say 150; BP 120/70). The cuff squeezes harder than even the peak of each beat. The artery stays shut. No blood gets through, so there is silence. 2. Cuff falls just below systolic (119). At the peak of each beat, blood pressure briefly beats the cuff. A spurt of blood forces the artery open and makes a tap. The first tap (Korotkoff phase I) = systolic pressure. 3. Cuff between systolic and diastolic (100). Each beat forces the artery open for part of the beat, and it is squeezed shut again for the rest. The taps continue and may change in quality as the cuff falls. 4. Cuff falls below diastolic (69). Now even the lowest pressure in each beat is higher than the cuff. The artery stays open all the time, flow is smooth again, and the sounds stop. Silence (Korotkoff phase V) = diastolic pressure in adults. In some situations, such as pregnancy or children, the point where the sounds muffle (phase IV) is used instead. Cuff pressure The artery under the cuff What you hear Above systolic Shut all the time Silence Just below systolic Forced open at each peak First tap = systolic Between the two Opens and closes with each beat Taps Below diastolic Open all the time Silence = diastolic Cuff simulator A patient with BP 120/70 at 70 beats/min. Press Deflate, or drag the cuff gauge, to lower cuff pressure from 160 to 50 mmHg at about 2–3 mmHg per beat. A drawing shows the artery under the cuff shut, spurting open with each beat, or open. Taps play in time with each beat only between 120 and 70 (sound can be switched off). Markers drop at the first tap (\"systolic: 120\") and the last (\"diastolic: 70\"), with one line underneath saying which of the four stages you are in. These are not the heart sounds. Korotkoff sounds are made in the squeezed brachial artery, not by heart valves. ( → Heart Sounds, §1) 8. How should BP actually be measured accurately? Rest first: about five minutes, seated, back supported, feet flat, not talking. Right cuff, right place: the correct cuff size on a bare upper arm, supported at heart level. Feel first: inflate while feeling the radial pulse. Note where it disappears; that is roughly the systolic pressure. Then listen: inflate about 20–30 mmHg above that point. Deflate slowly, about 2 mmHg per second, listening over the brachial artery. Record: the first tap (systolic) and the point where the sounds disappear (diastolic). Why feel first? In some people the taps fade out for a while between systolic and diastolic (an auscultatory gap). If you only listened, you might start below the real systolic pressure and miss it. Feeling alone gives systolic only. The radial pulse returns when the cuff falls below systolic, but nothing you can feel marks diastolic. Common errors: Error Reading will be Cuff too small for the arm Falsely high: a narrow cuff needs extra pressure to squeeze the artery shut Cuff too large Falsely low Arm below heart level Falsely high Arm above heart level Falsely low Talking, arm unsupported, just exercised Usually falsely high Cuff size goes by arm circumference: large arms need large cuffs, and children need child-sized cuffs. Automatic machines (oscillometric monitors) don't listen for Korotkoff sounds. They sense pressure wobbles in the cuff and calculate the BP. Source note: The lecture covers auscultation, palpation, direct arterial measurement and cuff size. Resting time, deflation rate and the auscultatory gap are standard clinical guidance (American Heart Association). 9. Why does pressure decrease as blood travels through the circulation? Pressure is used up as blood pushes through resistance, just as Q = ΔP ÷ R predicts. The biggest drop is across the arterioles, the main resistance vessels. ( → Haemodynamics, §8) Aorta / arteries High pressure · strong pulses → Arterioles Large pressure drop → Capillaries / veins Lower pressure · damped pulses The pulse also fades along the way. The elastic arteries and the arteriolar resistance smooth out the beat-to-beat swings, so blood reaches the capillaries at a low, steady pressure. That protects the thin capillary walls. Because the arterioles hold back so much pressure, tightening them does two things at once: it lowers flow to the tissue beyond and raises pressure in the arteries before them. Understanding Check: Sections 7–9 1. As the cuff deflates, what causes the first Korotkoff tap? A. The aortic valve closing at the end of systole B. A spurt of blood forcing open the squeezed artery at the peak of the beat C. The cuff releasing air through its valve D. Blood flowing smoothly through a fully open artery Show answer and explanation Answer: B. When cuff pressure falls just below systolic, each pressure peak forces the artery open, and the turbulent spurt makes a tap. 2. Why do the sounds disappear once the cuff is below diastolic pressure? A. The artery is shut all the time, so no blood flows B. The heart's valves stop making noise C. The artery stays open all the time, so flow is smooth again D. The stethoscope can't hear pressures that low Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Below diastolic, even the lowest pressure in each beat holds the artery open, so flow is no longer squeezed and turbulent. 3. A large man's BP is taken with a standard cuff that is too small for his arm. How will the reading be affected? A. Falsely high, because extra pressure is needed to squeeze the artery shut B. Falsely low, because the narrow cuff can't occlude the artery C. Accurate, because cuff size only matters for children D. Accurate for systolic but falsely low for diastolic Show answer and explanation Answer: A. A narrow cuff passes its pressure poorly to the artery, so a higher cuff pressure is needed to close it, and both readings come out too high. 4. Why can feeling the radial pulse give systolic but not diastolic pressure? A. The radial pulse disappears completely at diastolic pressure B. The pulse returns at systolic, but nothing you can feel marks diastolic C. The radial artery is too small to carry a pulse below systolic D. Diastolic pressure can only be measured inside the artery Show answer and explanation Answer: B. As the cuff falls below systolic, the pulse comes back, but it simply stays felt. There is no event at diastolic that you can feel. 5. Where does mean blood pressure fall the most? A. Across the aorta and large arteries B. Across the arterioles C. Across the capillaries D. Across the veins Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The arterioles are the main resistance vessels, so most of the pressure is used up there. This also smooths out the pulse before blood reaches the capillaries. 10. Chapter Essentials Systolic = the peak pressure in a beat; diastolic = the lowest, just before the next beat. Windkessel: the aorta stretches in systole and recoils in diastole, keeping blood moving between beats. A stiff aorta raises systolic and widens pulse pressure. MAP ≈ CO × SVR. BP depends on output and resistance, not heart rate alone. Normal BP ≠ normal flow. Pulse pressure = SBP − DBP. It widens with a bigger stroke volume or a stiffer aorta. MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓ pulse pressure (120/70 → about 87), closer to diastolic because diastole lasts longer. Gravity: about 0.77 mmHg per cm. Measure with the cuff at heart level. Korotkoff: silence (cuff above systolic) → first tap = systolic → taps → silence = diastolic. The taps come from blood forcing through the squeezed brachial artery, not from the heart valves. Feel, then listen: palpation gives systolic only and helps avoid the auscultatory gap. Cuff too small → reads high; too large → reads low. Arm below the heart → reads high; above → reads low. Pressure falls most across the arterioles, which also smooth out the pulse.","pulses":"Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse 1. What is an arterial pulse? Each time the left ventricle ejects, it pushes a fresh volume of blood into an aorta that is already full. The aortic wall stretches and the pressure inside it rises. That stretch and pressure rise travel down the arteries as a wave, like a ripple running along a tight hose. When the wave passes under your finger at the wrist, the artery wall bulges briefly: that bulge is the pulse. LV ejection → Aortic pressure ↑ → Arterial wall expands → Pressure wave travels The pulse is the wave, not the blood. As the lecture stresses, the pulse does not depend on how fast the blood is moving. The wave races ahead: about 3–5 m/s in the aorta, and faster further out. It reaches the wrist in roughly a tenth of a second. The blood itself moves far more slowly, well under 1 m/s on average in the aorta. So when you feel the pulse, the blood that left the heart in that beat is still nowhere near your wrist. Why does the wave speed up? A stiff tube passes a pressure wave on faster than a stretchy one, because a stiff wall barely gives, so the pressure change is handed on quickly. The lecture gives two examples: The wave gets faster further from the heart. The smaller arteries are stiffer than the stretchy aorta. The wave gets faster with age. The arteries become more rigid. In both cases it is the wave that speeds up, not the blood. Source note: Wave speeds are approximate textbook values (Guyton and Hall): about 3–5 m/s in the aorta, 7–10 m/s in large branches, and faster in small arteries. 2. What do we assess when palpating a pulse? The lecture lists five features: Feature What you check What it tells you Rate Beats per minute How many beats are reaching the arteries Volume How big the bulge under your finger is Mainly the pulse pressure: the rise from diastolic to systolic pressure, which grows with stroke volume Strength How firmly it pushes against your finger How much pressure is in the artery Character The shape of each beat: how fast it rises and falls How the left ventricle ejects, and how the aortic valve is working Location Which artery (radial, carotid, femoral…), and whether both sides match A weak or missing pulse on one side points to a blockage on that side Whether the rhythm is regular is also worth noting. Why can the wrist count fewer beats than the heart makes? In a very fast or irregular rhythm, some beats come so soon that the ventricle has hardly filled. Those beats eject too little blood to make a pulse you can feel. The pulse at the wrist can then be slower than the heart rate counted with a stethoscope. This difference is a pulse deficit. 3. Why is the jugular venous pulse visible in the neck? There is no valve between the right atrium and the large veins of the neck. So, as the lecture explains, pressure changes in the right atrium pass straight up into the neck veins, and you can see them. The internal jugular vein acts like a gauge attached to the right atrium: the higher the right atrial pressure, the higher up the neck the column of blood reaches. Pressure moves; the blood doesn't have to. A pressure change travels up the column of blood even while the blood itself keeps flowing down towards the heart. Why can you see it but not feel it? Venous pressure is only a few mmHg, far too low to push against a finger. Even light pressure squashes the vein flat. What you see is a soft, flickering movement of the skin. The lecture notes that the JVP is not palpable. Breathing changes it. Breathing in lowers the pressure inside the chest and draws blood into the heart, so the level in the neck falls on inspiration. Key distinction: the arterial pulse is the left ventricle's ejection wave. The JVP shows the right atrium's pressure changes. OPTIONAL VIDEO Understanding Jugular Venous Pressure (JVP) Zero to Finals. Starts at 0:23. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © Zero to Finals. Shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. You feel the radial pulse about a tenth of a second after the left ventricle ejects. What has travelled to the wrist in that time? A. The blood ejected in that beat, moving at about 8 m/s B. A pressure wave travelling along the artery walls C. A nerve signal from the heart to the arterial muscle D. Sound from the closing aortic valve, carried by the blood Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The ejection starts a pressure and stretch wave that travels along the arteries far faster than the blood itself moves. You feel the wave, not the blood. 2. Why does the pulse wave travel faster in an elderly person? A. Their blood is thicker, so it carries pressure better B. Their heart ejects blood at a higher speed C. Their stiffer arteries pass the pressure change on faster D. Their arteries are wider, so blood meets less resistance Show answer and explanation Answer: C. A stiff wall hardly stretches, so it hands the pressure change on quickly. Arteries stiffen with age, so the wave speeds up, even though the blood does not. 3. Why can the JVP be seen but not felt? A. Venous pressure is too low to push against a finger B. The jugular vein lies too deep to be touched C. The JVP only appears when the patient breathes in D. Venous blood flows away from the heart in the neck Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Pressure in the neck veins is only a few mmHg. Any pressure from a finger squashes the vein, so you can only see the movement. 4. Why are the a, c and v waves separated by x and y descents? In one heartbeat, the pressure in the right atrium rises three times (the a, c and v waves) and falls twice (the x and y descents). Each one follows from what the right atrium and the tricuspid valve are doing. They are the same atrial waves you meet on the Wiggers diagram. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §9) In order through one beat: a wave: the right atrium contracts. At the end of ventricular diastole, just after the P wave, the atrium squeezes and its pressure rises. There is no valve between the atrium and the great veins, so, as the lecture notes, venous inflow briefly stops and a little blood is pushed back up the veins. c wave: the closed tricuspid valve bulges back. As the right ventricle starts contracting, its rising pressure pushes the closed tricuspid valve so it bulges back into the atrium. The atrium itself is not contracting. x descent: the atrium relaxes and is stretched open. The atrium relaxes after its contraction. As the right ventricle ejects, it also pulls the tricuspid valve ring downwards, making the atrium bigger. Pressure falls. v wave: the atrium fills with the tricuspid valve shut. Blood keeps returning from the body throughout ventricular systole. With the tricuspid valve shut, it can only collect in the atrium, so pressure rises slowly. The atrium is not contracting. y descent: the tricuspid valve opens. When right ventricular pressure falls below right atrial pressure, the valve opens and the stored blood rushes into the ventricle. Atrial pressure drops. Wave What causes it When in the cycle a The atrium contracts End of ventricular diastole c The closed tricuspid valve bulges back Start of ventricular contraction x The atrium relaxes, and the valve ring is pulled down Ventricular ejection v The atrium fills with the tricuspid valve shut Late ventricular systole y The tricuspid valve opens and the atrium empties Early ventricular diastole A memory hook: a for a trial contraction, c for the c losed valve bulging, v for v enous filling. JVP waves at a glance Right atrial pressure a c x v y time → a right atrium contracts c closed tricuspid valve bulges back x atrium relaxes and is stretched v atrium fills, tricuspid shut y tricuspid opens, atrium empties Original teaching schematic (not a patient tracing). The a, c and v are pressure rises; x and y are falls. Timing and actual prominence vary between patients. JVP wave explorer Replaces the static curve. It shows one JVP trace, with the P wave, QRS, S1 and S2 marked above it, and a small right heart beside it. Next/Back steps through a → c → x → v → y. At each step the matching part of the trace is highlighted, and the heart shows what is happening: the atrium contracting, relaxing or filling; the tricuspid valve shut, bulging or opening; blood moving or collecting. One plain sentence underneath matches the bullet above. Schematic, not a patient tracing. OPTIONAL VIDEO JVP waveform explained Catherine Tsai, UCSF School of Medicine. An animated walk through the JVP waveform. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © Catherine Tsai. Written, illustrated, animated and voiced by Catherine Tsai, UCSF School of Medicine, Spring 2015, during her Medicine Sub-Internship at the SF VA Hospital. Shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video. OPTIONAL VIDEO Cardiac Cycle | Atrial Pressure | Part 3 | Jugular Venous Pulse | JVP | Cardiac Physiology Byte Size Med. How the atrial pressure changes of the cardiac cycle produce the jugular venous pulse. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © Byte Size Med. Shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video. 5. How do you distinguish the JVP from the carotid pulse? Both pulsations sit side by side in the neck, so they are easy to confuse. Jugular venous pulse Carotid arterial pulse Can you feel it? No: pressure is too low Yes: a firm thrust What it looks like A soft flicker with more than one movement per beat One sharp outward thrust per beat When the patient sits up The level falls No change On breathing in The level falls No regular change Light pressure at the base of the neck It disappears (the vein is squashed) It continues What it shows Right atrial pressure Left ventricular ejection Confusing the two leads to wrong conclusions about right atrial pressure. Source note: The lecture states that the JVP is not palpable and falls during inspiration. The other bedside differences are standard clinical teaching. Understanding Check: Sections 4–5 1. Which JVP wave appears while the right atrium is filling and the tricuspid valve is shut? A. The a wave B. The c wave C. The v wave D. The y descent Show answer and explanation Answer: C. During ventricular systole the tricuspid valve is shut, so returning blood collects in the atrium and its pressure rises slowly: the v wave. The atrium is not contracting. 2. What causes the c wave? A. The right atrium contracting at the end of diastole B. The shut tricuspid valve bulging back into the atrium C. Blood rushing into the ventricle as the valve opens D. The atrium filling while the ventricle ejects Show answer and explanation Answer: B. As the right ventricle starts contracting, its rising pressure pushes the closed tricuspid valve back towards the atrium, giving a small rise in atrial pressure. A is the a wave, C the y descent, and D the v wave. 3. At what moment does the y descent begin? A. When the pulmonary valve opens B. When the right atrium contracts C. When the tricuspid valve closes D. When the tricuspid valve opens Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Once the tricuspid valve opens, the blood stored in the atrium pours into the ventricle and atrial pressure falls. 4. A neck pulsation can be felt easily, shows one outward thrust per beat, and doesn't change when the patient sits up. What is it? A. The carotid pulse, from the left ventricle's ejection B. The JVP, raised high by right-sided heart failure C. A giant v wave from tricuspid regurgitation D. The JVP, seen with the patient lying unusually flat Show answer and explanation Answer: A. A pulsation you can feel, with a single thrust and no change with position, is arterial. The JVP cannot be felt, has more than one movement per beat, and changes with posture and breathing. 6. How is the JVP estimated at the bedside? The lecture's method: Recline the patient at about 45°, with the head turned slightly to the left, and look at the right internal jugular vein. Adjust the angle until you can see the top of the pulsating column. Sit the patient more upright if the neck veins are very full, or lie them flatter if the column is low. Find the sternal angle, the ridge where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum, level with the second rib. Measure the vertical height from the sternal angle up to the top of the column. Why measure vertically? The pressure in a column of fluid depends only on its vertical height, not on how long or slanted it is. Why from the sternal angle? It sits about 5 cm above the middle of the right atrium in most positions, so it makes a fixed reference point. Normal: the top of the column is no more than about 3–4 cm above the sternal angle. Higher than that means raised right atrial pressure. Figure 1. Measuring the JVP at the bedside. (1) The pointer is held level with the top of the venous column. (2) A ruler stands vertically on the sternal angle. (3) Where the level pointer crosses the ruler gives the JVP height in cm. Image credit Bedside photograph supplied by the DOMiNANCE Breakdowns project; numbered labels added. Source note: The lecture gives the 45° position, sitting upright if very engorged, and measuring from the sternum. The normal value (no more than about 3–4 cm above the sternal angle) and the 5 cm offset are standard clinical teaching; the offset varies between people. OPTIONAL VIDEO Jugular Venous Pressure (JVP) Examination - Clinical Skills - Dr Gill Dr James Gill. Examining the JVP at the bedside: internal versus external jugular veins, interpreting the height, and the hepatojugular reflux test. Starts at 8:01. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © Dr James Gill. Shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video. 7. Which abnormal JVP waves are most important? The lecture links three conditions to the JVP: Right-sided heart failure: a raised JVP. The right heart cannot pump on all the blood returning to it. Pressure backs up into the right atrium and the neck veins, so the column sits high. Tricuspid regurgitation: a large wave during systole. The leaky tricuspid valve lets the contracting right ventricle push blood back into the atrium, on top of the normal venous filling. The rise during systole becomes large. The lecture calls it a \"big c wave \"; because it joins the c and v waves, it is usually described as a giant v (or cv) wave. Complete heart block: giant (\"cannon\") a waves. The atria and ventricles beat independently. Sometimes the atrium contracts while the tricuspid valve is shut, because the ventricle is contracting at the same moment. The blood cannot go forward, so the pressure spikes up the neck. This only happens on the beats where the timing collides, so the giant a waves come and go. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §8) Figure 2. A very high JVP. Grossly distended neck veins in a patient lying almost flat. Right atrial pressure is so high that the venous column reaches the angle of the jaw. (Cause in this patient: cardiac tamponade, where fluid around the heart stops it filling.) Image credit Photograph: Ferencga, Wikimedia Commons. Key distinction: a giant a wave is the atrium contracting against a shut valve. A giant v wave is the ventricle pushing blood back through a leaky valve. Source note: The lecture describes a \"big C wave\" in tricuspid incompetence. In practice, the regurgitant wave merges the c and v waves and is usually called a giant v or cv wave. Understanding Check 1. Why is the JVP height measured vertically from the sternal angle? A. The sternal angle lies directly over the tricuspid valve B. The jugular vein runs straight up from the sternal angle C. Pressure in a column of fluid depends on its vertical height D. Measuring along the neck would include the carotid pulse Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Only vertical height sets the pressure in a fluid column. The sternal angle is used because it sits a fairly constant distance above the right atrium. 2. A patient's JVP is 8 cm above the sternal angle, and their ankles are swollen. What does this most suggest? A. High right atrial pressure from right-sided heart failure B. Low right atrial pressure from blood loss C. A stiff aorta raising the speed of the pulse wave D. Tricuspid stenosis reducing the return of venous blood Show answer and explanation Answer: A. A column well above 3–4 cm means raised right atrial pressure. With swollen ankles, this fits a failing right heart that cannot pump on the blood returning to it. 3. In complete heart block, giant \"a\" waves appear in the neck only now and then. Why only sometimes? A. The atria contract only when the ventricles are relaxed B. Only some atrial beats fall while the tricuspid valve is shut C. The vagus nerve blocks every other atrial contraction D. Giant a waves appear only during deep inspiration Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The atria and ventricles beat independently, so only now and then does an atrial contraction land while the ventricle is contracting and the tricuspid valve is shut. Those beats give the giant waves. 4. How does the giant wave of tricuspid regurgitation differ from a giant (\"cannon\") a wave? A. It comes from the atrium contracting against a shut valve B. It appears only in diastole, while the tricuspid valve is open C. It can be felt as an outward thrust, like the carotid pulse D. It comes from the ventricle pushing blood back through a leaky valve Show answer and explanation Answer: D. In tricuspid regurgitation the ventricle drives blood back into the atrium during systole. A cannon a wave is the atrium itself contracting against a shut valve. 8. Chapter Essentials The arterial pulse is a pressure wave, started by left ventricular ejection. It travels far faster than the blood itself. Stiffer arteries carry the wave faster: further from the heart, and with age. Pulse features (lecture): rate, volume, strength, character and location, plus rhythm. A pulse deficit means some beats are too weak to reach the wrist. The JVP shows right atrial pressure, because no valve separates the right atrium from the neck veins. It can be seen but not felt, and it falls on inspiration. a = the atrium contracts; c = the closed tricuspid valve bulges back; x = the atrium relaxes and the valve ring is pulled down; v = the atrium fills with the valve shut; y = the valve opens and the atrium empties. JVP versus carotid: the JVP is not palpable, flickers more than once per beat, and changes with position, breathing and light pressure. The carotid is a single palpable thrust. Measurement: recline at about 45° (adjust as needed) and measure the vertical height above the sternal angle. Normal is no more than about 3–4 cm. A raised JVP means high right atrial pressure, as in right-sided heart failure. Giant v (cv) wave: tricuspid regurgitation, the ventricle pushing blood back. Giant (cannon) a wave: complete heart block, the atrium contracting against a shut tricuspid valve.","venous-return":"Veins and Venous Return 1. What is venous return, and why is it essential? Venous return is the volume of blood flowing back into the right atrium each minute. Over time it must equal cardiac output: the heart can only pump out what comes back to it. ( → Cardiac Output, §1) The heart can't pump blood it doesn't receive. Even a strong heart ejects little if it is poorly filled. More venous return fills the ventricles more, and through Frank–Starling they eject more. ( → Cardiac Output, §4) Venous return ↑ → Right-heart filling ↑ → EDV ↑ → Stroke volume ↑ Why does the left side respond a few beats later? Extra returning blood enters the right heart first, then has to pass through the lungs before it reaches the left ventricle. 2. Why are veins called capacitance vessels? Veins have thin, stretchy walls. They can hold a lot of blood without much rise in pressure, like a half-filled balloon that takes more air easily. Arteries are stiffer, so the same added volume raises their pressure sharply. Vascular compliance C = Δ V Δ P The higher C of veins permits greater volume storage for a given pressure rise. So the veins are the body's blood reservoir: about half or more of the blood volume sits in the systemic veins at rest. ( → Haemodynamics, §10) Venous tone: squeezing the reservoir. Sympathetic nerves can tighten the smooth muscle in vein walls ( venoconstriction). The veins then hold less, so stored blood is pushed towards the heart, without adding any new blood. This is how the body quickly tops up venous return after a bleed or on standing. This is different from tightening arterioles. Arterioles control resistance and so flow into organs. Veins mainly control how much blood is stored and how much is sent back to fill the heart. 3. What pressure actually drives venous return? Blood flows back because pressure in the veins is higher than pressure in the right atrium. Venous return depends on the difference: Venous-return relation VR = P msf − P RA R VR Pmsf = mean systemic filling pressure; PRA = right atrial pressure; RVR = resistance to venous return. Pmsf (mean systemic filling pressure): how \"full\" the circulation is: the pressure that would exist everywhere if the heart stopped and pressures evened out. It rises with more blood volume or tighter veins. PRA (right atrial pressure): the pressure the returning blood flows into. RVR: the resistance of the veins on the way back. Picture a sink draining into a bucket. The flow depends on how much higher the sink's water level is than the bucket's. If the bucket fills up (right atrial pressure rises), flow slows, even with the sink unchanged. So: Venous return rises with more blood volume or venoconstriction (Pmsf ↑). Venous return falls if right atrial pressure rises, as when a failing right ventricle can't empty. There's a limit. Lowering right atrial pressure helps only so far. Once it falls below zero, the large veins entering the chest collapse and stop extra blood getting through. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why must venous return equal cardiac output over time? A. The heart can only pump out the blood that comes back to it B. Veins and arteries always have the same pressure C. The right atrium stores any extra blood indefinitely D. Valves in the veins measure the blood flow Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The circulation is a closed loop, so any lasting difference would pile blood up in one place. 2. Sympathetic nerves tighten the veins, with no change in blood volume. What happens to venous return? A. It falls, because veins now resist flow B. It rises, because stored blood is pushed towards the heart C. It is unchanged, because no blood has been added D. It falls, because right atrial pressure drops Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Tighter veins hold less, so mean systemic filling pressure rises and more blood returns. 3. A failing right ventricle can't empty, so right atrial pressure rises. What happens to venous return? A. It rises, because the atrium pulls harder B. It falls, because the pressure difference driving it shrinks C. It is unchanged, because Pmsf is the same D. It rises, because the veins tighten in response Show answer and explanation Answer: B. VR depends on Pmsf − PRA. A higher PRA leaves a smaller difference, so less blood flows back. 4. How do the skeletal-muscle pump and venous valves work? The deep veins of the legs run between muscles and contain one-way valves that open only towards the heart. Muscle contracts: it squeezes the vein. The blood can only go up, because the valve below snaps shut and the valve above is pushed open. Muscle relaxes: the squeezed section expands and refills from below. The valve above shuts, so blood that has moved up doesn't fall back. Each contraction pumps blood a step closer to the heart. Walking is a pump. The muscle pump at a glance To the heart ↑ Calf muscle Calf muscle Muscle squeezes the vein: blood is forced up through the open upper valve. The lower valve shuts, so blood cannot go back down. Illustration of the principle, not a scale drawing. Valves limit backward flow when muscle pressure is released. Muscle pump Shown with the original static diagram behind the Diagram | Interactive switch. A leg vein between two calf muscles, with a valve above and below. Squeeze shows the muscles pressing in, the lower valve shut, the upper valve open and blood moving up. Relax shows the muscles easing, the lower valve open (refilling from below) and the upper valve shut (no backflow). Play alternates them. A Stand still toggle stops the pump and shows blood pooling in a swollen lower vein, with the line \"Less blood returns → less filling → lower stroke volume\". Why do soldiers faint on parade? Standing still, gravity pools blood in the leg veins and the muscle pump isn't working. Less blood returns, so filling and stroke volume fall, blood pressure drops and the brain gets less blood. Gently tensing the calves or rising on the toes keeps the pump going. ( → Gravity) 5. How does breathing help venous return? Breathing in does two things at once: The chest expands, so pressure inside the chest falls. The veins in the chest and the right atrium are pulled open, and blood is drawn in from the veins outside the chest. The diaphragm moves down and raises pressure in the abdomen, squeezing blood up from the abdominal veins. Both push blood towards the right heart, so venous return rises during inspiration. This is the respiratory pump. ( → Pulses and JVP, §3) On a ventilator it reverses. A ventilator pushes air in, so chest pressure rises on inspiration and venous return falls. The lecture also notes that pulsing arteries next to veins squeeze them slightly with each beat, helping blood along. Understanding Check: Sections 4–5 1. When the calf muscle contracts, why does blood in the deep vein move up rather than down? A. Gravity pulls blood upwards during muscle contraction B. The valve below shuts and the valve above opens C. The vein's own muscle pumps blood upwards D. The arteries next to it pull blood up by suction Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The one-way valves let squeezed blood leave only towards the heart. 2. A soldier stands perfectly still for an hour and faints. What is the main reason? A. Blood pools in the leg veins with no muscle pump, so filling falls B. Standing raises blood pressure too high for the brain C. The heart muscle tires from beating while upright D. Breathing stops being able to help venous return Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Pooling lowers venous return, EDV, stroke volume and blood pressure, so the brain is underperfused. 3. How does breathing in help blood return to the heart? A. It raises chest pressure and pushes blood into the heart B. It slows the heart, giving more time to fill C. It lowers chest pressure and raises abdominal pressure D. It closes the venous valves in the legs Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Low chest pressure draws blood in, while the diaphragm pushes blood up from the abdomen. 6. What does central venous pressure tell us? Central venous pressure (CVP) is the pressure in the large veins next to the right atrium. It is about the same as right atrial pressure, and it is what the JVP shows at the bedside. ( → Pulses and JVP, §3) It is low, only a few mmHg. The lecture's example is about 4.6 mmHg, but the exact value depends on posture, breathing and how it is measured. CVP is not a measure of blood volume. A high CVP can mean too much fluid, or it can mean the right ventricle is failing and blood is backing up behind it, even while cardiac output is low. CVP has to be read together with the rest of the patient. 7. What happens to veins in the head when standing? Above the heart, gravity lowers venous pressure. In the neck, the veins can fall below atmospheric pressure. Ordinary neck veins simply collapse, which is why they look empty when you stand. The dural venous sinuses inside the skull are different: they are held open by tough dura. If one is cut open above heart level, as in a head wound or neurosurgery with the patient sitting, its below-atmospheric pressure can suck air in. An air bubble then travels to the heart and lungs: a dangerous air embolism. Understanding Check: Sections 6–7 1. A patient has a high CVP. Which conclusion is safest? A. They definitely have too much fluid in their circulation B. Their cardiac output must be high C. It may be fluid overload or a failing right ventricle D. Their left ventricle must be failing alone Show answer and explanation Answer: C. CVP reflects both filling and how well the right ventricle empties. It is not a simple blood-volume gauge. 2. Why can an open dural venous sinus draw in air when the head is above the heart? A. Its pressure is below atmospheric and its walls can't collapse B. Its pressure is high, so it bursts and draws in air C. The brain pumps air into the venous blood D. Breathing out pushes air into the sinus Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Ordinary veins would collapse at below-atmospheric pressure, but the dura holds the sinus open, so air is sucked in. 3. Why do neck veins look empty in someone standing upright? A. Gravity drops their pressure below atmospheric, so they collapse B. All the blood is pumped into the arteries C. The valves in the neck veins close D. Their walls tighten and squeeze them shut Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Above the heart, venous pressure falls. Thin neck veins collapse rather than stay open. 8. Chapter Essentials Venous return = blood returning to the right atrium per minute. It equals cardiac output over time. No filling, no output: even a strong heart can't pump blood it doesn't receive. Veins are capacitance vessels: stretchy, low-pressure, holding most of the blood. Venoconstriction squeezes the reservoir and raises venous return without adding blood. VR = (Pmsf − PRA) ÷ RVR. More fullness raises return; higher right atrial pressure lowers it. Muscle pump: contracting muscles squeeze deep veins, and one-way valves send blood only towards the heart. Standing still pools blood in the legs and can cause fainting; moving the calves helps. Breathing in lowers chest pressure and raises abdominal pressure, so venous return rises. CVP ≈ right atrial pressure (a few mmHg). High CVP can mean overload or right heart failure. Head veins collapse when you stand; dural sinuses can't, so an open sinus above the heart risks air embolism.","microcirculation":"Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange 1. Why is the microcirculation necessary? Every cell needs oxygen and nutrients, but the big arteries can't deliver them directly. The handover happens in the microcirculation: the tiny vessels inside each tissue. Small arteriole → Capillary exchange → Venule → Venous return Capillaries are built for exchange: Thin walls, so the distance for diffusion is tiny. Huge numbers, giving an enormous surface area. Almost every cell is within a few cell-widths of one. Slow flow, because their total cross-section is so large, which gives time for exchange. ( → Haemodynamics, §4) Yet capillaries hold only about 5% of the blood at any moment. 2. What is a capillary wall made of? A capillary wall is just one layer of endothelial cells sitting on a thin basement membrane, sometimes wrapped by pericytes. There is no muscle layer: capillaries can't constrict themselves. That job belongs to the arterioles that feed them. Three types, from tightest to leakiest: Type What the wall looks like Where What gets through Continuous Unbroken lining, with narrow gaps between cells Muscle, skin, lungs; brain (tightest of all) Water, gases and small molecules Fenestrated Cells with tiny pores ( fenestra = window) Gut villi, endocrine glands, kidney glomeruli Water and small solutes, quickly Discontinuous (sinusoidal) Large gaps, broken basement membrane Liver, spleen, bone marrow Even proteins and blood cells The tissue's job decides the type. The gut and glands move a lot of fluid quickly, so they have fenestrated capillaries. The liver handles proteins, so it has the leakiest kind. The brain must be protected, so it has the tightest. Source note: A lecture slide gives the size of gaps and fenestrations in millimetres. They are in fact nanometres wide, far smaller than one cell. 3. How do substances actually cross the capillary wall? Route What crosses What drives it Diffusion O₂, CO₂, glucose, ions Concentration difference: from more to less Gaps and pores Water and small water-soluble molecules The same gradients, through the openings Vesicles Some large molecules The cell carries them across in little bubbles Filtration Bulk water with its dissolved solutes Pressure differences ( §5) Diffusion and filtration are different. In diffusion, individual molecules move down their own concentration difference: O₂ out to the cells, CO₂ back in. In filtration, water as a whole is pushed out by pressure. Most gas and nutrient exchange is diffusion. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why are capillaries so good at exchange? A. Their thick walls protect the solutes as they cross B. Their thin walls, huge surface area and slow flow C. Their fast flow brings fresh blood to cells quickly D. Their muscle layer squeezes solutes out into tissue Show answer and explanation Answer: B. A short diffusion distance, a huge surface area and plenty of time all favour exchange. 2. Which capillaries let even proteins and blood cells through? A. The continuous capillaries of the brain B. The fenestrated capillaries of the gut C. The continuous capillaries of muscle D. The sinusoidal capillaries of the liver Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Sinusoids have large gaps and a broken basement membrane. Brain capillaries are the tightest. 3. How does oxygen get from capillary blood into a working muscle cell? A. By diffusion, from higher to lower concentration B. By filtration, pushed out by blood pressure C. Carried across inside vesicles D. Pumped across by the endothelial cells Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The cell uses oxygen, keeping its own level low, so oxygen diffuses out of the blood towards it. 4. What decides which capillaries receive more blood? Capillaries have no muscle, so they can't control their own flow. Flow is set upstream, by the arterioles (and, in classic teaching, by rings of muscle at the capillary entrances: precapillary sphincters). Active tissue calls for more blood. Working muscle releases metabolites (CO₂, H⁺, adenosine, K⁺) that relax the nearby arterioles. More blood flows in, and more capillaries open up ( recruitment), so more surface area is available for exchange. ( → Local Control) Source note: Precapillary sphincters are classic teaching, but they are not found at every capillary. In many tissues the arterioles do the controlling. 5. Why does fluid leave capillaries even when protein stays in blood? Fluid movement across the capillary wall is a tug-of-war between two kinds of pressure: Hydrostatic pressure is the push of fluid against the wall. Blood pressure inside the capillary ( Pc) pushes water out. Oncotic pressure is the pull of proteins. Proteins can't cross the wall easily, and they hold water with them. Plasma proteins, mainly albumin, pull water in ( πc). The tissue side has its own, smaller, versions of each: Force Symbol Direction Typical size (classic values) Capillary blood pressure Pc Out About 30 mmHg (arterial end), falling to about 10 (venous end) Plasma protein pull πc In About 28 mmHg Tissue fluid pressure Pi Usually slightly out (it is slightly below zero) About −3 mmHg Tissue protein pull πi Out About 8 mmHg Why does fluid leave even though proteins stay in? Because the push out (Pc, with help from the tissue forces) beats the pull in (πc), at least at the arterial end. The proteins stay behind, but water still filters out. Starling balance One capillary drawn left (arterial end) to right (venous end). At each end, an outward arrow shows the push (Pc) and an inward arrow the pull (πc), sized to scale, with the net result: \"fluid leaves\" or \"fluid returns\". Sliders for capillary pressure and plasma protein. Scenario buttons: Normal, Heart failure (Pc ↑), Low albumin (πc ↓), Leaky capillary (proteins escape, so the pull weakens). A line underneath states the net effect in plain words. Classic textbook values. Why does albumin matter so much? It provides most of the pull. If albumin falls (liver disease, kidney protein loss, malnutrition), the pull weakens, more fluid stays out, and tissues swell. ( → Oedema, §5) OPTIONAL VIDEO Starling forces: hydrostatic and oncotic pressure A visual explanation of how capillary pressure and plasma proteins affect fluid movement across the capillary wall. Watch video Open on YouTube Video hosted by its original creator on YouTube; embedded without rehosting. 6. Classic Starling model versus revised understanding The classic model (the lecture's): the push wins at the arterial end, so fluid filters out. At the venous end, Pc has fallen below the protein pull, so fluid is drawn back in. Of about 24 L filtered a day, about 85% is reabsorbed into the capillaries, and 15% is returned by the lymph. What we now know: the inner surface of the capillary is coated with a gel-like layer, the glycocalyx. The protein pull that matters is the one across this layer, and it stays strong along the whole capillary. So in most tissues, little fluid is reabsorbed at the venous end. Instead, a small net filtration continues all along, and the lymphatics return almost all of it. What to learn: know the four forces and the lecture's classic numbers for the exam. But understand that the lymphatics are the main return route, not a back-up. That is why blocked lymphatics cause swelling so readily. ( → Lymphatics) Source note: The 24 L/day and 85%/15% figures are the lecture's classic model. The revised (glycocalyx) model is current physiology (Levick and Michel, 2010). 7. What changes microvascular filtration or permeability? Change Effect on filtration Why Arterioles dilate ↑ More upstream pressure reaches the capillary, so Pc rises Veins congested or blocked ↑ Blood backs up, so Pc rises Leaky capillaries (histamine, bradykinin, substance P) ↑ Proteins escape, weakening the pull and widening the gaps More capillaries open ↑ total exchange More surface area Two very different causes of swelling: a raised pressure (a blocked vein) pushes more water through a normal wall. A leaky wall (inflammation) lets water and proteins through even at normal pressure. ( → Oedema) Understanding Check: Sections 4–7 1. Capillary blood pressure rises with nothing else changed. What happens to filtration? A. It rises, because the push out is stronger B. It falls, because the pull in is stronger C. It stays the same, because proteins stay in D. It reverses, so fluid is drawn back in Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Pc is the main outward force. Raising it increases the net push out. 2. A patient's plasma albumin is very low. Why might their tissues swell? A. Albumin pushes water out of capillaries B. Capillary blood pressure rises when albumin falls C. Less protein in the blood weakens the pull holding water in D. The lymphatics stop working without albumin Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Albumin provides most of the plasma oncotic pull. With less of it, more fluid stays out in the tissues. 3. In the revised (glycocalyx) model, how does most filtered fluid get back to the blood? A. It is reabsorbed at the venous end of the capillaries B. It is returned through the lymphatics C. It evaporates through the skin D. It is excreted by the kidneys Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Little fluid is reabsorbed at the venous end in most tissues. The lymphatics are the main return route. 8. Chapter Essentials Capillaries exchange: thin walls, huge surface area and slow flow; they hold only about 5% of the blood. The wall is one layer of endothelium on a basement membrane, with no muscle. Three types: continuous (tightest in the brain), fenestrated (gut, glands, kidney), sinusoidal (liver, spleen, marrow). Diffusion moves gases and nutrients down their gradients. Filtration pushes bulk water out with pressure. Arterioles and precapillary sphincters control which capillaries get blood. Active tissue opens more. Starling tug-of-war: Pc pushes out, πc (albumin) pulls in; the tissue forces Pi and πi are smaller. Classic model: filtration at the arterial end, reabsorption at the venous end; 24 L/day, 85% back to the capillaries, 15% to the lymph. Revised model: the glycocalyx keeps the pull strong, so little is reabsorbed, and the lymph returns most filtered fluid. Filtration rises with higher Pc (arteriolar dilation, venous congestion), lower πc (low albumin) or a leaky wall (histamine, bradykinin, substance P).","edema":"Formation of Oedema 1. What exactly is oedema? Oedema is too much fluid in the spaces between the cells (the interstitium), enough to make tissue swell. Fluid is always leaving the capillaries, and the lymphatics are always carrying it back. Oedema happens when more leaves than can be carried away. Filtration ↑ More fluid entering tissue + Drainage insufficient Less fluid leaving tissue → Oedema Fluid accumulates So there are only two ways to get oedema: too much filtration, or too little drainage. 2. What physical forces govern filtration? The four Starling forces decide how much fluid leaves the capillaries ( → Microcirculation, §5): Pc, capillary blood pressure: pushes out. πc, plasma protein (albumin) pull: holds water in. Pi and πi, the tissue's own pressure and protein pull: smaller. Traditional Starling filtration model J v = K f [ ( P c − P i) − σ ( π c − π i)] Two extra terms: Kf, how leaky the wall is (and how much wall there is). A leaky, inflamed wall has a high Kf. σ, how well the wall holds back protein. σ = 1 means proteins can't cross at all, so their full pull counts. In leaky, inflamed capillaries σ falls, proteins slip through, and their pull is lost. 3. Five routes to the same outcome: excess interstitial fluid What fails What changes Examples Push too high Pc ↑ Blocked veins, heart failure, standing still, leaky vein valves Pull too weak πc ↓ (low albumin) Liver disease, kidney protein loss ( nephrotic syndrome), malnutrition Wall too leaky Kf ↑, σ ↓ Inflammation, burns, allergy Drain blocked Lymph flow ↓ Filariasis, lymph nodes removed in breast cancer surgery Too much fluid overall Salt and water retained Kidney failure Starling balance: what tips it towards oedema? The Starling balance interactive from Microcirculation, opening on Heart failure. Each scenario button shows which arrow changes and the net result. The key question for any swelling: which part failed? Different diseases can look alike on the outside but work in completely different ways. 4. Why does raised venous pressure cause oedema? If blood can't drain out of the veins, it backs up into the capillaries, raising Pc and pushing more fluid out. The lecture lists: Blocked veins (a clot in a leg vein): one leg swells. Leaky vein valves (varicose veins): blood falls back and pools. Standing still for long periods: gravity raises leg venous pressure, and the muscle pump isn't working, so the ankles swell. ( → Venous Return, §4) Heart failure: A failing right heart raises systemic venous pressure, causing swollen ankles. A failing left heart raises pressure in the lung veins, causing pulmonary oedema: fluid in the lungs, which stops oxygen getting into the blood and makes the patient breathless. Arteriole and venule tone matter too. Widening the arterioles lets more of the arterial pressure through to the capillaries. Tightening the venules holds blood back in them. Both raise Pc, and the lecture lists both as causes. 5. Why can low plasma proteins cause oedema? Albumin is the main protein holding water in the blood. If it falls, that pull weakens and fluid stays out in the tissues, often all over the body. Albumin falls when: The liver can't make enough: liver failure ( cirrhosis). The kidneys leak it into the urine: nephrotic syndrome. The diet lacks protein: severe malnutrition (kwashiorkor). The lecture also mentions osmotically active substances building up in the tissues: they pull water out into the tissue from the other side. 6. How does inflammation increase oedema? Histamine, bradykinin and substance P make the capillary wall leaky: the gaps between endothelial cells widen. Water and proteins pour out. The escaped proteins now pull water outwards, adding to the swelling. This is why a sting or a sprain swells within minutes. It is not a pressure problem; the wall itself has changed. Understanding Check: Sections 1–6 1. A man has a clot blocking the main vein of one leg. Why does that leg swell? A. Blood backs up and raises capillary pressure, pushing more fluid out B. The clot releases proteins that pull water into the tissue C. The lymphatics in that leg are blocked by the clot D. Albumin in that leg is destroyed by the clot Show answer and explanation Answer: A. A blocked vein raises Pc upstream, so filtration rises in that leg only. 2. A child with severe protein malnutrition has a swollen belly and legs. What is the main mechanism? A. Raised capillary blood pressure from heart failure B. Low albumin weakens the pull holding water in the blood C. Inflammation makes every capillary leaky D. Blocked lymphatics in the abdomen Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Without enough dietary protein, albumin falls, and the plasma oncotic pull weakens throughout the body. 3. Why does a bee sting swell within minutes? A. The sting raises blood pressure in the arm B. The venom blocks the local lymph vessels C. Histamine makes capillaries leaky, so fluid and proteins escape D. The sting lowers the level of plasma albumin Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Inflammatory mediators open the gaps in the capillary wall. The leaked proteins then pull still more water out. 4. Why can left-sided heart failure make a patient breathless? A. Fluid collects around the heart and squeezes it B. Raised lung venous pressure pushes fluid into the lungs C. The right ventricle stops pumping blood to the lungs D. Low albumin causes swelling throughout the chest Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Back pressure into the lung capillaries causes pulmonary oedema, which blocks oxygen getting into the blood. 7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause persistent swelling? Even with normal pressures, some fluid and protein leave the capillaries all the time, and only the lymphatics can take the protein back. If they are blocked, protein-rich fluid builds up. Over time it causes inflammation and scarring, and the swelling becomes firm and permanent: lymphoedema. The lecture's examples: Filariasis ( elephantiasis): parasitic worms block the lymph vessels, so the legs or scrotum swell enormously. Radical mastectomy: removing the armpit lymph nodes in breast cancer surgery leaves that arm swollen. ( → Lymphatics) 8. Why are pitting and non-pitting oedema different? Press a thumb firmly into swollen skin for a few seconds. Pitting oedema: a dent stays after you let go. The extra fluid is free to move and is pushed aside. This is typical of heart failure, low albumin and venous causes. Non-pitting oedema: no dent forms. The tissue is firm, because the fluid is bound up with protein and the tissue has become scarred. Pitting oedema in both feet. Firm thumb pressure for a few seconds pushes the free interstitial fluid aside. When the thumb is lifted, a pit is left behind and fills in slowly as the fluid seeps back. Lymphoedema is usually non-pitting, but only once it is long-standing. Early lymphoedema can still pit, and it becomes firm as scarring develops. Source note: The lecture states that lymphoedema does not pit. That describes its usual late stage; early lymphoedema can pit. 9. Which location of oedema suggests which mechanism? Where the swelling is What it suggests Both ankles, worse at the end of the day Gravity plus raised venous pressure (standing, right heart failure) The lungs (breathlessness, crackles) Left heart failure, or leaky lung capillaries All over, including around the eyes Low albumin (kidney or liver disease, malnutrition) One arm or leg, firm Blocked lymphatics, or a blocked vein These are clues, not diagnoses. Heart and kidney disease often cause oedema by more than one route at once. Understanding Check: Sections 7–9 1. A woman's arm has been firmly swollen since her armpit lymph nodes were removed. What is the mechanism? A. Raised venous pressure from a clot in the arm B. Fluid and protein can't drain away through the lymphatics C. Low plasma albumin from the surgery D. Histamine released by the scar tissue Show answer and explanation Answer: B. With the nodes removed, protein-rich fluid can't drain, so lymphoedema develops. 2. You press on a patient's shin and a dent remains. What does that tell you? A. The extra fluid is free to move and was pushed aside B. The tissue is scarred and firm C. The swelling must be caused by blocked lymphatics D. The patient's albumin level must be normal Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Pitting means mobile fluid. It is typical of heart failure, low albumin and venous causes, and of early lymphoedema. 3. Why do proteins matter so much in lymphatic oedema? A. Only the lymphatics can return escaped proteins to the blood B. Proteins block the capillary pores and raise pressure C. Lymph contains no protein, so it dilutes the tissue D. Proteins make the veins collapse and stop draining Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Capillaries can't take protein back. If the lymph is blocked, protein builds up and holds water in the tissue. 10. Chapter Essentials Oedema = too much fluid between the cells. More leaves the capillaries than the lymphatics can carry away. Only two ways: too much filtration, or too little drainage. Five routes: push too high (Pc ↑), pull too weak (albumin ↓), wall too leaky, drain blocked, or too much fluid overall. Raised venous pressure: blocked veins, leaky valves, standing still, heart failure. Right failure → swollen ankles; left failure → pulmonary oedema. Low albumin: liver failure, nephrotic syndrome, malnutrition → swelling everywhere. Inflammation: histamine, bradykinin and substance P make the wall leaky; escaped proteins pull more water out. Lymphatic blockage: filariasis, removal of lymph nodes → protein-rich, firm lymphoedema. Pitting = mobile fluid. Non-pitting = firm, scarred tissue (late lymphoedema). Where the swelling is points to the cause: both ankles → raised venous pressure (standing, right heart failure); the lungs → left heart failure; all over, including around the eyes → low albumin; one firm limb → a blocked lymphatic or vein.","lymphatics":"Lymphatic Circulation 1. Why do we need lymphatic vessels if we already have veins? Every day, capillaries filter fluid and a little protein into the tissues, and most of it doesn't go straight back into the blood. Capillaries can't take protein back at all. Without another route, tissues would steadily swell. ( → Microcirculation, §6) The lymphatic system is that route. It collects the excess fluid, proteins and particles from the tissues and returns them to the veins. Once inside a lymph vessel, the fluid is called lymph. Capillary filtration → Interstitial fluid → Lymph capillaries → Lymph vessels and ducts → Venous blood The lymphatics are a main route, not a back-up. In most tissues, they return most of the fluid filtered from the capillaries. ( → Microcirculation, §6) 2. How does lymph enter a lymphatic capillary? Lymph capillaries start as blind-ended tubes in the tissues, closed at one end like the finger of a glove. Their wall is one layer of endothelial cells whose edges overlap like roof tiles. Fine anchoring filaments tie these cells to the surrounding tissue. One-way flaps: When tissue fluid builds up, it pushes on the overlapping edges, and the anchoring filaments pull them apart, so the flaps open inwards and fluid, proteins and even bacteria flow in. When pressure inside the lymph capillary rises, the flaps are pushed shut, so nothing leaks back out. This is how proteins get back into the circulation. They are too big to re-enter blood capillaries, but they pass easily through the lymphatic flaps. 3. How can lymph move when there is no central lymphatic heart? Lymph flows at very low pressure, with no heart to pump it. Several things keep it moving: The vessels pump themselves. Larger lymph vessels have smooth muscle that contracts rhythmically, segment by segment. Valves inside the vessels allow flow only towards the heart. The skeletal muscle pump. Moving muscles squeeze the lymph vessels, just as they squeeze veins. ( → Venous Return, §4) Breathing. Pressure changes in the chest and abdomen draw lymph upwards. Pulsing arteries next to lymph vessels squeeze them with each beat. Like veins, but with one key difference: veins continue on from blood capillaries in a closed circuit. Lymphatics start blind, in the tissue, and only join the blood at the very end. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why do we need lymphatics if capillaries already return some fluid? A. Capillaries can't take back protein that has leaked into the tissues B. Lymphatics carry oxygen to tissues that capillaries miss C. Capillaries only filter fluid at night, so lymph covers the day D. Lymphatics are needed only when blood pressure is high Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Only the lymphatics can return escaped protein, and in most tissues they also return most of the filtered fluid. 2. How do lymph capillaries let fluid in but not out? A. They have thick muscular walls that pump fluid in B. Their overlapping endothelial flaps open inwards and shut against backflow C. Proteins inside them pull fluid in by oncotic pressure D. They are connected directly to the blood capillaries Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Fluid pressure opens the overlapping edges inwards. A rise in pressure inside pushes them shut. 3. Lymph has no heart to pump it. Which of these does NOT help it move? A. Contractions of smooth muscle in the lymph vessel walls B. One-way valves inside the lymph vessels C. Squeezing by nearby skeletal muscles D. Pumping by the lymph nodes Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Lymph nodes filter lymph for the immune system. They are not pumps. 4. Where does lymph finally return to blood? All lymph drains into two main ducts. Each empties into the veins at the venous angle, where the internal jugular vein joins the subclavian vein: Duct What it drains Where it empties Thoracic duct Everything except the right upper quarter: both legs, the abdomen, the left side of the chest, left arm, left head and neck Left venous angle Right lymphatic duct The right upper quarter only: the right side of the head and neck, the right arm, the right side of the chest Right venous angle Figure 1. Which duct drains where? Right lymphatic duct: right upper quarter Thoracic duct: everything else left venous angle right venous angle → diaphragm level patient's right patient's left Front view (the patient's right is on your left). The thoracic duct starts in the abdomen and runs up the middle of the body to the left venous angle. The short right lymphatic duct drains only the right upper quarter, into the right venous angle. Memory hook: the thoracic duct takes three-quarters of the body; the right lymphatic duct takes the right upper quarter. What do lymph nodes do? On its way, lymph passes through lymph nodes: bean-shaped filters packed with immune cells. They check the lymph for bacteria, viruses and cancer cells. That is why nodes swell during an infection, and why cancer often spreads first to the nearest nodes. Nodes filter; they don't pump. 5. Why is the intestinal lymphatic system important in fat absorption? Each finger-like villus of the small intestine contains a lymph capillary called a lacteal ( lac = milk, after the milky look of lymph after a fatty meal). How fat gets into the blood: Long-chain dietary lipids → Enterocyte chylomicrons → Villus lacteal → Mesenteric lymph → Thoracic duct → Blood Gut cells absorb the digested fats and repackage them into large fat droplets wrapped in protein: chylomicrons. Chylomicrons are too big to enter blood capillaries, so they enter the leaky lacteals instead. They travel up the lymphatics and the thoracic duct, and enter the blood at the left venous angle. This milky, fat-rich lymph from the gut is called chyle. Not all fat goes this way. Short- and medium-chain fatty acids are small enough to go straight into the blood capillaries, and on to the liver in the portal vein. It is the long-chain fats (and fat-soluble vitamins) that need the lymph. Understanding Check: Sections 4–5 1. Lymph from the left leg reaches the blood through which duct? A. The right lymphatic duct, at the right venous angle B. The thoracic duct, at the left venous angle C. The thoracic duct, at the right venous angle D. The portal vein, through the liver Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The thoracic duct drains everything except the right upper quarter, and empties at the left venous angle. 2. Why do long-chain dietary fats enter lacteals rather than blood capillaries? A. Blood capillaries in the gut have no openings B. They are packaged into chylomicrons, too big for blood capillaries C. Lacteals actively pump fat out of the gut cells D. Fat would raise blood pressure if it entered the blood directly Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Chylomicrons are large particles. They pass through the leaky lacteal wall but not into blood capillaries. 3. Why do lymph nodes in the neck swell during a throat infection? A. Lymph backs up behind the nodes because they stop pumping B. Immune cells in the nodes multiply as they respond to the infection C. Fat from the throat collects in the nodes D. The nodes fill with blood from inflamed capillaries Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Nodes are immune filters. When they trap microbes, their immune cells multiply and the nodes enlarge. 6. How does lymphatic drainage protect tissue fluid balance? The lymphatics keep the tissues from swelling by matching what the capillaries filter. When filtration rises, lymph flow can rise several-fold to keep up, which is a safety margin. Oedema appears only when filtration outstrips this, or when the lymphatics themselves fail. ( → Oedema, §1) Problem What fails Example Too much filtration The lymphatics can't keep up Venous congestion, heart failure Too little drainage Normal filtration, but it can't be removed Filariasis, surgery that removes lymph nodes 7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause lymphoedema? When lymphatics are blocked, protein-rich fluid builds up. The protein holds the water in the tissue, and over time causes inflammation and scarring. The result is lymphoedema: swelling that is soft at first and becomes firm and non-pitting as it lasts. ( → Oedema, §8) The lecture's examples: Filariasis: parasitic worms (spread by mosquitoes) live in and damage the lymph vessels, and can cause the gross swelling of elephantiasis. Radical mastectomy: removing the armpit lymph nodes in breast cancer surgery can leave the arm swollen. Cancer blocking lymph nodes, and lymphatics that never formed properly (congenital), can do the same. 8. What happens if lymphatic function is lost? Three jobs fail: Fluid balance: fluid and protein stay in the tissues, causing lymphoedema. Immune defence: lymph no longer reaches the nodes, so infections are detected and fought less well, and swollen limbs get infected easily. Fat absorption: if the gut lymphatics fail, long-chain fats and fat-soluble vitamins can't be absorbed properly. Understanding Check: Sections 6–8 1. Filtration into a tissue doubles, but the tissue doesn't swell. Why? A. Capillaries reabsorb all the extra fluid at the venous end B. Lymph flow rises to carry away the extra fluid C. The extra fluid is absorbed by the tissue cells D. Filtration can't double without the tissue swelling Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Lymph flow can increase several-fold, a safety margin against oedema. 2. A man from an area where filariasis is common has one hugely swollen, thickened leg. What is the mechanism? A. Worms in the lymph vessels block drainage, so protein-rich fluid builds up B. Worms in the veins block blood returning from the leg C. The infection lowers his plasma albumin D. The worms release histamine that makes capillaries leaky Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Filarial worms damage the lymphatics. Protein-rich fluid accumulates, and chronic inflammation causes the thick, firm swelling of elephantiasis. 3. Which problem would loss of the gut lymphatics specifically cause? A. Inability to absorb glucose from the gut B. Inability to absorb long-chain fats and fat-soluble vitamins C. Inability to absorb water from the gut D. Inability to absorb amino acids from the gut Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Long-chain fats travel as chylomicrons through the lacteals. Glucose, amino acids and water go straight into the blood. 9. Chapter Essentials Lymphatics return excess tissue fluid and all escaped protein to the blood. They are a main route, not a back-up. Lymph capillaries are blind-ended, with overlapping flaps that open inwards and shut against backflow. Lymph moves by vessel-wall contractions, valves, the muscle pump, breathing and pulsing arteries. There is no heart, and nodes don't pump. Thoracic duct drains three-quarters of the body into the left venous angle. Right lymphatic duct drains the right upper quarter into the right venous angle. Lymph nodes filter lymph for the immune system. Lacteals carry long-chain fats as chylomicrons. Milky gut lymph is chyle. Short- and medium-chain fats go straight to the blood. Lymph flow can rise several-fold to prevent oedema. Swelling appears when filtration outstrips it or drainage fails. Lymphoedema (filariasis, node removal, cancer): protein-rich, becoming firm and non-pitting. Lost lymphatics → swelling (fluid and protein are left in the tissues), weaker immune defence (lymph no longer reaches the nodes' immune cells) and poor fat absorption (lacteals can't carry chylomicrons).","local-regulation":"Local Control of Blood Flow 1. Why does a tissue need to control its own blood flow? The heart sets the total output, but each tissue needs a supply matched to its own needs, moment to moment. A sprinting leg muscle needs far more blood than it did at rest. The brain and kidneys need a steady supply, even when blood pressure wobbles. Recall: flow = pressure difference ÷ resistance ( → Haemodynamics, §2). A tissue can't change the arterial pressure, but it can change its own resistance, by widening or narrowing its arterioles. And because resistance depends on the fourth power of the radius, a small change in arteriolar width has a big effect on flow: halving the radius raises the resistance about 16 times. Local control comes in two forms: Autoregulation: keeping flow steady when the pressure changes (§2–3). Hyperaemia: raising flow when the tissue needs more ( §4). 2. Autoregulation: steady flow despite changing pressure Autoregulation is a tissue's ability to keep its blood flow relatively constant when its perfusion pressure changes, by adjusting its own resistance. If perfusion pressure… Flow at first… The arterioles… So flow… Rises Rises Constrict (resistance up) Returns towards normal Falls Falls Dilate (resistance down) Returns towards normal It is strongest in the brain, kidneys and heart. ( → Cerebral Circulation, §3) It only works within a range. If pressure falls so far that the arterioles are already fully dilated, they have nothing left to give, and flow falls with pressure. This is why severe shock damages the brain and kidneys despite autoregulation. ( → Shock) How does a tissue do it? The lecture gives two theories. They are not rivals: both work at the same time. 3. Two mechanisms: myogenic and metabolic The myogenic mechanism: the vessel responds to stretch Pressure rises, so the arteriole is stretched. Stretch opens channels in the smooth muscle, which contracts. The arteriole narrows, raising resistance, so flow comes back down. When pressure falls, the opposite happens: less stretch, the muscle relaxes, and the arteriole widens. Figure 1. Myogenic opposition to a rise in pressure Pressure Flow pressure rises time → brief rise Pressure rises → the wall is stretched → smooth muscle contracts → radius falls → resistance rises, so flow returns close to where it started. Original conceptual schematic. The first pressure-driven increase in flow occurs before the slower corrective vascular response. Why is this protective? It stops a surge of pressure from being passed straight into the fragile capillaries downstream. The law of Laplace: why small vessels don't burst. For a cylindrical vessel, the wall tension needed to resist the pressure inside is: T = P × r (wall tension = transmural pressure × radius) Transmural pressure = pressure inside − pressure in the surrounding tissue. At the same pressure, a vessel with half the radius needs only half the wall tension. So a capillary, with its tiny radius, can withstand blood pressure with a wall only one cell thick. And a vessel that widens (as in an aneurysm) needs more tension at the same pressure, so it is more likely to stretch further and rupture. Source note: The lecture writes the general form as P = T(1/R₁ + 1/R₂), which for a cylinder simplifies to P = T/r, i.e. T = P × r. Its other form, P = T/(R₁ + R₂), is not equivalent and has been left out. The metabolic mechanism: the tissue signals its needs When a tissue's blood supply falls short of its needs, its metabolites build up. These dilate the arterioles, so flow rises and washes the metabolites away. As they clear, the arterioles narrow again. It is a self-correcting loop. Which signals dilate? Think about what a tissue short of blood would produce: Low O₂ High CO₂ and H⁺ (low pH), including lactic acid K⁺, released by active cells Adenosine (and ADP in heart muscle), from ATP breakdown Higher temperature Which signals constrict? Serotonin (from platelets, at an injured vessel), cold and angiotensin II. Myogenic or metabolic: what does each sense? Myogenic senses pressure (stretch of the vessel wall). Metabolic senses supply versus demand in the tissue. 4. Active and reactive hyperaemia Hyperaemia simply means increased blood flow. Active hyperaemia Reactive hyperaemia When During increased activity After blood flow was cut off for a while Example Working muscle in exercise The red flush of a hand after a tight cuff is released Why Active cells make more metabolites, which dilate the arterioles During the blockage, metabolites built up and the arterioles dilated; when flow returns, it surges into the wide-open bed Then Flow stays high as long as the activity continues Flow overshoots, washes the metabolites out, then returns to normal The longer the blockage, the bigger and longer the reactive surge: the tissue is \"repaying\" its oxygen debt. Don't confuse these with autoregulation. Autoregulation keeps flow steady when pressure changes. Hyperaemia raises flow because the tissue needs it. Understanding Check: Sections 1–4 1. Perfusion pressure to a kidney rises suddenly. How does autoregulation respond? A. The arterioles dilate, lowering resistance so flow rises further B. The heart slows, lowering the pressure that reaches the kidney C. The arterioles constrict, raising resistance so flow returns towards normal D. The kidney's veins constrict, holding more blood in the organ Show answer and explanation Answer: C. More pressure stretches the arterioles, which constrict. The higher resistance brings flow back down. 2. Why can a capillary withstand blood pressure with a wall only one cell thick? A. Its blood pressure is higher than in the larger arteries B. Its tiny radius means only a small wall tension is needed C. Its wall contains thick smooth muscle that resists stretch D. Its large radius spreads the pressure over a bigger area Show answer and explanation Answer: B. By the law of Laplace, T = P × r. A very small radius needs very little tension. 3. A tourniquet is removed after five minutes, and the limb flushes red. What explains this? A. The muscles in the limb began to work harder after release B. The rise in arterial pressure stretched and opened the arterioles C. Sympathetic nerves to the limb were activated by the tourniquet D. Metabolites built up during the blockage, dilating the arterioles Show answer and explanation Answer: D. This is reactive hyperaemia: the dilated bed receives a surge of blood when flow returns. 5. Signals from the endothelium The endothelium, the single-cell lining of the vessels, is not just a passive tube. It releases signals that act on the smooth muscle underneath: Signal Effect Notes Nitric oxide (NO) Dilates Released when flowing blood drags on the endothelium ( shear stress). It diffuses into the smooth muscle, raises cGMP, and the muscle relaxes. Prostacyclin Dilates Also stops platelets clumping Endothelin-1 Constricts A powerful vasoconstrictor Several other substances dilate vessels through the endothelium, by making it release NO: bradykinin, substance P and acetylcholine. Histamine (via H₂ receptors) and ANP also dilate vessels. ( → Cutaneous Circulation, §3) Why does this matter? When the endothelium is damaged (in atherosclerosis, smoking or diabetes), it makes less NO. The vessels dilate less well, and the balance tips towards constriction and clotting. 6. Local control and the whole body Local control works alongside the nerves and hormones of the next two chapters. ( → Neural and Hormonal Control) Sometimes they cooperate; sometimes they compete: In exercise, sympathetic nerves constrict arterioles throughout the body. But in the working muscles, metabolites dilate them so strongly that they override the sympathetic signal. So blood is redirected to where it is needed. In haemorrhage, sympathetic constriction diverts blood from the skin and gut. If a tissue becomes badly starved of blood, its metabolites start to fight back, dilating its arterioles. Understanding Check: Sections 5–6 1. How does nitric oxide relax vascular smooth muscle? A. It raises cGMP in the smooth muscle, which relaxes it B. It blocks sympathetic nerves supplying the arteriole C. It raises calcium in the smooth muscle, which relaxes it D. It lowers the blood pressure inside the arteriole Show answer and explanation Answer: A. NO diffuses from the endothelium into the smooth muscle, activates guanylyl cyclase and raises cGMP. 2. During exercise, sympathetic activity rises, yet blood flow to the working muscles increases. Why? A. Sympathetic nerves do not supply the arterioles in skeletal muscle B. The rise in blood pressure alone stretches open the muscle arterioles C. Myogenic constriction is switched off in skeletal muscle D. Local metabolites dilate the muscle arterioles, overriding the constriction Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Metabolic dilation in active muscle beats sympathetic constriction, so blood is redirected to the muscle. 3. Which of these constricts arterioles locally? A. Adenosine released from active cells B. Serotonin released from platelets C. Potassium released from active cells D. Nitric oxide released from endothelium Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Serotonin, cold and angiotensin II constrict. Adenosine, K⁺ and NO all dilate. 7. Chapter Essentials Flow = pressure difference ÷ resistance. Tissues control their flow by changing arteriolar radius (resistance ∝ 1/r⁴). Autoregulation keeps flow steady when pressure changes: arterioles constrict when pressure rises and dilate when it falls. It works within a range and is strongest in the brain, kidneys and heart. Myogenic: stretch → smooth muscle contracts → arteriole narrows. Senses pressure. Metabolic: low O₂, high CO₂/H⁺, lactic acid, K⁺, adenosine, heat → dilation → metabolites washed out. Senses supply versus demand. Local constrictors: platelet serotonin, cold and angiotensin II narrow arterioles, raising resistance and cutting local flow. Law of Laplace (cylinder): T = P × r. Small vessels need little wall tension; widened vessels need more. Active hyperaemia: more flow during activity, because the tissue's own metabolites dilate its arterioles. Reactive hyperaemia: a surge after a blockage, because metabolites and an oxygen debt build up while flow is stopped. Endothelium: NO (via cGMP) and prostacyclin dilate; endothelin-1 constricts. Bradykinin, substance P and ACh dilate through NO. In exercise, local metabolic dilation overrides sympathetic constriction in working muscle.","neural-humoral":"Neural and Hormonal Control 1. Why does the body need central control? Local control looks after one tissue at a time. ( → Local Control) But someone has to look after the whole circulation: keeping arterial pressure high enough to perfuse every organ, and moving blood to where it's needed. That central control works on the variables you already know ( → Blood Pressure): Mean arterial pressure ≈ cardiac output × total peripheral resistance, and cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume. So the nervous system and hormones can raise blood pressure in three ways: Speed up the heart (heart rate). Strengthen each beat (stroke volume, via contractility and venous return). Narrow the arterioles (peripheral resistance). 2. The control centre in the medulla The heart and blood vessels are controlled by groups of neurons in the medulla oblongata, together called the vasomotor centre. It has two opposing parts: Pressor area Depressor area Where Upper, lateral part of the medulla Lower, inner part of the medulla Does Excites the sympathetic neurons in the spinal cord Inhibits the pressor area Result Blood pressure rises Blood pressure falls The pressor area sends fibres straight down to the sympathetic neurons in the spinal cord. Nearby are the vagal neurons that slow the heart. Sensory signals from the body's pressure and chemical sensors arrive at a relay station, the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS), which adjusts all of these. ( → Reflexes) Source note: The lecture's \"pressor\" and \"depressor\" areas correspond to what modern texts call the rostral ventrolateral medulla (pressor) and the caudal ventrolateral medulla (depressor). 3. Sympathetic control of the blood vessels Sympathetic fibres supply the smooth muscle of all blood vessels except the capillaries (and the smallest venules, which have little muscle). Most release noradrenaline, which acts on α₁ receptors to constrict the vessel. They do two different jobs, depending on the vessel: Vessel What constriction does Arterioles (resistance vessels) Raises resistance, so blood pressure rises and flow is redistributed Veins (capacitance vessels) Squeezes stored blood out of the veins towards the heart, raising venous return and so cardiac output The key idea: a constant background \"tone\" The sympathetic constrictor fibres fire all the time, at a low rate. This tonic discharge keeps every arteriole partly constricted. There is no equivalent background firing in dilator fibres. So the body controls vessel width by turning the sympathetic tone up or down: More sympathetic firing → constriction. Less sympathetic firing → dilation. No dilator nerve is needed; the vessel simply relaxes. That is why cutting the sympathetic nerves (or a spinal injury) makes vessels dilate and blood pressure fall. An exception: sympathetic cholinergic dilator fibres. Some sympathetic fibres to skeletal muscle release acetylcholine and dilate the vessels. They are switched on by the cortex, in anticipation of exercise or in alarm. Source note: These cholinergic dilator fibres are well shown in animals; their importance in humans is uncertain. Human sympathetic control of blood vessels is mainly noradrenergic and constrictor. 4. Control of the heart: sympathetic and vagus Sympathetic Parasympathetic (vagus) Transmitter and receptor Noradrenaline on β₁ Acetylcholine on M₂ Heart rate Faster ( SA node) Slower (SA node) AV node Faster conduction Slower conduction Force of contraction Stronger Little effect on the ventricles ( → Cardiac Conduction) At rest, the vagus is in charge. The SA node on its own would fire at about 100 beats/min. Constant vagal tone holds the resting rate down to about 70. So: Cutting the vagus (vagotomy) → heart rate rises. Atropine, which blocks the vagus's muscarinic receptors → heart rate rises. The vagus acts mainly on the heart, not the vessels. Most blood vessels have little or no parasympathetic supply. So the vagus controls blood pressure mainly through heart rate. Understanding Check: Sections 1–4 1. How do the sympathetic nerves make an arteriole dilate? A. They release acetylcholine onto every arteriole in the body B. They increase their firing, which opens the arteriole wider C. They switch on a separate set of tonic dilator fibres D. They reduce their background constrictor firing, so the muscle relaxes Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Sympathetic constrictor fibres fire tonically. Turning that tone down is how most vessels dilate. 2. A patient is given atropine. What happens to the resting heart rate, and why? A. It rises, because atropine blocks the vagal brake on the SA node B. It falls, because atropine blocks sympathetic β₁ receptors on the heart C. It rises, because atropine directly stimulates the SA node cells D. It stays the same, because the vagus has no effect at rest Show answer and explanation Answer: A. At rest, vagal tone holds the rate below the SA node's own rate. Removing it lets the rate rise. 3. What does sympathetic constriction of the veins achieve? A. It raises peripheral resistance, the main cause of high pressure B. It reduces the volume of blood returning to the heart C. It pushes stored blood towards the heart, raising venous return D. It makes new blood to replace volume lost in a bleed Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Veins are the blood reservoir. Squeezing them raises venous return and so cardiac output. 5. Hormones that act on the circulation Hormones act more slowly than nerves, but their effects can last much longer. Constrictors and volume-raisers: Hormone From Main effects Adrenaline Adrenal medulla β₁: faster, stronger heart. β₂: dilates arterioles in skeletal muscle and liver. α₁ (at high levels): constricts most other vessels. Angiotensin II Made in the blood when the kidney releases renin (low pressure) Constricts arterioles; triggers aldosterone, thirst and ADH Aldosterone Adrenal cortex Kidneys keep Na⁺ (and water follows), raising blood volume over hours to days ADH (vasopressin) Posterior pituitary Kidneys keep water; at high levels, also constricts arterioles Dilators (from the lecture): Substance Main effects ANP (atrial natriuretic peptide) Released when the atria are stretched by too much volume. Dilates vessels and makes the kidneys lose Na⁺ and water. Kinins (e.g. bradykinin) Made in the plasma and tissues. Dilate arterioles, through endothelial NO. VIP (vasoactive intestinal polypeptide) Dilates vessels, especially in the gut Why can adrenaline both dilate and constrict? Because different vessels carry different receptors. Muscle arterioles have many β₂ receptors, which dilate; skin and gut arterioles have mainly α₁ receptors, which constrict. So adrenaline redirects blood towards the muscles. 6. Fast, slow and slower: putting it together Speed Mechanism Good for Seconds Nerves (sympathetic, vagus) and the reflexes that drive them Standing up, sudden blood loss, exercise Minutes Adrenaline, angiotensin II, ADH Sustaining the response Hours to days Aldosterone, ADH and the kidneys' control of salt and water Restoring blood volume; long-term blood pressure Haemorrhage, step by step: Blood volume falls, so venous return, stroke volume and arterial pressure fall. Within seconds: sympathetic firing rises and vagal tone falls. The heart beats faster and harder, arterioles constrict, and veins squeeze stored blood towards the heart. Within minutes: adrenaline, angiotensin II and ADH reinforce the constriction. Over hours to days: aldosterone and ADH make the kidneys keep salt and water, and thirst rises, slowly refilling the circulation. The nerves can hold up the pressure, but they can't replace lost blood. Only the slower, kidney-driven steps restore the volume. ( → Shock) Understanding Check: Sections 5–6 1. Why does adrenaline increase blood flow to skeletal muscle while reducing it to the skin? A. Muscle arterioles have α₁ receptors that dilate; skin arterioles have β₂ that constrict B. Muscle arterioles have β₂ receptors that dilate; skin arterioles have α₁ that constrict C. Adrenaline reaches muscle only, because skin has no blood supply from the aorta D. Adrenaline acts only on the heart, and the muscle simply receives the extra output Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The response depends on the receptors each vessel carries. 2. What stimulates the release of ANP, and what does it do? A. Low blood pressure; it constricts vessels and makes the kidneys keep salt B. Low blood volume; it triggers thirst and the release of aldosterone C. Atrial stretch; it dilates vessels and makes the kidneys lose salt and water D. Sympathetic firing; it speeds the heart and strengthens its contraction Show answer and explanation Answer: C. ANP is the body's response to too much volume: it lowers volume and pressure. 3. After a large haemorrhage, which mechanism actually restores the lost blood volume? A. Sympathetic constriction of the arterioles throughout the body B. Kidney salt and water retention driven by aldosterone and ADH C. Sympathetic constriction of the veins that store the blood D. A faster heart rate driven by the withdrawal of vagal tone Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Nerves raise pressure within seconds but don't add volume. The kidneys, over hours to days, do. 7. Chapter Essentials MAP ≈ CO × TPR, and CO = HR × SV. Central control adjusts heart rate, contractility, venous return and arteriolar tone. Vasomotor centre in the medulla: the pressor area excites spinal sympathetic neurons; the depressor area inhibits it. Sensory input arrives at the NTS. Sympathetic constrictor fibres (noradrenaline, α₁) supply all vessels except capillaries. Arterioles: resistance. Veins: push stored blood to the heart. Tonic constrictor discharge: less firing = dilation. There is no tonic dilator discharge. Sympathetic cholinergic dilators supply skeletal muscle (doubtful importance in humans). Heart: sympathetic (β₁) = faster and stronger; vagus (M₂) = slower. Resting vagal tone holds the rate down, so vagotomy or atropine raises it. Hormones: adrenaline (β₁, β₂ dilation in muscle, α₁ constriction elsewhere), angiotensin II, aldosterone, ADH raise pressure or volume; ANP, kinins and VIP dilate. Timescales: nerves in seconds, hormones in minutes, kidneys over hours to days. Only the kidneys restore lost volume.","reflexes":"Cardiovascular Reflexes 1. What is a cardiovascular reflex? Neural and Hormonal Control described the controls: the vasomotor centre, the sympathetic nerves, the vagus. ( → Neural and Hormonal Control) This chapter describes the sensors that tell the centre when to act. Every reflex has the same five links: Stimulus → receptor → sensory (afferent) nerve → medulla → autonomic (efferent) nerves → heart and vessels Most cardiovascular reflexes are negative feedback: they push the change back the other way. 2. Baroreceptors: the body's pressure sensors Baroreceptors are stretch-sensitive nerve endings in the walls of the large arteries. They don't sense pressure directly: they sense stretch. Higher pressure stretches the wall more, so they fire faster. Site Sensory nerve Arrives at Carotid sinus (where the common carotid divides) Carotid sinus nerve, a branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) NTS in the medulla Aortic arch Vagus nerve (X) NTS in the medulla Both sit in high-pressure arteries, right at the \"inlets\" to the brain and body. 3. The baroreflex, step by step The baroreflex loop when blood pressure rises Blood pressure rises ↑ Baroreceptors carotid sinus, aorta fire faster ↑ IX and X → NTS in the medulla Depressor area ↑ inhibits pressor area Vagal neurons ↑ in the medulla Sympathetic ↓ heart, arterioles, veins Vagus to SA node ↑ slows the heart Slower, weaker heart · arterioles dilate · veins relax …so pressure falls back ↓ When blood pressure falls, every arrow reverses: the baroreceptors fire less, sympathetic firing rises, vagal firing falls, and the heart speeds up while the vessels constrict. Reflex tracer Three buttons: \"Blood pressure rises\", \"Blood pressure falls\" and \"Intracranial pressure rises\". Each press steps through the reflex one link at a time (Next / Back), lighting up each part of the loop with an up or down arrow and one sentence of explanation. A final summary row shows heart rate, contractility, arteriolar tone, venous tone and blood pressure. Shown with the static baroreflex figure behind the Diagram | Interactive switch. When blood pressure rises: The arterial walls stretch more, so the baroreceptors fire faster. Signals travel up IX and X to the NTS. The NTS excites the depressor area, which inhibits the pressor area. Sympathetic firing falls. The NTS also excites the vagal neurons. Vagal firing to the heart rises. Result: the heart beats slower and less forcefully, arterioles dilate and veins relax. Cardiac output and resistance both fall, so blood pressure falls back towards normal. When blood pressure falls, everything runs in reverse: Less stretch, so the baroreceptors fire less. The depressor area is no longer excited, so the pressor area is released. Sympathetic firing rises. Vagal firing falls. Result: faster heart ( tachycardia), stronger beats, arterioles constrict, veins squeeze stored blood towards the heart. Blood pressure rises back towards normal. OPTIONAL VIDEO Baroreflex Regulation of Blood Pressure Watch the pressure-sensing reflex in motion, then follow how autonomic activity changes heart rate and blood-vessel tone. Watch video Open on YouTube Video hosted by its creator on YouTube; embedded without rehosting. What the baroreflex is good at, and what it isn't: It is excellent at buffering sudden changes: standing up, a sudden bleed. ( → Gravity) It is not in charge of long-term blood pressure. If pressure stays high for days, the baroreceptors reset to the new level and defend that instead. Long-term pressure is set mainly by the kidneys' control of salt and water. ( → Neural and Hormonal Control, §6) It can't replace lost blood. A fast heart after a bleed shows the reflex is working, not that the problem is solved. ( → Shock) Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Which nerve carries signals from the carotid sinus baroreceptors? A. The vagus nerve (X), via its branches to the aortic arch B. The hypoglossal nerve (XII), via its branches in the neck C. The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX), via the carotid sinus nerve D. The sympathetic chain, via the superior cervical ganglion Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Carotid sinus → IX. Aortic arch → X. 2. A patient's blood pressure suddenly rises. What does the baroreflex do to heart rate and arterioles? A. Slows the heart and dilates the arterioles B. Speeds the heart and constricts the arterioles C. Slows the heart and constricts the arterioles D. Speeds the heart and dilates the arterioles Show answer and explanation Answer: A. More stretch → more vagal firing and less sympathetic firing: a slower heart and wider arterioles bring pressure down. 3. Why can't the baroreflex alone explain long-term high blood pressure? A. The baroreceptors stop firing completely after a few hours B. The baroreceptors reset to defend whatever pressure persists C. The baroreceptors only respond to falls in blood pressure D. The baroreceptors sense oxygen, not the stretch of the artery Show answer and explanation Answer: B. After days at a higher pressure, the reflex defends the new level. The kidneys set long-term pressure. 4. Low-pressure receptors: sensing how full the circulation is There are also stretch receptors in low-pressure sites: the walls of the atria (where the venae cavae and pulmonary veins enter) and the pulmonary vessels. Because pressure there depends mainly on how much blood fills them, they act as volume receptors. The lecture describes two types in the atria: Type A Type B Fires during Atrial systole (contraction) Later, during atrial filling Mainly signals Heart rate How full the atria are When the atria are overfilled (too much volume), the type B receptors fire more. This triggers responses that get rid of volume: Vasodilation and a fall in blood pressure. Less ADH is released, so the kidneys excrete more water (the lecture also notes less ACTH). The stretched atria release ANP, which does the same. ( → Neural and Hormonal Control, §5) A rise in heart rate (the Bainbridge reflex), which pumps the extra blood onwards, out of the congested veins. Notice the surprise: this reflex lowers pressure but speeds the heart. That is one of the lecture’s “exceptions” ( §7). Source note: In humans, the Bainbridge reflex is weak and usually outweighed by the arterial baroreflex; whether the heart rate rises or falls with extra volume depends on the balance between the two. 5. Chemoreceptors Peripheral chemoreceptors sit in small clusters of cells, the carotid bodies and aortic bodies, close to the baroreceptors. They sense low O₂, high CO₂ and high H⁺ in arterial blood. Their main job is to stimulate breathing. They also raise sympathetic drive to the vessels, which helps hold up blood pressure when it is very low, because low flow starves the chemoreceptor cells of oxygen. Don't mix them up: the carotid sinus (baroreceptor) senses stretch; the carotid body (chemoreceptor) senses blood chemistry. The vasomotor centre also senses chemistry directly. If its own blood supply falls, the local hypoxia and high CO₂ stimulate it, and sympathetic firing rises. That is the basis of the Cushing reflex ( §6). 6. The Cushing reflex: when the brain is squeezed When intracranial pressure rises (from a tumour, a blood clot or hydrocephalus), it compresses the brain's vessels and cerebral perfusion pressure falls. ( → Cerebral Circulation, §4) Blood flow to the medulla falls, so the vasomotor centre becomes hypoxic and hypercapnic. That directly stimulates the vasomotor centre: sympathetic firing surges, and arterial pressure rises sharply, forcing blood back into the brain. The arterial baroreceptors detect the high pressure and trigger reflex bradycardia. Pressure on the brainstem also disturbs breathing. Cushing triad: hypertension + bradycardia + irregular breathing. It is a late, dangerous sign that the brain is being compressed. 7. Other inputs, and the lecture’s “exceptions” The vasomotor centre doesn't only listen to pressure and chemical sensors. The lecture lists other influences: Input Effect on blood pressure Anger, excitement (cortex → hypothalamus) Rises, with a faster heart Sharp, brief pain Rises Prolonged, severe pain Falls: vasodilation, and possibly fainting Fear and grief (cortex) Falls, with a slower heart: the basis of an emotional faint Lung inflation (vagal sensory fibres) Falls: vasodilation Hypoxia, high CO₂ (direct, on the vasomotor centre) Rises Usually, heart rate and blood pressure move in the same direction in these responses. But a reflex that is correcting a pressure change moves heart rate in the opposite direction. Always start with the stimulus: Stimulus Blood pressure Heart rate Why Haemorrhage Low Fast Baroreflex: less stretch → more sympathetic Raised ICP (Cushing) High Slow Brainstem ischaemia raises pressure; baroreflex slows the heart Overfilled atria (Bainbridge) Lower Fast Volume receptors: dilate and pump the excess onwards Understanding Check: Sections 4–7 1. How does the body respond when the atria are overstretched by too much volume? A. More ADH and less ANP, so the kidneys keep salt and water B. More aldosterone, so the kidneys keep sodium and water C. More renin, so angiotensin II constricts the arterioles D. Less ADH and more ANP, so the kidneys lose salt and water Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Atrial stretch signals overfilling, so the responses all reduce volume. 2. What is the difference between the carotid sinus and the carotid body? A. The sinus senses O₂, CO₂ and H⁺; the body senses arterial stretch B. The sinus senses arterial stretch; the body senses O₂, CO₂ and H⁺ C. Both sense stretch, but the sinus responds only to falls in pressure D. Both sense chemistry, but the body sends signals through the vagus Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Sinus = baroreceptor. Body = chemoreceptor. Both send signals through IX. 3. A patient with a head injury develops hypertension and a heart rate of 45. What explains the slow heart? A. The injured brain directly stimulates the SA node to fire more slowly B. Low intracranial pressure removes the sympathetic drive to the heart C. Atrial stretch receptors respond to the rise in blood volume D. Baroreceptors respond to the high pressure caused by brainstem ischaemia Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Rising ICP makes the vasomotor centre ischaemic, which raises arterial pressure. The baroreflex then slows the heart: the Cushing reflex. 8. Chapter Essentials Reflex: stimulus → receptor → afferent nerve → medulla (NTS) → autonomic nerves → heart and vessels. Mostly negative feedback. Baroreceptors sense stretch: carotid sinus (IX) and aortic arch (X). Pressure rises → more firing → more vagal, less sympathetic → slower heart, dilation → pressure falls. Pressure falls → the reverse: tachycardia and constriction. The baroreflex buffers sudden changes; it resets with sustained changes and can't replace lost volume. Atrial volume receptors (type A in atrial systole, type B in filling): overfilling → dilation, less ADH, more ANP, faster heart ( Bainbridge). Chemoreceptors (carotid and aortic bodies) sense low O₂, high CO₂ and H⁺; mainly drive breathing. Cushing reflex: raised ICP → medullary ischaemia → hypertension → baroreflex bradycardia; irregular breathing. A late sign. Other inputs: anger and sharp pain raise pressure; fear, grief, prolonged pain and lung inflation lower it. Start with the stimulus: haemorrhage = low BP + fast heart; Cushing = high BP + slow heart; Bainbridge = lower BP + fast heart.","gravity":"Gravity, Posture and Acceleration 1. Gravity adds pressure below the heart and takes it away above When you stand, the blood in your vessels forms a tall column. Like water in a tall tank, the pressure is higher at the bottom and lower at the top. This extra pressure comes from the weight of the blood, not from the heart. How much? About 0.77 mmHg for every centimetre of height (ΔP = ρgh). ( → Venous Return) Standing, with no compensation (the lecture's figures): Level About how far from the heart Mean arterial pressure Head 45 cm above About 60 mmHg (about 35 less) Heart — About 93 mmHg (120/80) Feet 115 cm below About 180 mmHg (about 90 more) The same applies to the veins: venous pressure in the feet rises to about 90 mmHg, while the veins of the neck collapse. Pressures in a standing body (no compensation) 45 cm −35 115 cm +90 Head ≈ 60 mmHg Heart level ≈ 93 mmHg (120/80) Leg veins stretch ≈ 500 mL pools Feet ≈ 180 mmHg Mean arterial pressure at each level. Gravity adds about 0.77 mmHg for every centimetre below the heart and removes the same above it. 2. What standing up does to the circulation Stand up quickly, and this is what happens in the first few seconds: The high pressure in the legs stretches the leg veins, which are very compliant. About 500 mL of blood pools in them and in the abdominal veins. Higher capillary pressure in the legs pushes fluid out into the tissues. ( → Oedema) Less blood returns to the heart, so venous return and end-diastolic volume fall. By the Frank–Starling mechanism, stroke volume falls, so cardiac output and blood pressure fall. ( → Cardiac Output) Blood pressure at head level is already lower because of gravity, so cerebral blood flow falls. You may feel light-headed or see the world grey out for a moment. 3. How the body rescues blood pressure The baroreflex (seconds). The carotid sinus and aortic arch sit above the heart, so they feel the fall in pressure first. ( → Reflexes, §3) Baroreceptors fire less → the vasomotor centre is released. Sympathetic firing rises, vagal firing falls: Faster heart (chronotropic) and stronger beats (inotropic). Arterioles constrict, raising peripheral resistance. Veins constrict, pushing pooled blood back towards the heart. Cardiac output and blood pressure recover. That is why your heart rate rises by 10–20 beats/min when you stand. The muscle pump. Every time the leg muscles contract, they squeeze the deep veins, and the one-way valves send blood upwards. ( → Venous Return) Hormones (minutes to hours). Renin, angiotensin II and aldosterone help hold pressure and volume if you stay upright. ( → Neural and Hormonal Control, §5) Why the brain is partly protected Standing lowers arterial pressure at head level, but the brain has three extra defences: Venous pressure in the head falls too. Cerebral perfusion pressure is the difference between arterial and venous pressure, so it falls less than arterial pressure does. Intracranial pressure falls, so there is less pressure squeezing the cerebral vessels. ( → Cerebral Circulation, §4) The small fall in flow lets CO₂ rise and O₂ fall locally, which dilates the cerebral arterioles. ( → Cerebral Circulation, §3) So cerebral blood flow falls only a little. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. A person stands with mean arterial pressure of 93 mmHg at heart level. Why is the pressure in the foot arteries about 180 mmHg? A. The leg arteries constrict and raise their own pressure B. The weight of the blood column adds pressure below the heart C. The heart pumps harder to push blood down to the feet D. The leg veins return blood faster, raising arterial pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The extra pressure comes from gravity (about 0.77 mmHg per cm), not from the heart. 2. Why does cardiac output fall briefly when you stand up suddenly? A. The baroreceptors fire faster and slow the heart down B. The arterioles in the legs dilate and lower peripheral resistance C. Gravity makes the ventricles contract less strongly D. Blood pools in the leg veins, lowering venous return and stroke volume Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Less blood returns to the heart, so end-diastolic volume and stroke volume fall (Frank–Starling). 3. Why does cerebral blood flow fall only a little on standing? A. Venous and intracranial pressures fall too, and the arterioles dilate B. The Circle of Willis pumps extra blood up to the brain C. Gravity does not affect blood pressure above the heart D. The vertebral arteries constrict to hold blood in the brain Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Perfusion pressure depends on the arterial–venous difference, ICP falls, and local CO₂ dilates the vessels. 4. When compensation fails: prolonged standing and fainting Standing still for a long time (a soldier on parade) defeats these defences: With no walking, there is no muscle pump. Blood keeps pooling in the legs, and fluid keeps filtering into the tissues. Venous return, cardiac output and cerebral blood flow fall. The person faints. Fainting is protective. Falling flat brings the head level with the heart, so gravity no longer drains blood away from the brain, and consciousness returns quickly. Holding a fainted person upright is dangerous. Vasovagal syncope (the common faint). Strong emotion (fear, the sight of blood), pain, heat or prolonged standing can trigger a sudden reflex: sympathetic tone switches off (vessels dilate) and vagal tone switches on (the heart slows). Blood pressure and cerebral flow drop, and the person faints. ( → Reflexes, §7) Notice the difference from ordinary standing: ordinary standing gives a faster heart; vasovagal syncope gives a slower heart and dilated vessels. The lecture lists other types of syncope: Micturition syncope: fainting while passing urine, especially after standing up at night. Carotid sinus syncope: pressure on a sensitive carotid sinus (a tight collar, turning the head) triggers a strong baroreflex, with bradycardia and vasodilation. 5. Orthostatic (postural) hypotension Orthostatic hypotension is a fall in blood pressure on standing that the body fails to correct. It is defined as a sustained fall of at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within 3 minutes of standing. Why does it happen? Something stops the sympathetic response from working: Autonomic neuropathy damages the sympathetic nerves, for example in diabetes (or, classically, syphilis). Sympatholytic drugs block the sympathetic response. Autonomic failure, including during and after surgery and anaesthesia. Low blood volume (dehydration, bleeding) leaves less blood to work with. Prolonged bed rest blunts the reflexes. What gives it away? In a patient with autonomic failure, blood pressure falls on standing but the heart rate doesn't rise, and noradrenaline levels don't rise either. The baroreceptors detect the fall, but the signal never reaches the vessels. Understanding Check: Sections 4–5 1. Why does a soldier standing motionless on parade sometimes faint? A. Standing still raises intracranial pressure and compresses the brain B. The baroreceptors fire faster when the body is completely still C. Without the muscle pump, blood pools in the legs and cerebral flow falls D. Holding the breath for long periods lowers the blood's oxygen content Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Pooling lowers venous return and cardiac output. Fainting brings the head to heart level and restores flow. 2. How does vasovagal syncope differ from the normal response to standing? A. The heart slows and the vessels dilate, instead of speeding up and constricting B. The heart speeds up and the vessels constrict, as in normal standing C. Only the heart rate changes, while the vessels stay the same D. Blood pressure rises suddenly, and the high pressure causes fainting Show answer and explanation Answer: A. In vasovagal syncope, sympathetic tone is withdrawn and vagal tone rises. 3. A man with diabetic autonomic neuropathy feels dizzy on standing. His blood pressure falls but his heart rate stays at 72. Why? A. His baroreceptors fire faster when he stands, slowing his heart B. Damaged sympathetic nerves can't raise heart rate or constrict vessels C. His blood volume is too high for the heart rate to rise D. His vagus nerve has been cut, so his heart rate is fixed Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The baroreceptors detect the fall, but the sympathetic pathway to the heart and vessels is damaged. 6. Acceleration: when “gravity” gets stronger In a fast aircraft or a roller coaster, acceleration acts like stronger gravity. It is measured in g (1 g = normal gravity). Positive g (+g): blood is thrown towards the feet. For example, pulling out of a dive. Blood pools in the legs and abdomen; head-level pressure falls. At first, the same defences as standing help: venous and intracranial pressures fall, and sympathetic drive raises heart rate and contractility. Cardiac output is held for a while. Above about 5 g, cerebral perfusion fails: vision fails first (\"grey-out\"), then blackout, then unconsciousness. Why does vision fail first? The retina is supplied at head level by arteries that must push against the pressure inside the eye. When arterial pressure at head level falls, retinal flow is the first to stop. Negative g (−g): blood is thrown towards the head. For example, an outside loop. Arterial and venous pressures in the head rise. The head and neck vessels congest: bleeding around the eyes, a throbbing headache, mental confusion and reddened vision (\" red-out \"). Why don't the brain vessels burst? Intracranial pressure rises at the same time (from the venous congestion), so the pressure outside the vessels rises with the pressure inside. The transmural pressure stays small. ( → Local Control, §3) How pilots cope with +g: Anti-g suits inflate around the abdomen and legs, squeezing them with a force matched to the g. This reduces pooling and raises venous return. Lying horizontal (as astronauts do during launch) makes the blood column across the body short, so much higher g is tolerated. g is tolerated better when it acts across the body rather than along it. Understanding Check: Section 6 1. A pilot pulling out of a dive at 6 g loses vision before losing consciousness. Why? A. The retina needs less oxygen than the rest of the brain B. Blood is thrown towards the head, congesting the eyes C. The intraocular pressure falls, letting the retina detach D. Retinal flow stops first as head-level arterial pressure falls Show answer and explanation Answer: D. At +g, blood moves to the feet. Retinal arteries must overcome the pressure in the eye, so their flow fails first. 2. In negative g, why don't the cerebral vessels rupture despite the high pressure inside them? A. The cerebral arteries constrict strongly enough to resist any pressure B. Blood is thrown towards the feet, emptying the cerebral vessels C. Intracranial pressure rises too, keeping the pressure across the wall small D. The Circle of Willis diverts the extra blood away from the brain Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Pressure outside the vessels rises with the pressure inside, so the transmural pressure stays low. 3. How does an anti-g suit protect a pilot? A. It squeezes the head and neck, stopping blood from leaving the brain B. It squeezes the abdomen and legs, reducing pooling and raising venous return C. It warms the body, dilating the cerebral arterioles to raise flow D. It raises oxygen pressure in the lungs to protect the brain Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Compressing the lower body stops blood pooling there, so venous return and cardiac output are maintained. 7. Chapter Essentials Gravity adds about 0.77 mmHg per cm below the heart and removes it above. Standing: about 60 mmHg at the head, 93 at the heart, 180 at the feet. Standing → about 500 mL pools in the legs → venous return, stroke volume and cardiac output fall → cerebral flow falls. Rescue: baroreflex (faster, stronger heart; arterioles and veins constrict), muscle pump, then renin–angiotensin–aldosterone. The brain is protected because venous pressure and ICP fall too, and local CO₂ dilates its arterioles. Prolonged standing without the muscle pump → pooling → fainting, which restores flow by bringing the head to heart level. Vasovagal syncope: sympathetic withdrawal + vagal activation → dilation and bradycardia. Also micturition and carotid sinus syncope. Orthostatic hypotension: fall of ≥20 systolic or ≥10 diastolic within 3 min. Causes: autonomic neuropathy (diabetes), drugs, surgery, low volume. Heart rate and noradrenaline fail to rise. +g: blood to the feet → grey-out, blackout, unconsciousness above about 5 g. −g: blood to the head → congestion, red-out; vessels protected by the rise in ICP. Anti-g suits compress the abdomen and legs, so less blood pools there; lying horizontal shortens the column of blood between heart and head, so much higher g is tolerated.","shock":"Circulatory Shock 1. What is shock? Shock is a failure of the circulation to deliver enough blood to the tissues to keep them working normally. If it goes on long enough, the tissues are damaged. Shock is not defined by a blood pressure number. Recall mean arterial pressure ≈ cardiac output × total peripheral resistance. ( → Blood Pressure) Early in shock, the body constricts its arterioles hard, so blood pressure can look nearly normal while cardiac output, and the flow to many organs, has already fallen. Every type of shock comes down to one of four failures: Type What has failed Examples (from the lecture) Hypovolaemic (low volume) Not enough blood volume Haemorrhage, trauma, surgery, burns, severe diarrhoea and vomiting Cardiogenic The heart can't pump Myocardial infarction, heart failure, arrhythmias Distributive (low resistance) Vessels dilate massively, so blood pools and is maldistributed Septic shock, anaphylaxis, neurogenic shock Obstructive Blood flow is blocked Cardiac tamponade, tension pneumothorax, pulmonary embolism, cardiac tumour 2. Haemorrhage: the model for all shock The lecture uses haemorrhage to illustrate the events in every kind of shock. Follow it link by link: Blood is lost, so blood volume falls. Less blood fills the veins, so venous return falls. ( → Venous Return) Less filling, so stroke volume falls (Frank–Starling), and cardiac output falls. ( → Cardiac Output) Blood pressure falls, and the tissues are underperfused. Everything else, the signs, the compensation and the deterioration, follows from this chain. 3. What you see, and why Sign Why it happens Low blood pressure Low cardiac output. (Early on, constriction may hide it.) Fast, weak pulse Baroreceptors fire less, so the vasomotor centre is released: sympathetic drive gives reflex tachycardia. The pulse is weak because stroke volume is small. ( → Reflexes, §3) Pale, cool, clammy skin Sympathetic vasoconstriction is generalised but most marked in the skin; sweating adds the clamminess. The heart and brain are spared. ( → Cutaneous Circulation, §2) Low urine output Constriction of renal arterioles lowers GFR; the kidneys also keep Na⁺ and water. Urea builds up in the blood. Rapid breathing Low flow starves the chemoreceptors of oxygen, and lactic acid builds up; both drive breathing. ( → Reflexes, §5) Intense thirst Angiotensin II and ADH rise. Restless and anxious, later dull and apathetic Early: sympathetic arousal (restlessness also works the muscle pump). Later: cerebral ischaemia. Lactic acidosis Without enough oxygen, cells switch to anaerobic glycolysis, making lactic acid. 4. How the body fights back Within seconds: nerves. Falling blood pressure unloads the baroreceptors, and the chemoreceptors are stimulated. Sympathetic discharge rises: Heart: faster and stronger. Arterioles: constrict everywhere except the heart and brain, raising resistance and redirecting flow to them. Veins: constrict, squeezing out stored blood. The splanchnic veins, the skin and even the pulmonary veins give up blood to the systemic circulation. ( → Splanchnic Circulation, §3) Adrenal medulla: releases adrenaline and noradrenaline, reinforcing all of this. Within minutes to hours: hormones. Renin → angiotensin II → aldosterone: constriction, salt and water retention, thirst. ADH (vasopressin): water retention and constriction. ACTH → cortisol. ( → Neural and Hormonal Control, §6) Within hours: fluid moves into the blood. Arteriolar constriction and low pressure lower capillary hydrostatic pressure, so the balance at the capillaries tips towards reabsorption. Fluid moves from the interstitial spaces into the blood, partly refilling it. ( → Microcirculation) Over days to weeks: full repair. If bleeding stops, blood volume is restored in about 12–72 hours, mainly by drawing in fluid and retaining salt and water. That added fluid dilutes the blood, so plasma proteins and haemoglobin concentration fall. Erythropoietin from the kidney drives new red cell production; haemoglobin returns to normal in about 4–5 weeks. The lecture's question: Hb and haematocrit after losing whole blood Immediately after a bleed, Hb and haematocrit are normal. Whole blood is lost, so red cells and plasma are lost in the same proportion: what remains has the same concentration. They fall later, as interstitial fluid (and any IV fluid) dilutes the remaining red cells. So a normal Hb doesn't rule out a large, recent bleed. Understanding Check: Sections 1–4 1. Why can blood pressure look nearly normal early in shock? A. Cardiac output rises early in shock, offsetting the fall in volume B. The blood becomes more viscous, raising resistance and pressure C. Shock affects only the veins, not the arteries, at first D. Sympathetic constriction raises resistance, hiding a fall in cardiac output Show answer and explanation Answer: D. MAP ≈ CO × TPR. A rising TPR can hold MAP up while CO and organ flow fall. 2. Why is the skin pale and cool in haemorrhagic shock, while the brain stays perfused? A. The skin loses blood first, because its vessels are closest to the wound B. Sympathetic constriction is marked in the skin but spares the heart and brain C. The brain constricts its own arteries to hold blood inside the skull D. Low body temperature constricts the skin vessels in all patients Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Generalised constriction redirects flow to the heart and brain, which have little sympathetic constriction. 3. A man has lost 1.5 L of blood in the last 30 minutes. His haemoglobin is normal. Why? A. The spleen has released enough stored red cells to replace those lost B. Erythropoietin has already restored the red cell count C. Red cells and plasma were lost together, so the concentration hasn't changed yet D. The kidneys have retained enough water to restore the concentration Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Whole blood loss keeps the ratio the same. Hb falls later, as fluid moves in and dilutes the blood. 5. When shock feeds on itself: progressive and irreversible shock If the blood loss is large or prolonged, the compensation fails, and several vicious cycles (positive feedback) take over. Each one makes perfusion worse, which makes the cycle turn faster: Progressive shock: the vicious cycles Low cardiac output and blood pressure 1 · Heart weakens low coronary flow and acidosis 2 · Brainstem fails heart slows and vessels dilate 3 · Lactic acidosis weak heart; vessels ignore adrenaline 4 · Capillary stasis blood stagnates and leaks: volume falls Each loop starts from poor perfusion and ends by lowering cardiac output and blood pressure further, so the cycle turns faster. The heart fails. Low blood pressure means low coronary flow. ( → Coronary Circulation, §3) Ischaemia and acidosis weaken the heart muscle, so cardiac output falls further. The vasomotor centre fails. Prolonged cerebral ischaemia depresses it. Sympathetic drive collapses: the heart slows and the vessels dilate. Blood pressure and brain flow fall further. Acidosis. Lactic acid depresses the heart and makes the vessels stop responding to adrenaline and noradrenaline. Capillary stasis and leak. Late in shock, the precapillary sphincters relax but the venules stay constricted. Blood enters the capillaries but can't leave, so it stagnates, capillary hydrostatic pressure rises, and fluid is lost into the tissues. Volume falls further. The kidneys are damaged. Prolonged low flow can cause acute tubular damage. Irreversible shock: eventually, even if the lost blood is fully replaced, the circulation doesn't recover: resistance stays low, the heart stays weak, and the patient dies. The tissues, especially the heart and brain, have been too badly damaged. 6. The other types of shock Hypovolaemic shock without visible bleeding. Bleeding can be concealed: A fractured femur can hide over a litre of blood in the thigh. A ruptured spleen or a ruptured ectopic pregnancy bleeds into the abdomen. Other causes of volume loss (the lecture's list): Burns: plasma seeps from the burnt skin, but the red cells stay behind. So the blood becomes concentrated (haemoconcentration): Hb and haematocrit rise, the opposite of haemorrhage. Traumatic shock: crushed muscle releases myoglobin, which damages the kidneys. Surgical shock: bleeding and dehydration. Diarrhoea, uncontrolled diabetes and adrenal insufficiency: salt and water loss. Cardiogenic shock. The volume is normal, but the heart can't pump it, so cardiac output falls (myocardial infarction, heart failure, arrhythmias). Blood backs up behind the failing ventricle, so venous pressure and the JVP are often high, unlike haemorrhage. ( → Heart Failure) Distributive (low-resistance) shock. The blood volume is normal, but massive vasodilation lets blood pool and flow is maldistributed: Type Cause Key feature Neurogenic Sudden loss of sympathetic tone (spinal cord injury, spinal anaesthesia; the lecture also includes fainting from strong emotion) Vessels dilate; the heart may be slow Septic Bacterial toxins Vasodilation and leaky capillaries; skin may be warm early ( → Cutaneous Circulation, §2) Anaphylactic An antigen–antibody reaction releases histamine Widespread dilation and leaky capillaries Obstructive shock. Something blocks filling or outflow: fluid compressing the heart ( tamponade), a tension pneumothorax squeezing the great veins, or a pulmonary embolism blocking the pulmonary artery. Understanding Check: Sections 5–6 1. In progressive shock, why does failure of the vasomotor centre make things worse? A. Sympathetic drive collapses, so the heart slows and the vessels dilate B. The vasomotor centre starts releasing more adrenaline into the blood C. The baroreceptors stop firing, so blood pressure rises suddenly D. Breathing stops, so the lungs no longer supply oxygen Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Prolonged cerebral ischaemia depresses the vasomotor centre, removing the constriction that was holding up pressure. 2. Late in shock, the precapillary sphincters relax while the venules stay constricted. What follows? A. Blood flows faster, washing out the lactic acid from the tissues B. Capillary pressure falls, so fluid moves back into the blood C. The venules pump blood back to the heart more efficiently D. Blood stagnates, capillary pressure rises and fluid leaks out Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Blood enters the capillaries but can't leave, so filtration rises and more volume is lost. 3. A patient with severe burns has a raised haematocrit. Why is this different from haemorrhage? A. Burns lose red cells but not plasma, so the blood becomes diluted B. Burns stimulate erythropoietin at once, so new red cells appear C. Burns lose plasma but not red cells, so the blood becomes concentrated D. Burns lose whole blood, which always raises the haematocrit Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Plasma seeps from the burnt skin while red cells stay behind: haemoconcentration. 4. How does septic shock differ from haemorrhagic shock in its early stage? A. Volume is low and vessels constrict, so the skin is always cold B. Volume is normal but vessels dilate and leak, so the skin may be warm C. The heart stops pumping, so blood backs up into the veins D. A blood clot blocks the pulmonary artery, so filling falls Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Bacterial toxins cause vasodilation and leaky capillaries: a distributive shock. 7. Chapter Essentials Shock = tissue perfusion too low for normal function, which damages tissues. Blood pressure can look normal early. Four types: hypovolaemic (volume), cardiogenic (pump), distributive (vessels dilate), obstructive (flow blocked). Haemorrhage chain: volume ↓ → venous return ↓ → stroke volume ↓ → cardiac output ↓ → perfusion ↓. Signs and their causes: low BP and a weak pulse (small stroke volume); fast pulse (baroreflex); pale, cool skin and low urine (sympathetic constriction of skin and kidney vessels); rapid breathing and lactic acidosis (too little oxygen, so cells switch to anaerobic glycolysis); thirst (angiotensin II and ADH); restless (sympathetic arousal), then dull (cerebral ischaemia). Compensation: baroreflex and chemoreceptors (seconds), adrenal medulla, renin–angiotensin–aldosterone, ADH, cortisol (minutes to hours), interstitial fluid moving into the blood (hours). Repair: volume in 12–72 h; Hb by erythropoietin in 4–5 weeks. Hb is normal straight after a bleed, then falls with dilution. Progressive shock is a set of vicious circles: low coronary flow weakens the heart; brain ischaemia shuts down the vasomotor centre, so vessels dilate and the heart slows; acidosis stops vessels responding to adrenaline; capillaries stagnate and leak; the kidneys are damaged. Irreversible shock: no recovery even when volume is replaced. Burns concentrate the blood (plasma loss); trauma releases myoglobin; bleeding can be concealed (femur, spleen, ectopic). Distributive: neurogenic, septic (warm skin early), anaphylactic (histamine). Cardiogenic: high venous pressure. Obstructive: tamponade, tension pneumothorax, pulmonary embolism.","heart-failure":"Heart Failure Physiology 1. What is heart failure? Heart failure is when the heart can't pump enough blood for the body's needs, or can only do so at abnormally high filling pressures. Both parts matter. Some patients have a near-normal cardiac output at rest, but only because their filling pressures are very high, and that high pressure is what causes their symptoms. So heart failure is not simply \"low cardiac output\". Every failing heart causes two kinds of problem: Forward failure: too little blood goes out, so the organs are underperfused. This causes tiredness, poor exercise tolerance, and kidneys that hold on to salt and water. Backward failure: blood backs up behind the failing chamber, so the veins upstream become congested. This causes fluid in the lungs or the body. These are two sides of the same failing pump, not two different diseases. 2. Systolic and diastolic failure: can't squeeze, or can't fill? Systolic failure Diastolic failure The problem The ventricle can't squeeze properly The ventricle can't relax and fill properly (it is stiff) Ejection fraction Reduced (it ejects a smaller fraction) Often preserved (normal fraction of a small volume) Filling pressure High, because blood is left behind after each beat High, because a stiff ventricle needs high pressure to fill Typical causes Heart attack, cardiomyopathy Long-standing high blood pressure, ageing, hypertrophy Both raise filling pressure, so both cause congestion. A normal ejection fraction does not rule out heart failure: a stiff ventricle can eject a normal percentage of a small, high-pressure fill. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §7) A fast heart makes both worse. Diastole shortens, and a stiff ventricle, which already fills slowly, gets even less time to fill. ( → Cardiac Output, §2) 3. Left versus right failure: where does the blood back up? Blood backs up into the circulation that feeds the failing ventricle. Figure 1. Left versus right failure: where the blood backs up Left ventricle fails Left atrial pressure ↑ Lung vein and capillary pressure ↑ Fluid filters into the lungs Breathlessness, worse lying flat (pulmonary oedema) Right ventricle fails Right atrial pressure ↑ Systemic vein pressure ↑ Congested liver and tissues Raised JVP, swollen liver, ascites, swollen ankles Blood backs up into the circulation that feeds the failing ventricle. Left failure is the commonest cause of right failure. Left heart failure → the lungs. The left ventricle can't empty, so pressure rises in the left atrium and the lung veins. The lung capillaries filter fluid into the lungs faster than the lymphatics can clear it. This is pulmonary oedema, and it causes breathlessness. ( → Oedema, §4) Why worse lying flat (orthopnoea)? Lying down, blood that pooled in the legs returns to the chest. The failing left ventricle can't pump the extra on, so lung pressure rises further. Patients prop themselves up on pillows to breathe. Right heart failure → the body. The right ventricle can't empty, so pressure rises in the right atrium and all the systemic veins: Raised JVP, the visible sign of high right atrial pressure ( → Pulses and JVP, §7) Swollen, tender liver, congested with blood Ascites: fluid in the abdomen Swollen ankles: dependent, pitting oedema The two often go together. The commonest cause of right failure is left failure: high lung pressure strains the right ventricle, which has to pump into it. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why can a patient have heart failure with a normal ejection fraction? A. A stiff ventricle ejects a normal fraction of a small volume at high pressure B. A normal ejection fraction always means a normal cardiac output C. Ejection fraction is measured only in the right ventricle D. The heart compensates fully by beating more slowly Show answer and explanation Answer: A. In diastolic failure the ventricle fills poorly and needs high pressure. The fraction ejected is normal, but filling is not. 2. A patient with left ventricular failure becomes breathless. What is the mechanism? A. Low blood pressure reduces oxygen delivery to the lungs B. Pressure backs up into the lung veins, so fluid leaks into the lungs C. The right ventricle pumps too much blood into the lungs D. Fluid in the abdomen pushes up on the diaphragm Show answer and explanation Answer: B. High left atrial pressure raises lung capillary pressure, causing pulmonary oedema. 3. Which finding points to right-sided rather than left-sided failure? A. Breathlessness when lying flat B. Fluid in the lungs on a chest X-ray C. Breathlessness on exertion D. A raised JVP with swollen ankles Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Right failure raises systemic venous pressure: a raised JVP, liver congestion and ankle oedema. The others reflect lung congestion from left failure. 4. What does the body do about it, and why does that make it worse? When the heart pumps too little, the kidneys and baroreceptors sense a low arterial pressure (the arteries are \"underfilled\") and switch on three rescue systems: System What it does Short-term help Long-term harm Sympathetic nerves Faster, stronger heart; tighter arterioles Holds up blood pressure and output Fast rate shortens filling; tight arterioles raise afterload; the heart muscle is overworked Renin–angiotensin– aldosterone (RAAS) Constricts vessels; the kidneys keep salt and water Raises blood volume and pressure More volume → more congestion and oedema; higher afterload ADH The kidneys keep water Raises blood volume Worsens fluid overload The vicious circle: the failing heart → the body senses \"too little blood\" → it keeps more fluid and tightens vessels → the heart faces more filling pressure and more afterload → it fails further. The paradox: a patient can be waterlogged (oedema everywhere) while the kidneys still act as if the body is short of blood. The total body fluid is high, but the arteries are underfilled because the pump is weak. ( → Shock) Frank–Starling helps less and less. At first, extra filling stretches the ventricle and raises stroke volume. But a failing ventricle sits on a flat, low curve, so extra filling mainly raises pressure and congestion. ( → Cardiac Output, §5) Remodelling. Over months, the ventricle dilates or thickens and becomes less efficient: another long-term harm of chronic stimulation. Understanding Check: Section 4 1. Why do the kidneys retain salt and water in heart failure, even when the patient is oedematous? A. The kidneys are damaged directly by the failing heart B. They sense underfilled arteries from the weak pump and activate RAAS C. Oedema fluid blocks the kidneys' filtration D. The patient drinks more water because of thirst alone Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Low effective arterial filling triggers RAAS and ADH, so the kidneys keep fluid despite the total body excess. 2. How does sympathetic activation eventually harm the failing heart? A. It slows the heart, so cardiac output falls B. It dilates the arteries, so blood pressure falls too far C. It raises afterload and heart rate, increasing the heart's work D. It lowers blood volume through more urine production Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Tighter arterioles raise afterload, and a faster rate shortens filling and raises oxygen demand, so the weak heart works harder. 3. Why does giving extra fluid help a failing heart only a little? A. Its Frank–Starling curve is low and flat B. Extra fluid lowers the heart's contractility C. The extra fluid goes straight to the kidneys D. Extra fluid lowers the heart rate too much Show answer and explanation Answer: A. On a flat curve, more filling adds little stroke volume but raises pressure and congestion. 5. Chapter Essentials Heart failure: the heart can't meet the body's needs, or only at high filling pressures. It is not just low output. Forward failure = too little output. Backward failure = congestion upstream. They are two sides of one problem. Systolic failure = can't squeeze (low EF). Diastolic failure = can't fill (stiff; EF often preserved). Both raise filling pressure. Left failure → lungs: pulmonary oedema, breathlessness, worse lying flat (orthopnoea). Right failure → body: raised JVP, congested liver, ascites, ankle oedema. Left failure is its commonest cause. Rescue systems (sympathetic, RAAS, ADH) hold up pressure at first, but raise afterload and fluid, so they worsen failure. Paradox: the patient is waterlogged, yet the weak pump leaves the arteries underfilled, so the kidneys sense \"too little blood\" and keep retaining salt and water. Frank–Starling gives diminishing returns on a failing, flat curve.","cerebral":"Cerebral Circulation, CSF and the Blood–Brain Barrier 1. Why the brain can't wait for blood The brain is about 2% of body weight but uses about 15% of the cardiac output and about 20% of the body's oxygen at rest. Yet it stores almost no oxygen and very little glucose. So it depends on a continuous blood supply: About 10 seconds without blood flow causes unconsciousness. A few minutes causes permanent damage. Not every part is equally fragile. The cerebral cortex, basal ganglia and thalamus are the most sensitive to low oxygen. The brainstem, which runs breathing and circulation, is more resistant. That is why a patient can survive a severe hypoxic injury with brainstem functions intact. Fuel: glucose supplies about 90% of the brain's energy. Brain cells take it up without needing insulin. So hypoglycaemia hits the brain fast: confusion, then convulsions and coma. In prolonged starvation, the brain switches partly to ketone bodies, made by the liver from fat. Source note: The lecture says that in starvation the brain uses \"other substances (amino acids, lipids and proteins)\". More precisely, the main alternative fuel is ketone bodies, which the liver makes from fatty acids; ordinary fatty acids are not a major brain fuel. 2. Where does the blood come from? The real anatomical arrangement of the Circle of Willis. Image credit Rhcastilhos, Wikimedia Commons · public domain · unmodified. Two pairs of arteries supply the brain: The two internal carotid arteries supply most of the front and middle of the brain. The two vertebral arteries join to form the basilar artery, which supplies the back of the brain and the brainstem. At the base of the brain, these connect in a ring: the Circle of Willis. In principle, if one feeding artery narrows slowly, blood can come round the ring from the others. In practice, the ring is often incomplete or has thin segments, so it can't always compensate. Beyond the ring, there is little backup. The cerebral arteries that leave the circle have few connections (anastomoses) with each other. So when one is blocked suddenly, the tissue it supplies has no other source, and it dies. Artery Main territory Blockage typically affects Anterior cerebral (ACA) Inner (medial) surface of the frontal and parietal lobes The opposite leg Middle cerebral (MCA) Most of the outer (lateral) surface of the hemisphere The opposite face and arm; speech, if the dominant side Posterior cerebral (PCA) Occipital lobe Vision The middle cerebral artery is a branch of the internal carotid. It is not part of the ring itself. 3. How does the brain keep its blood flow steady? Pressure autoregulation — conceptual blood-flow curve Autoregulated range flow nearly constant Below it, flow falls Above it, flow rises ≈ 60 ≈ 150 Mean arterial pressure (mmHg) → Cerebral blood flow → As driving pressure changes, cerebral arterioles adjust resistance to buffer cerebral blood flow. The apparent plateau is approximate, not perfectly flat; its boundaries vary and can shift with disease or chronic hypertension. Autoregulation. When perfusion pressure rises, the cerebral arterioles constrict; when it falls, they dilate. So cerebral blood flow stays nearly constant across a wide range of blood pressures (classically quoted as a mean arterial pressure of about 60–150 mmHg; the limits vary between people). Outside that range, or after a brain injury, flow simply follows pressure. ( → Local Control) Carbon dioxide: the strongest controller. A rise in arterial CO₂ raises H⁺ around the vessels, and the arterioles dilate, so flow rises. A fall in CO₂ constricts them. That is why overbreathing (which blows off CO₂) can make you dizzy: cerebral blood flow falls. Severe hypoxia also dilates cerebral vessels. Active areas get more blood. When a brain region works harder, local signals dilate its arterioles, so flow rises just in that region. This is what brain-activity scans detect. Nerves play only a small part. Cerebral vessels are supplied by sympathetic (adrenergic), cholinergic and sensory nerves. The sensory nerves explain why the vessels and meninges feel pain (a cause of headache), although brain tissue itself does not. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Which part of the brain is most resistant to a period of low oxygen? A. The cerebral cortex, which controls conscious thought B. The basal ganglia, which help to control movement C. The brainstem, which controls breathing and circulation D. The thalamus, which relays sensory signals to the cortex Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The cortex, basal ganglia and thalamus are the most sensitive. The brainstem tolerates hypoxia better. 2. Why can a sudden blockage of the middle cerebral artery kill brain tissue, even with a Circle of Willis? A. Beyond the circle, cerebral arteries have few connections with each other B. The middle cerebral artery carries blood away from the brain C. The Circle of Willis closes completely during a stroke D. The middle cerebral artery has no branches into the brain Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The ring sits upstream. The arteries leaving it have little anastomosis, so a blocked branch leaves its territory without supply. 3. A student hyperventilates and feels dizzy. Why? A. High CO₂ dilates the cerebral arterioles, so brain pressure rises B. Low oxygen in the blood constricts the cerebral arterioles C. Fast breathing raises blood pressure above the autoregulation range D. Low CO₂ constricts the cerebral arterioles, so brain blood flow falls Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Overbreathing blows off CO₂. Cerebral arterioles constrict, and flow drops. 4. A rigid box: intracranial pressure and perfusion The Monro–Kellie doctrine. The skull is a closed, rigid box. It holds three things whose total volume stays nearly constant: Brain, about 1400 g Blood, about 75 mL CSF, about 75 mL If one increases (a bleed, a tumour, swelling), something else must leave. At first, CSF and venous blood are squeezed out, so the pressure barely rises. Once that reserve is used up, even a small extra volume makes the intracranial pressure (ICP) shoot up, and the rising pressure compresses the cerebral vessels. Cerebral perfusion pressure: the pressure that actually drives blood through the brain. CPP = MAP − ICP Normal: MAP 90, ICP 10 → CPP 80 mmHg. ICP rises to 35, MAP still 90 → CPP 55 mmHg. The arm blood pressure hasn't changed, yet the brain is getting less blood. Increasing ICP reduces the driving pressure available to perfuse the brain MAP 90 mmHg in both cases ICP 10 mmHg CPP = 90 − 10 = 80 mmHg ICP 35 mmHg CPP = 90 − 35 = 55 mmHg CPP = MAP − ICP: the higher the ICP, the less pressure is left to perfuse the brain. Illustrative pressure calculation; these bars are not a patient-specific treatment threshold. Cerebral flow additionally depends on resistance. The Cushing reflex: a last-ditch defence. When ICP climbs very high (the lecture gives more than about 33 mmHg), the brainstem itself becomes ischaemic. Its vasomotor centre fires a massive sympathetic discharge that raises arterial pressure, trying to force blood back in. The baroreceptors detect the high pressure and slow the heart. Irregular breathing may follow. So the Cushing triad is high blood pressure, slow heart rate and irregular breathing. It is a late, dangerous sign: the brain is about to be compressed past its limit. ( → Reflexes) 5. The coverings, the ventricles and CSF Meninges and the subarachnoid compartment. Image credit Mysid and Wikimedia contributors, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. The meninges, from outside in: Dura mater: tough, attached to the skull. Arachnoid mater: thin, joined to the brain surface by fine strands (trabeculae). Subarachnoid space: contains CSF and the cerebral blood vessels. Pia mater: clings to the brain surface, following every fold. Source note: The lecture says the subdural space also contains CSF. In normal anatomy, the subdural space is only a potential space; CSF lies in the subarachnoid space. Where CSF is made and where it goes. CSF is made mainly by the choroid plexus in the lateral, third and fourth ventricles. It then flows: Lateral ventricles → through the foramen of Monro (interventricular foramen) → Third ventricle → through the aqueduct of Sylvius (cerebral aqueduct) → Fourth ventricle → out through the foramen of Magendie (middle) and the foramina of Luschka (sides) → Subarachnoid space, around the brain and spinal cord → Absorbed through the arachnoid villi into the venous sinuses, and so into the blood. CSF route from ventricular production to subarachnoid circulation Made mainly by the choroid plexus Lateral ventricles Third ventricle Fourth ventricle Subarachnoid space Venous sinuses (blood) foramen of Monro cerebral aqueduct foramina of Magendie and Luschka absorbed through arachnoid villi Original conceptual diagram; arrows show the direction of bulk flow, not anatomical proportions. CSF value Typical adult Volume About 150 mL Production About 550 mL/day, so it is replaced several times a day Pressure (lumbar, lying down) 70–180 mm CSF Protein About 20 mg/dL, far less than plasma Glucose About 64 mg/dL, roughly two-thirds of blood glucose What CSF does: Cushions the brain against blows to the head. Buoyancy: a brain weighing about 1400 g in air weighs only about 50 g in CSF, so it doesn't crush its own base. Removes waste: the one-way flow into the blood carries metabolites and drugs away. Carries hormones to distant parts of the brain. Hydrocephalus: when CSF builds up. Non-communicating (obstructive): something blocks the pathway inside the ventricles, for example at the narrow aqueduct. The ventricles upstream of the block swell. Communicating: the ventricles connect normally, but the arachnoid villi can't absorb CSF fast enough. Lumbar puncture samples CSF from the subarachnoid space in the lower back, below where the spinal cord ends. If ICP may be raised by a mass, it must be checked first, because releasing pressure from below can pull the brain downwards. Understanding Check: Sections 4–5 1. A patient's MAP is 90 mmHg and ICP rises from 10 to 40 mmHg. What happens to cerebral perfusion pressure? A. It stays at 80 mmHg, because autoregulation keeps it constant B. It falls from 80 to 50 mmHg, although arm blood pressure is unchanged C. It rises from 80 to 130 mmHg, because ICP adds to the MAP D. It falls to 40 mmHg, because CPP always equals ICP Show answer and explanation Answer: B. CPP = MAP − ICP: 90 − 10 = 80, then 90 − 40 = 50 mmHg. 2. In the Cushing reflex, why does the heart rate fall? A. The vagus nerve is crushed directly by the swollen brain B. Raised ICP blocks sympathetic nerves to the heart C. The heart receives less blood when ICP is high D. The baroreceptors respond to the sympathetic rise in blood pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Brainstem ischaemia drives blood pressure up. The baroreflex then slows the heart. 3. A tumour blocks the aqueduct of Sylvius. Which ventricles enlarge? A. The lateral and third ventricles, upstream of the block B. The fourth ventricle only, downstream of the block C. All four ventricles equally, because CSF absorption fails D. None, because CSF can bypass the aqueduct easily Show answer and explanation Answer: A. CSF made in the lateral and third ventricles can't get past the aqueduct, so they swell: non-communicating hydrocephalus. 6. The blood–brain barrier What makes cerebral capillaries different? Image credit David Nascari and Alan Sved, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. In most capillaries, small molecules slip through gaps between the endothelial cells. In the brain, the endothelial cells are sealed together by tight junctions, and there are no gaps or pores. So anything leaving the blood must pass through the cells. This is the blood–brain barrier, and it lies in the capillary endothelium. Surrounding pericytes and astrocyte endfeet help maintain it. Crosses easily Crosses poorly Water, O₂, CO₂ Proteins Lipid-soluble substances (e.g. steroid hormones) Substances bound to plasma proteins Glucose, quickly, but only by a carrier (GLUT1) Most ions and water-soluble molecules Why have it? It keeps the neurons' environment constant, despite swings in blood ions and hormones, and shields them from toxins and circulating antigens. Clinical points: A drug acting on the brain must be able to cross the barrier. The barrier breaks down in infection, injury, tumours and inflammation. The choroid plexus has its own barrier between blood and CSF, but there the seal is formed by the epithelial cells, not the capillaries. 7. Stroke A stroke is a sudden loss of blood supply to part of the brain, causing ischaemia and cell death. The signs depend on which artery is affected ( §2). Ischaemic stroke Haemorrhagic stroke What happens An artery is blocked An artery ruptures and bleeds Causes Atherosclerotic plaque, thrombus or embolus Often a ruptured aneurysm Extra danger — The blood adds volume, so ICP rises ( §4) Understanding Check: Sections 6–7 1. Where does the blood–brain barrier lie? A. In the arachnoid villi that absorb the cerebrospinal fluid B. In the pia mater that covers the surface of the brain C. In the tight junctions of the brain's capillary endothelium D. In the smooth muscle wall of the cerebral arterioles Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Endothelial cells sealed by tight junctions force substances to pass through the cells, not between them. 2. How does glucose cross the blood–brain barrier? A. Slowly, by diffusing between the endothelial cells B. Quickly, by a specific carrier in the endothelial cells C. Only when insulin opens channels in the endothelium D. Bound to plasma proteins that carry it across Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Glucose is water-soluble, so it needs a carrier (GLUT1). The carrier moves it fast, and no insulin is needed. 3. Why can a haemorrhagic stroke damage brain tissue far from the bleeding artery? A. The bleeding artery stops all flow through the Circle of Willis B. The blood dissolves the blood–brain barrier throughout the brain C. The bleed lowers the CSF pressure, so the brain collapses D. The blood adds volume inside the skull, raising ICP everywhere Show answer and explanation Answer: D. In a rigid skull, extra volume raises ICP, lowering perfusion pressure across the whole brain. 8. Chapter Essentials The brain can't store oxygen: about 10 seconds without flow causes unconsciousness. The cortex, basal ganglia and thalamus are most sensitive; the brainstem is more resistant. Fuel: glucose (about 90%), taken up without insulin. Prolonged starvation: ketone bodies. Supply: internal carotids + vertebrals → basilar, joined in the Circle of Willis. Beyond the ring, little anastomosis. Autoregulation keeps flow steady across a wide pressure range. CO₂ is the strongest controller: high CO₂ dilates, low CO₂ constricts. Monro–Kellie: brain + blood + CSF share a rigid skull, so once CSF and venous blood can no longer be pushed out, any extra volume raises ICP steeply. CPP = MAP − ICP, so a rising ICP cuts brain perfusion. Cushing reflex: very high ICP → brainstem ischaemia → hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing. A late sign. CSF: made by the choroid plexus; lateral → Monro → third → Sylvius → fourth → Magendie/Luschka → subarachnoid space → arachnoid villi. About 150 mL, about 550 mL/day. Hydrocephalus: non-communicating (pathway blocked) or communicating (absorption fails). Blood–brain barrier: tight junctions in the capillary endothelium. Water, gases and lipid-soluble substances cross; glucose crosses by carrier; proteins don't. Stroke: ischaemic (blocked) or haemorrhagic (ruptured, often an aneurysm).","coronary":"Coronary Circulation 1. Why does the heart need a separate blood supply? The blood inside the chambers can't nourish the thick ventricular walls: oxygen can only diffuse a fraction of a millimetre. So the heart has its own arteries, the right and left coronary arteries. They arise from the aortic sinuses, the small pouches just above the aortic valve cusps. The heart already takes out most of its oxygen at rest (about 70%, compared with about 25% for the body as a whole). So when it works harder, it can't simply extract more. Coronary blood flow has to rise. ( → Measuring Cardiac Output, §7) Oxygen delivered = coronary blood flow × oxygen content of the blood. If either falls, the heart muscle runs short. 2. Trace coronary inflow and venous drainage Anterior heart and ventricular coronary vessels. Image credit Blausen Medical Communications, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. Left main coronary artery → divides into: the left anterior descending (LAD), running down the front, which supplies the front of the left ventricle and most of the septum; the left circumflex (LCx), curving round the left side, which supplies the side wall of the left ventricle. Right coronary artery (RCA) supplies the right ventricle and, usually, the bottom (inferior) wall of the heart. Dominance just means which artery gives off the posterior descending artery, which supplies the back of the septum. It is the RCA in most people (\"right dominant\"). It does not mean that artery supplies one whole side. Figure 1. Major coronary routes and drainage — simplified flow map Aorta (aortic sinuses) Left main LAD Front of LV, septum Circumflex Side of LV Right coronary Right ventricle, inferior wall Posterior desc. Back of septum Usually from the RCA (right dominant) Drainage Veins → coronary sinus → right atrium Conceptual branches, not an anatomical map of coronary vessel positions. Dominance and individual territories vary. Drainage: most coronary venous blood collects in the coronary sinus, which empties into the right atrium. A little drains straight into the chambers. 3. Why is left ventricular coronary perfusion greatest during diastole? Left coronary blood flow through one cardiac cycle — qualitative waveform Systole Diastole S1 S2 time → Left coronary flow Systole: contracting muscle squeezes the vessels inside it, so flow is low. Diastole: the muscle relaxes, the vessels reopen and flow peaks. Conceptual shape, not measured patient data: left-ventricular compression restricts flow during systole; relaxation allows a marked early-diastolic increase. Right coronary flow may be less strongly diastolic. In systole, the left ventricle squeezes its own arteries shut. The coronary branches run through the muscle wall. When the muscle contracts hard, it compresses them, and left ventricular coronary flow almost stops. In diastole, the muscle relaxes and the arteries open. Aortic pressure is still high, so blood rushes in. So the left ventricle is fed mainly in diastole. The right ventricle, which squeezes far less hard, is fed in both systole and diastole. The pressure that drives left coronary flow ≈ aortic diastolic pressure − left ventricular diastolic pressure. Anything that lowers aortic diastolic pressure (shock, aortic regurgitation) or raises left ventricular filling pressure (heart failure) cuts this driving pressure. Why is the inner layer (subendocardium) most at risk? The inner layer of the left ventricle is squeezed hardest, because it is closest to the high-pressure cavity. So it depends most on diastole, and it is the first to become ischaemic when perfusion falls. In aortic stenosis, for example, very high ventricular pressure both raises oxygen demand and squeezes the inner-layer vessels harder. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why must coronary flow rise when the heart works harder? A. The heart already extracts most of the oxygen from its blood B. The coronary arteries narrow during exercise C. Blood carries more oxygen during exercise D. The heart can store extra oxygen for later use Show answer and explanation Answer: A. With about 70% extraction at rest, there is little more to take, so more oxygen needs more flow. 2. Why does the left ventricle receive most of its coronary flow in diastole? A. Aortic pressure is highest in diastole B. Its contracting muscle squeezes its own arteries in systole C. The coronary arteries close their valves in systole D. The right ventricle takes all the flow during systole Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Contraction compresses the arteries running through the wall. When the muscle relaxes, they open and fill. 3. Which part of the left ventricle becomes ischaemic first when perfusion falls? A. The outer layer, nearest the surface arteries B. The inner layer (subendocardium), squeezed hardest in systole C. The papillary muscles only D. All layers equally at the same time Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The inner layer is compressed most and depends most on diastolic flow. 4. How does coronary flow match changing myocardial work? The heart's oxygen demand rises with heart rate, contractility and wall tension. ( → Measuring Cardiac Output, §7) Working heart muscle opens its own arteries. As it works harder, it releases local signals, chiefly adenosine and also K⁺, low O₂ and nitric oxide from the endothelium. These relax the coronary arterioles, so flow rises to match the work. This is active hyperaemia. ( → Local Control) Sympathetic nerves: two effects, one winner. They can constrict coronary arteries directly (α receptors). But they also make the heart beat faster and harder (β₁), which raises its metabolism and releases dilating metabolites. The dilation wins, so in exercise coronary flow rises. 5. Why can tachycardia cause ischaemia? A fast heart hits the heart muscle from both sides: Demand rises: more beats per minute means more work. Supply time falls: as heart rate rises, diastole shortens much more than systole. Diastole is when the left ventricle is perfused. Diastole clock A heart-rate slider (60–180 beats/min). One beat is drawn as a bar split into systole and diastole, to scale. Below it: one minute's worth of diastole, in seconds. Readout: \"At 70: 0.86 s per beat, diastole ≈ 0.57 s. At 150: 0.40 s per beat, diastole ≈ 0.16 s.\" A line warns when diastole falls below about a third of the beat: \"the left ventricle has little time to be perfused\". In a healthy heart, the arterioles dilate enough to cope. But with a narrowed coronary artery, a low diastolic pressure or a high filling pressure, the shrinking diastole can tip the balance into ischaemia. 6. Coronary flow reserve, angina and myocardial infarction Normal coronary artery versus a narrowed vessel. Image credit BruceBlaus, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. Coronary flow reserve is how much coronary flow can rise above its resting level when the arterioles fully dilate. Normally, it can rise about 4–5 times. Why angina comes on with exertion, not at rest: behind a narrowed artery, the arterioles dilate at rest to keep flow normal. They have used up their reserve. When exercise raises demand, there is nothing left to give, so supply falls short and angina (ischaemic chest pain) appears. Resting cures it. Angina Myocardial infarction (heart attack) What happens Temporary ischaemia: supply falls short of demand Prolonged, severe ischaemia: heart muscle dies Reversible? Yes, when demand falls or flow returns No: dead muscle becomes scar Usual cause A fixed narrowing plus higher demand A plaque ruptures and a clot blocks the artery Understanding Check: Sections 4–6 1. What mainly dilates the coronary arterioles when the heart works harder? A. Parasympathetic nerves releasing acetylcholine B. Local signals such as adenosine from working heart muscle C. Direct sympathetic stimulation of α receptors D. A rise in coronary venous pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Metabolic signals from the working muscle (adenosine, K⁺, low O₂, nitric oxide) relax the arterioles: active hyperaemia. 2. Why can a heart rate of 160 cause ischaemia in a patient with coronary narrowing? A. It raises oxygen demand and shortens diastole, when the left ventricle is fed B. It raises aortic diastolic pressure too high C. It makes the coronary arteries contract more strongly D. It increases the time the left ventricle is perfused Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Demand rises while the time available for left ventricular perfusion shrinks. 3. A man has chest pain on climbing stairs that goes when he rests. What is the best explanation? A. A coronary artery is completely blocked, killing heart muscle B. A narrowed artery has used up its reserve, so supply can't rise with demand C. His heart muscle is extracting too little oxygen D. His coronary veins are blocked during exercise Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The arterioles behind the narrowing are already dilated at rest. On exertion, supply can't rise, which gives stable angina. 7. Chapter Essentials Coronary arteries arise from the aortic sinuses. The heart extracts about 70% of its oxygen at rest, so more demand needs more flow. LAD = front of the LV and the septum; circumflex = side of the LV; RCA = right ventricle and inferior wall. Dominance = which artery gives the posterior descending artery. Venous blood drains via the coronary sinus to the right atrium. The left ventricle is fed mainly in diastole, because contraction squeezes its arteries. Driving pressure ≈ aortic diastolic − LV diastolic pressure. The subendocardium is the most vulnerable layer: it is squeezed hardest in systole, so it depends most on diastole and becomes ischaemic first. Active hyperaemia: adenosine and other metabolites dilate the arterioles. Sympathetic β₁ stimulation makes the heart work harder, so the extra metabolites outweigh the direct α constriction and the net result is dilation. Tachycardia raises demand and shortens diastole, so it can cause ischaemia in diseased arteries. Narrowed artery → reserve used up at rest → angina on exertion. Infarction = muscle death from prolonged ischaemia.","pulmonary":"Pulmonary Circulation 1. Two circulations in one lung The lung receives blood from two separate systems, and they do different jobs: Pulmonary circulation Bronchial circulation Job Gas exchange Nourishes the airways and supporting tissue Comes from Right ventricle → pulmonary arteries Aorta → bronchial arteries Pressure Low Systemic (high) Drains to Pulmonary veins → left atrium Partly into the pulmonary veins Why does that last row matter? Bronchial blood has already given its oxygen to the lung tissue. When some of it drains into the pulmonary veins, it mixes a little oxygen-poor blood into the freshly oxygenated blood heading for the left atrium. This is one reason arterial blood carries slightly less oxygen than the air in the alveoli. Pulmonary circuit anatomy. Image credit OpenStax College, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. 2. Same flow, much lower pressure The two sides of the heart are in series, so the right ventricle must pump the same output as the left: about 5 L/min at rest. Yet the pressures are very different: Pulmonary Systemic Arterial pressure About 25/8 mmHg (mean about 15) About 120/80 mmHg (mean about 93) Pressure at the end of the circuit Left atrium, about 5 Right atrium, about 2 Pressure drop across the circuit About 10 mmHg About 90 mmHg The same flow is pushed through by a pressure drop about one-ninth as large, so pulmonary resistance is only about one-tenth of systemic resistance. ( → Haemodynamics) Why so low? The pulmonary vessels are short, wide, thin-walled and stretchy, and the capillary network is huge, with countless paths in parallel. Exercise: more flow, little extra pressure. When cardiac output rises, two things happen: Recruitment: capillaries that were closed or barely perfused open up. Distension: capillaries that were already open widen. Both lower the resistance, so pulmonary flow can rise several-fold while pulmonary arterial pressure rises only a little. That protects the thin capillaries, and the right ventricle, from high pressure. 3. Why the alveoli stay dry The alveolar wall must be thin for gas exchange, so the lung can't afford fluid leaking into it. Compare the two main forces across a pulmonary capillary ( → Microcirculation): Capillary hydrostatic pressure, pushing fluid out: only about 10 mmHg. Plasma oncotic pressure, pulling fluid in: about 25 mmHg. The outward push is unusually weak. So only a small amount of fluid filters into the lung's interstitium, and the lymphatics carry it away easily, long before it can reach the alveoli. Source note: The full balance also includes the lung's interstitial forces (a slightly negative interstitial pressure and a fairly high interstitial protein level), which leave a small net outward filtration that the lymphatics clear. The lecture gives the two main values, 10 and 25 mmHg; Guyton and Hall give the full balance. When does this safety margin fail? In left heart failure, left atrial pressure rises and backs up into the pulmonary veins and capillaries. Once capillary hydrostatic pressure climbs past about 25 mmHg, roughly the plasma oncotic pressure, filtration outruns lymph drainage. Fluid collects first in the interstitium, then floods the alveoli: pulmonary oedema, which thickens the barrier and impairs gas exchange. ( → Heart Failure, §3) Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. What is the main job of the bronchial circulation? A. To carry blood from the right ventricle for gas exchange B. To nourish the airways and supporting lung tissue C. To return oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart D. To drain lymph from the alveoli into the bloodstream Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The bronchial arteries come from the aorta and supply the airways. Gas exchange is the job of the pulmonary circulation. 2. The right and left ventricles pump the same output, but pulmonary arterial pressure is far lower. Why? A. The right ventricle pumps a smaller volume with each beat B. Blood flows through the lungs only during diastole C. Pulmonary arteries contain valves that lower the pressure D. Pulmonary vascular resistance is only about one-tenth of systemic Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Same flow with a much smaller pressure drop means a much lower resistance: short, wide, stretchy vessels arranged in parallel. 3. In left heart failure, what makes fluid flood the alveoli? A. Plasma oncotic pressure rises and pulls fluid into the alveoli B. Bronchial arteries leak fluid directly into the alveolar spaces C. Pulmonary capillary pressure rises until filtration outruns lymph drainage D. Pulmonary vascular resistance falls, so capillaries collapse Show answer and explanation Answer: C. High left atrial pressure backs up into the capillaries. Once the outward push beats the oncotic pull and the lymphatics, fluid accumulates. 4. Gravity, posture and the lung's blood reservoir Standing up: the lung apex sits above the heart and the base below it. Gravity adds pressure to the blood in the lower vessels and takes it away from the upper ones. ( → Gravity) So: At the base, vascular pressure is higher, more vessels are open and wider, and blood flow is greatest. At the apex, vascular pressure is lowest and blood flow is least. West zones: compare three pressures. Whether a capillary is open depends on the pressure inside it versus the alveolar air pressure squeezing it from outside: Zone Pressures What happens Zone 1 Alveolar > arterial > venous Capillaries squeezed shut: no flow. Not present in healthy lungs; can appear after haemorrhage or with positive-pressure ventilation. Zone 2 (upper lung) Arterial > alveolar > venous Flow depends on arterial − alveolar pressure, so it is intermittent Zone 3 (lower lung) Arterial > venous > alveolar Capillaries always open: continuous flow West zones are pressure relationships, not fixed stripes of lung. They shift with posture, blood pressure and breathing. West zones in an upright lung Zone 1 (apex) PA > Pa > Pv: no flow Not in healthy lungs Zone 2 Pa > PA > Pv Flow on and off Zone 3 (base) Pa > Pv > PA Continuous, greatest flow PA alveolar · Pa arterial · Pv venous pressure Blood flow increases from apex to base because gravity raises vascular pressure lower in the lung. Zones are pressure relationships, not fixed stripes: they shift with posture, blood pressure and breathing. Lying down: the whole lung is at about the level of the heart, so flow is spread more evenly. And about 400 mL of blood moves from the body into the lungs. Standing up returns it to the circulation. So the pulmonary vessels act as a blood reservoir. Why this matters: orthopnoea. In left heart failure, the pulmonary capillaries are already near their limit. Lying flat adds that extra blood to the lungs, capillary pressure rises further, and the patient becomes breathless lying down. Sitting up moves blood back to the lower body and eases it. ( → Heart Failure, §3) 5. Low oxygen constricts lung vessels In most tissues, low oxygen dilates the arterioles, bringing in more blood. ( → Local Control) The lung does the opposite: Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction: when an alveolus has low oxygen, the small pulmonary arteries supplying it constrict. Why the opposite? In the lung, low oxygen in an alveolus means that alveolus is poorly ventilated. Sending blood there would waste it, because the blood can't pick up oxygen. Constricting those vessels diverts blood to well-ventilated alveoli, matching blood flow to air flow (the ventilation–perfusion ratio). A rise in CO₂ (and H⁺) also constricts pulmonary vessels, which helps in the same way. Local versus everywhere: One poorly ventilated region (for example, a blocked airway): blood is redirected, and oxygenation improves. Every alveolus hypoxic (high altitude, chronic lung disease): there is nowhere better to send the blood. Constriction everywhere raises total pulmonary resistance. If it persists, the result is pulmonary hypertension, and the right ventricle strains against it. Before birth, the same response keeps fetal pulmonary resistance high, so blood bypasses the fluid-filled lungs. ( → Fetal Circulation, §1) Nerves and nitric oxide: the pulmonary vessels have an extensive autonomic supply; sympathetic stimulation constricts them. Nitric oxide from the endothelium dilates them. Understanding Check: Sections 4–5 1. In an upright person, why does the lung base receive more blood than the apex? A. Gravity raises vascular pressure at the base, opening and widening vessels B. Alveolar pressure is much higher at the base than at the apex C. The bronchial arteries supply the base but not the apex D. Hypoxic vasoconstriction opens the vessels at the base Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Below the heart, gravity adds pressure to the blood, so more capillaries are open and distended. 2. Why does a patient with left heart failure become breathless when lying flat? A. Lying flat lowers the pressure in the pulmonary capillaries B. Lying flat raises the plasma oncotic pressure in the lungs C. The heart pumps less blood into the lungs when lying flat D. About 400 mL of extra blood moves into the already congested lungs Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The extra blood raises pulmonary capillary pressure further, pushing more fluid into the lung tissue. This is orthopnoea. 3. At high altitude, why does pulmonary arterial pressure rise? A. Low oxygen dilates pulmonary vessels, so more blood enters the lungs B. The bronchial arteries constrict and divert blood into the lungs C. Every alveolus is hypoxic, so vessels constrict throughout the lung D. Gravity has a stronger effect on the lungs at high altitude Show answer and explanation Answer: C. With hypoxia everywhere, there is no better-ventilated region to send blood to. Widespread constriction raises total resistance. 6. Chapter Essentials Two circulations: pulmonary (gas exchange, low pressure) and bronchial (from the aorta, nourishes the airways, partly drains into pulmonary veins). Same output as the left side at about one-tenth of the resistance: mean pulmonary arterial pressure about 15 mmHg. Exercise: recruitment and distension let flow rise with little rise in pressure. Alveoli stay dry: capillary hydrostatic pressure is about 10 mmHg against an oncotic pressure of about 25; lymphatics clear the small leak. Above about 25 mmHg capillary pressure (left heart failure), pulmonary oedema develops. Upright: gravity raises vascular pressure at the base, so most flow goes there. West zones: zone 1 (alveolar > arterial) no flow, not in healthy lungs; zone 2 (arterial > alveolar > venous) on-off flow; zone 3 (both vascular pressures > alveolar) continuous flow. Lying down moves about 400 mL of blood into the lungs, which explains orthopnoea in heart failure. Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction diverts blood to well-ventilated alveoli. When hypoxia is everywhere, it causes pulmonary hypertension.","splanchnic":"Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation 1. An unusual route through two capillary beds The gut is supplied by three unpaired arteries from the aorta: the coeliac trunk, the superior mesenteric artery and the inferior mesenteric artery. The unusual part is where the blood goes next. Venous blood from the gut and spleen does not go straight back to the heart. It collects in the portal vein (formed by the superior mesenteric and splenic veins) and passes through the liver first. So this blood crosses two capillary beds in a row: the gut's capillaries, then the liver's sinusoids. Why? Everything absorbed from the gut (nutrients, drugs, toxins, bacteria) is sent to the liver to be processed before it reaches the rest of the body. Portal venous system. Image credit Jmarchn, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. 2. The liver's double blood supply and its zones The liver gets blood from two inflows: Inflow Share of the flow Brings Portal vein About 75% Nutrient-rich, but partly deoxygenated, blood from the gut Hepatic artery About 25% Oxygen-rich arterial blood (about half the liver's oxygen) Both enter at the portal triad (portal vein branch + hepatic artery branch + bile duct). They mix in the sinusoids, the leaky, wide liver capillaries, and flow towards the central vein → hepatic veins → inferior vena cava. Bile flows the opposite way, towards the bile ducts in the triad. Hepatic lobule and acinar oxygen zonation. Image credit MarquardtM, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. Zones: why the centre suffers first. Along each sinusoid, liver cells take oxygen out as blood passes: Zone 1 (near the portal triad) gets the freshest, most oxygen-rich blood. Zone 3 (near the central vein) gets blood that has already given up much of its oxygen. So when blood flow or oxygen falls (in shock or heart failure), zone 3 is damaged first. Between the sinusoid lining and the liver cells is a narrow gap, the space of Disse, where plasma touches the cells directly. Fluid that collects here drains as hepatic lymph. 3. Blood flow after meals, and in shock After a meal, the gut works harder and its arterioles dilate, so splanchnic blood flow rises: postprandial hyperaemia. The splanchnic veins are a blood reservoir: stretchy, and holding a large volume. In haemorrhage, sympathetic nerves constrict the splanchnic vessels. This sends stored blood back to the heart and diverts flow to the brain and heart. It helps hold up blood pressure, but if it lasts, the gut lining becomes ischaemic and can break down. ( → Shock) Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why does gut venous blood pass through the liver before reaching the heart? A. The liver adds oxygen to the venous blood from the gut B. The portal vein is too narrow to reach the heart C. The liver processes absorbed nutrients and toxins first D. The gut veins have no valves, so they need the liver to pump Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The portal route sends everything absorbed from the gut to the liver to be processed before it enters the general circulation. 2. In shock, why are liver cells near the central vein (zone 3) damaged first? A. They receive blood only from the portal vein B. They are closest to the hepatic artery C. They do not have sinusoids D. They receive blood after upstream cells have used much of its oxygen Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Blood flows from the portal triad to the central vein, losing oxygen along the way, so zone 3 runs short first. 3. How do the splanchnic vessels help in haemorrhage? A. They dilate to receive more blood from the heart B. They constrict, sending stored blood back to the heart C. They make new red cells to replace lost blood D. They stop the liver from releasing glucose Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Sympathetic constriction squeezes the splanchnic reservoir and diverts flow to vital organs, at the cost of gut perfusion. 4. Why does fluid collect as ascites? Ascites is fluid in the peritoneal cavity. It usually develops when the liver is scarred ( cirrhosis), through several routes at once: High portal pressure: scarring blocks flow through the liver, so pressure backs up in the portal vein and sinusoids, pushing fluid out. ( → Oedema, §4) Much more hepatic lymph: the leaky sinusoids filter so much fluid that the lymphatics can't keep up, and the excess weeps into the abdomen. The kidneys keep salt and water, sensing underfilled arteries. Low albumin (a failing liver makes less), so the pull holding fluid in the vessels weakens. ( → Oedema, §5) Right heart failure can cause ascites too: high pressure backs up through the hepatic veins into the liver. The protein content of ascitic fluid varies with the cause: it is not always high, and not always low. Understanding Check: Section 4 1. Which change most directly starts ascites in cirrhosis? A. Raised pressure in the hepatic artery B. Raised pressure in the portal vein and liver sinusoids C. Increased flow through the coeliac trunk D. Increased albumin production by the liver Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Scarring obstructs flow, raising portal and sinusoidal pressure. Lymph overflow, salt retention and low albumin add to it. 2. How can right heart failure cause ascites? A. Pressure backs up through the hepatic veins into the liver B. It raises albumin, pulling fluid into the abdomen C. It blocks the portal vein directly D. It stops the kidneys from retaining salt Show answer and explanation Answer: A. High right atrial pressure is passed back into the hepatic veins and sinusoids, so filtration rises. 3. Why do the liver's sinusoids produce so much lymph? A. They have no blood flow at all B. They contain muscle that pumps fluid out C. They are very leaky, so fluid and protein filter out easily D. They are lined by thick, continuous capillaries Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Sinusoids are discontinuous capillaries, so much fluid passes into the space of Disse and drains as hepatic lymph. 5. Chapter Essentials Gut arteries: coeliac trunk, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric. Portal route: gut capillaries → portal vein → liver sinusoids → hepatic veins → IVC: two capillary beds in a row. Two liver inflows: the portal vein (about 75% of flow) and the hepatic artery (about 25%, but about half the oxygen). Zone 1 (portal triad) is oxygen-rich; zone 3 (central vein) is oxygen-poor and damaged first. After meals, gut flow rises. In haemorrhage, splanchnic vessels constrict, mobilising blood but risking gut ischaemia. Ascites: portal hypertension + lymph overflow + salt retention + low albumin (cirrhosis), or right heart failure.","skin":"Cutaneous Circulation and the Triple Response 1. Skin blood flow is mainly about heat, not nutrition Most tissues get the blood flow they need to feed their cells. The skin is different: its flow is controlled mainly to regulate body temperature. It can carry far more blood than its cells need. Hot: skin vessels dilate, so warm blood flows near the surface and heat is lost (flushed skin). Cold: sympathetic nerves constrict skin vessels, so blood stays deep and heat is kept (pale, cool skin). Arteriovenous anastomoses (AVAs) are direct shortcuts from small arteries to veins, found in the fingers, toes, palms, ears and nose. When they open, a large volume of warm blood passes just under the skin, ideal for losing heat. They are controlled almost entirely by sympathetic nerves. Blood vessels within the skin. Image credit BruceBlaus, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. 2. Skin blood flow in shock In haemorrhage, sympathetic nerves shut down skin blood flow, diverting blood to the brain and heart. That is why a bleeding patient has cold, pale, clammy skin, often before blood pressure falls much. ( → Shock) Septic shock can be different early on: toxins dilate the vessels, so the skin may be warm and flushed. Skin temperature alone can't diagnose shock; it must be read with the rest of the patient. Understanding Check: Sections 1–2 1. What mainly controls skin blood flow? A. The oxygen needs of the skin cells B. The need to regulate body temperature C. The amount of sweat being produced D. The pressure in the skin's veins Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Skin flow can far exceed its nutritional needs. It is set mainly to keep or lose heat. 2. Why does a patient who is bleeding heavily have cold, pale skin? A. Sympathetic nerves shut down skin flow to protect vital organs B. Blood loss lowers body temperature everywhere equally C. The skin's arteriovenous anastomoses open widely D. Histamine constricts the skin vessels Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Sympathetic vasoconstriction diverts blood from the skin to the brain and heart. 3. Where are arteriovenous anastomoses mostly found, and what are they for? A. In the trunk, to supply nutrients to the skin B. In the fingers, toes, ears and nose, to lose or keep heat C. In deep muscles, to supply oxygen in exercise D. In the scalp, to drain blood from the brain Show answer and explanation Answer: B. AVAs in the extremities shunt warm blood under the skin when heat must be lost. 3. The triple response of Lewis Draw a blunt point firmly across the skin of the forearm. Within a minute, three things appear, in this order: Sign What you see Why 1. Red line A red line exactly along the stroke, within seconds Local capillaries dilate where the skin was injured 2. Flare A wider, blotchy red area spreading out around the line Axon reflex: sensory nerve endings send signals along their own branches, dilating nearby arterioles 3. Wheal A raised, pale swelling along the line Histamine makes the capillaries leaky, so fluid leaks into the tissue (local oedema) Lewis triple response — what each visible sign means 1. Red line Capillaries dilate exactly along the stroke 2. Flare Arterioles dilate around it (the axon reflex) 3. Wheal Leaky vessels: fluid swells the skin A schematic of three related features after firm stroking: narrow local redness, wider surrounding redness and raised oedema. The brief white response to light stroking is separate from Lewis's triple response. Why does the flare spread? The axon reflex, step by step A single sensory nerve fibre in the skin doesn't end at one point. Near the skin it splits into many branches, like the fingers of a hand, and together they cover a patch of skin a few centimetres across. The stroke stimulates one branch. The firm stroke damages a few cells and stimulates the sensory nerve endings in the line of the stroke. The signal travels up that branch. It travels towards the spinal cord, as any sensory signal would. At the branch point, it also travels back down the other branches. An impulse travels in both directions along a nerve fibre. So when it reaches the fork, it carries on up towards the spinal cord and also runs back down every other branch, into skin that was never touched. The endings of those branches release vasodilators. They release substance P and CGRP (calcitonin gene-related peptide). These dilate the nearby arterioles directly, and substance P also makes nearby mast cells release histamine, which dilates them further. The arterioles across the whole patch dilate, giving the flare. The redness spreads as far as that fibre's branches reach, which is why the flare is wider than the stroke but stops a few centimetres out. Why \"axon\" reflex? The whole loop happens within the branches of one nerve fibre. There is no synapse, and the spinal cord and brain are not needed. That makes it different from a true reflex arc, which passes through the spinal cord. How do we know? A local anaesthetic that blocks the nerve abolishes the flare. The red line and the wheal still appear, because they come from local injury and histamine, not from the nerve. After the sensory nerve has been cut and has degenerated, the flare disappears. Cutting the nerve above the branch point, closer to the spinal cord, does not stop the flare, because the loop doesn't need the spinal cord. The axon reflex: why the flare spreads Spinal cord No spinal cord needed one sensory fibre 1 2 3 Stroke skin 4 Substance P + CGRP dilate arterioles → flare ① The stroke stimulates one branch. ② The impulse travels up to the branch point. ③ It also runs back down the other branches. ④ Their endings release substance P and CGRP, so arterioles across the whole patch dilate. Source note: Substance P and CGRP as the axon-reflex mediators, and the local-anaesthetic and nerve-degeneration findings, are standard physiology (Lewis, 1927; Guyton and Hall). The lecture names the axon reflex as the cause of the flare. White line, not part of the triple response. A light stroke produces only a brief pale line: the local vessels constrict for a moment. Memory hook: red line = local redness; flare = spreading redness; wheal = raised swelling. Understanding Check: Section 3 1. Why does the flare spread beyond the line of the stroke? A. Histamine diffuses widely through the skin B. An axon reflex dilates arterioles through nearby nerve branches C. The brainstem sends dilating signals to the area D. The wheal pushes blood outwards into the surrounding skin Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Sensory nerve branches carry the signal sideways and release dilators, without involving the spinal cord or brain. 2. What causes the raised wheal? A. Local capillaries dilating with blood B. Histamine making capillaries leaky, so fluid leaks into the tissue C. Sympathetic nerves constricting the local arterioles D. Blood clotting under the skin Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The wheal is local oedema from increased capillary permeability. 3. Does the axon reflex need the spinal cord? A. Yes: the signal must reach the spinal cord to be relayed B. Yes: the brainstem must coordinate the flare C. No: the signal stays within the branches of one sensory nerve fibre D. No: the response is caused by histamine alone, with no nerves Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The signal travels from one branch of a sensory neuron back down its other branches, so no central connection is involved. 4. A local anaesthetic is injected into the forearm skin before a firm stroke. Which part of the triple response is lost? A. The red line along the stroke B. The flare spreading around the line C. The raised wheal along the line D. All three parts of the response Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The flare depends on the axon reflex, so blocking the nerve stops it. The red line and the wheal come from local injury and histamine. 4. Chapter Essentials Skin blood flow regulates heat, not just nutrition. It can far exceed what the skin needs. Hot → dilate (lose heat); cold → sympathetic constriction (keep heat). Arteriovenous anastomoses in the fingers, toes, ears and nose shunt warm blood near the surface to lose heat. Haemorrhage → cold, pale skin (sympathetic shutdown); early septic shock may give warm skin. Triple response: red line (local dilation) → flare (axon reflex) → wheal (histamine, leaky capillaries, oedema). Flare = axon reflex: a stroked branch sends impulses back down the fibre's other branches, which release substance P and CGRP to dilate arterioles. No spinal cord needed. A light stroke gives only a brief white line (local constriction), which is not part of the triple response.","fetal":"Fetal Circulation and the Transition at Birth 1. Why does the fetus need shortcuts? Before birth, the placenta, not the lungs, does gas exchange. The fetal lungs are filled with fluid, and their vessels are tightly constricted, so pulmonary resistance is very high. Pumping all the blood through them would be wasted effort. So the fetal circulation has three shortcuts (shunts) that send oxygen-rich blood where it is needed, mainly the heart and brain, and route most of it past the liver and lungs. Complete fetal circulation. Image credit OpenStax College, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. 2. Trace the fetal circuit Placenta → umbilical vein: the most oxygen-rich blood in the fetus (yet still only about 80% saturated) travels up the single umbilical vein. Shortcut 1: ductus venosus. Much of it bypasses the liver, going straight to the inferior vena cava. Into the right atrium. The stream from the IVC is aimed straight across the atrium at an opening in the septum. Shortcut 2: foramen ovale. This oxygen-rich stream crosses from the right atrium to the left atrium, then goes to the left ventricle → aorta → heart and brain first. SVC blood (oxygen-poor, from the head) goes mainly into the right ventricle → pulmonary trunk. Shortcut 3: ductus arteriosus. Pulmonary resistance is high, so most of this blood bypasses the lungs, crossing from the pulmonary trunk into the descending aorta. Back to the placenta: the two umbilical arteries (branches of the internal iliac arteries) carry blood back to be reoxygenated. Shunt Connects Bypasses Ductus venosus Umbilical vein → inferior vena cava The liver Foramen ovale Right atrium → left atrium The lungs (via the right ventricle) Ductus arteriosus Pulmonary trunk → aorta The lungs Why does blood flow right-to-left through the shunts? Because, before birth, the right side has the higher pressure: pulmonary resistance is high, while the placenta keeps systemic resistance low. 3. Fetal haemoglobin: grabbing oxygen from the mother Fetal haemoglobin (HbF) holds oxygen more tightly than adult haemoglobin (HbA), because it binds the molecule 2,3-BPG less strongly. 2,3-BPG normally loosens haemoglobin's grip on oxygen. So, at the low oxygen levels in the placenta, fetal blood can pull oxygen across from the mother's blood. After birth, HbF is gradually replaced by HbA over several months. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why is most blood diverted away from the fetal lungs? A. The fetal lungs are fluid-filled and have very high vascular resistance B. The fetal lungs already receive oxygen from amniotic fluid C. The fetal heart is too weak to pump blood through the lungs D. The lungs would take oxygen away from the placenta Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Gas exchange happens in the placenta. The fluid-filled lungs' constricted vessels resist flow, so blood takes the shunts. 2. Which shunt carries blood from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta? A. The ductus venosus B. The foramen ovale C. The ductus arteriosus D. The umbilical artery Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The ductus arteriosus links the pulmonary trunk to the descending aorta, bypassing the lungs. 3. Why does fetal haemoglobin help the fetus take oxygen from the mother? A. It binds 2,3-BPG more strongly, so it releases oxygen easily B. It holds oxygen more tightly because it binds 2,3-BPG less strongly C. It carries carbon dioxide instead of oxygen D. It is found only in the placenta, not in fetal blood Show answer and explanation Answer: B. With less 2,3-BPG bound, HbF has a higher affinity for oxygen, so it loads oxygen from maternal blood. 4. What closes after delivery? Two events at birth flip the pressures: The first breaths: the lungs fill with air and oxygen rises, so the lung vessels dilate and pulmonary resistance falls sharply. Lung blood flow surges, and much more blood returns to the left atrium. Clamping the cord: the placenta, a low-resistance circuit, is cut off, so systemic resistance rises. The flow up the umbilical vein stops. At birth — two changes that redirect circulation First breaths Lung vessels dilate: resistance falls More blood returns to the left atrium LA pressure > RA: foramen ovale shuts Cord clamped Placenta removed: systemic resistance rises Umbilical vein flow stops Ductus venosus closes These simultaneous changes shift atrial pressures, favor functional foramen ovale closure and contribute to ductus arteriosus constriction. Anatomical closure occurs later and varies by structure. Circulation after birth. Image credit BruceBlaus, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. Now the left side has the higher pressure, and each shunt closes: Shunt Why it closes Becomes (anatomical remnant) Foramen ovale Left atrial pressure now exceeds right, which presses the flap shut Fossa ovalis Ductus arteriosus Higher oxygen and less prostaglandin (from the placenta) make it constrict Ligamentum arteriosum Ductus venosus No more umbilical venous flow Ligamentum venosum Umbilical vein No more flow Ligamentum teres (of the liver) Umbilical arteries No more flow Medial umbilical ligaments Closure happens in two steps: functional closure within minutes to days, as flow stops; then anatomical closure over weeks to months, as tissue seals the channel. Understanding Check: Section 4 1. Why does the foramen ovale close at birth? A. Prostaglandin levels rise after the cord is clamped B. Left atrial pressure becomes higher than right atrial pressure C. The umbilical vein carries more blood after birth D. Pulmonary resistance rises when the lungs fill with air Show answer and explanation Answer: B. More blood returns from the lungs to the left atrium, and its pressure rises above the right, pressing the flap shut. 2. What makes the ductus arteriosus constrict after birth? A. Rising oxygen and falling prostaglandins B. Falling oxygen and rising prostaglandins C. A rise in pulmonary vascular resistance D. Increased flow through the umbilical arteries Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Oxygen constricts the ductus, and the prostaglandins that kept it open fall once the placenta is gone. 3. At birth, pulmonary resistance falls while systemic resistance rises. Why do both happen? A. Both are caused by the first breath alone B. Breathing dilates the lung vessels; cord clamping removes the low-resistance placenta C. Both are caused by clamping the cord alone D. The ductus arteriosus closing raises both resistances Show answer and explanation Answer: B. They are two separate events: lung aeration lowers pulmonary resistance, and losing the placenta raises systemic resistance. 5. Chapter Essentials The placenta does fetal gas exchange. The fluid-filled lungs have high resistance. Three shunts: ductus venosus (bypasses the liver), foramen ovale (right → left atrium), ductus arteriosus (pulmonary trunk → aorta). The best-oxygenated blood goes through the foramen ovale to the heart and brain. Before birth the right side has the higher pressure, so flow through the shunts is right-to-left. HbF binds oxygen more tightly (less 2,3-BPG binding), so it loads oxygen from the mother. At birth: the first breaths lower pulmonary resistance; cord clamping raises systemic resistance; left pressure > right. Remnants: foramen ovale → fossa ovalis; ductus arteriosus → ligamentum arteriosum; ductus venosus → ligamentum venosum; umbilical vein → ligamentum teres; umbilical arteries → medial umbilical ligaments."};
